ed by the PdNCs@CNFs electrocatalyst during nitrogen reduction reactions is 0.028 mA cm(-2). This value indicates the performance of the electrocatalyst in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction, highlighting its effectiveness in this electrochemical process.0.05 M H2SO4 serves as the electrolyte in various electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) involving multiple electrocatalysts. Specifically, it is utilized with the CoFeB@rGO electrocatalyst, BNFC, BNC-NSs, FeNiPBA nanocrystals, and the Co-N-x-C electrocatalyst. Additionally, it is employed in the NRR with Co3O4@NCs, where it provides an acidic environment that is conducive to enhancing the performance of these catalysts. The consistent use of 0.05 M H2SO4 across these different systems highlights its importance in optimizing the electrochemical processes involved in nitrogen reduction.At an applied potential of 0.05 V, both Mo3Fe3C and RuFeCoNiCu demonstrate significant catalytic performance in nitrogen reduction reactions. Specifically, Mo3Fe3C exhibits excellent nitrogen-fixing capabilities, while RuFeCoNiCu achieves a high yield of ammonia (NH3). This potential is critical for optimizing the efficiency of these electrocatalysts in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, highlighting the importance of applied potential in enhancing the performance of various catalysts in electrochemical processes.At an applied potential of 0.05 V vs RHE, the electrocatalyst Mo3Fe3C demonstrates a high Faradaic efficiency for the nitrogen reduction reaction. Under these conditions, the catalyst achieves an ammonia (NH3) yield of 5.14 Œºg cm^(-2)h^(-1), indicating its effectiveness in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia.0.07 eV is the energy barrier for the first-step protonation in the nitrogen reduction reaction facilitated by the PdFe3 nano-alloy.The applied potential range from -0.07 to -0.22 V is where efficient conversion of N2 to NH3 is achieved with NH3 production rates >= 2.0 Œºg(NH3)h(-1) mg(cat)(-1).0.097 V vs RHE is the applied potential at which the faradaic efficiency of 27% is measured for Pd-NiTiO3 electrocatalysts.The term "0.1 M" refers to the concentration of electrolytes used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction. Specifically, it indicates a concentration of 0.1 M for both HCl and Na2SO4. The 0.1 M HCl serves as an electrolyte that plays a crucial role in the reaction environment, while the 0.1 M Na2SO4 electrolyte is significant for influencing the pH level of the solution. The presence of Na2SO4 is particularly important as it can affect the overall electrochemical performance and efficiency of the nitrogen reduction process. Thus, the concentration of 0.1 M is critical for optimizing the conditions under which nitrogen is reduced to ammonia, impacting both the yield and faradaic efficiency of the reaction.0.1 M H2SO4 is the electrolyte used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) with FeNiPBA nanocrystals.0.1 M HCl serves as the electrolyte in various electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) involving a wide range of electrocatalysts. It is utilized for testing the catalytic performance of LiMn2O4, Nb2O5 nanofibers, TiO2/Ti3C2Tx hybrids, CoTPP, Mo2N, Bi nanodendrites, CoPc nanotubes, Ti3C2Tx MXene nanosheets, carbon quantum dots, and 17% V-alpha-Fe2O3, among others. Additionally, it is employed in reactions with VN nanosheet arrays, NP-C-MOF-5, structured polyaniline, V-NiS2, ReS2/CC, O-KFCNTs, 2D C3N4-NV, OV-Ti2O3, LiFe5O8-rGO, PdPb/C, molybdenum-based single atom catalysts, S-G electrocatalysts, TiC/C, Au-25-Cys-Mo, V8C7/C, and VN/CC electrocatalysts. The use of 0.1 M HCl in these reactions is critical for achieving high Faradaic efficiency and ammonia yield, highlighting its importance in the optimization of electrochemical processes for nitrogen reduction.0.1 m HCl solution is used in the nitrogen reduction reaction with the Au-25-Cys-M catalyst.0.1 M hydrochloric acid is the electrolyte used in the nitrogen reduction reaction with Fe-2(MoO4)(3) nanoparticles.0.1 m K2SO4 is the electrolyte used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction with isolated Pt sites.0.1 M KOH is a versatile alkaline electrolyte employed in various electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) across multiple catalysts. It is utilized with Ni2P nanoparticles, Fe/Cu catalysts, gamma-Fe2O3, MoN/CFC electrodes, and K2Ti4O9, demonstrating its broad applicability in enhancing ammonia synthesis. Additionally, 0.1 M KOH serves as the electrolyte for C@NiO@Ni microtubes, Ru@MXene, Fe-N/C-CNT, Al-doped Co3O4, Cu/Ti3C2, CuAg/Ti(3)C(2)MXene, and TiO2@N-C electrocatalysts, indicating its critical role in optimizing reaction conditions and performance. Furthermore, it is involved in the electrochemical synthesis of ammonia (ESA) and is also used with 450-NiMnO3 and NiFe-LDH, influencing the overall efficiency of the NRR. Lastly, 0.1 M KOH is noted as one of the electrolytes used in conjunction with RuFeCoNiCu, underscoring its significance in the field of electrochemical catalysis for nitrogen reduction.0.1 M KOH aqueous electrolyte is used in the nitrogen reduction reaction with the ZIF-8 derived catalyst.0.1 M Li2SO4 is an electrolyte utilized in the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction, specifically in systems involving RuFeCoNiCu and NiCoS/C nanocages. Its role as an electrolyte is crucial for facilitating the electrochemical processes necessary for nitrogen reduction, contributing to the overall efficiency and effectiveness of the reactions.0.1 M LiClO4 serves as the electrolyte in various electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) involving different catalysts. Specifically, it is utilized with the La-doped TiO2 catalyst, the N-NiO/CC electrocatalyst, and in conjunction with FeOOH quantum dots decorated on a graphene sheet. This consistent use of 0.1 M LiClO4 across multiple systems highlights its importance in facilitating the NRR process, potentially influencing the efficiency and yield of ammonia production in these electrochemical setups.0.1 M LiClO4 + 20 % PEG is the electrolyte system used to inhibit the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) during eNRR.0.1 M Na(2)SO(4) is the electrolyte used for testing FeMoO(4) in the nitrogen reduction reaction.0.1 M Na2SO4 is a versatile electrolyte used extensively in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It serves as a medium for various electrocatalysts, facilitating the production of ammonia (NH3) through the reduction of nitrogen. This electrolyte has been employed in conjunction with a wide range of catalysts, including CoFe2O4 nanoclusters, ultrafine Co3O4 nanoparticles, CoS/MoS2, TiS2 NSs, Ag-Cu-NS/CP, Mo-doped W18O49, Ni12P5/FeP4, C18@Fe3P/CP, and many others. The use of 0.1 M Na2SO4 is noted in systems involving 3D COFs, NbSe2, C-doped TiO2 nanoparticles, and various other composite and nanostructured materials, indicating its broad applicability in optimizing NRR processes. The electrolyte is characterized by a neutral pH of 7, which is conducive to the electrochemical reactions involved. Overall, 0.1 M Na2SO4 plays a crucial role in enhancing the efficiency and yield of ammonia production in diverse electrochemical setups.0.1 M Na2SO4 at pH=7 is a reaction condition used for the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.0.1 M Na2SO4 solution is used in the nitrogen reduction reaction with the porous tin heterostructure.)<|COMPLETE|>0.1 M NaOH is another electrolyte used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, yielding a high NH3 yield rate of 39 ¬µgNH3 h-1 mgcat.-1 and a faradaic efficiency of 16% at -0.4 V vs. RHE.0.1 M PBS is the phosphate buffered saline electrolyte used for the iron oxyhydroxide nanosheets during nitrogen reduction reactions.0.1 mol L-1 Na2SO4 is the electrolyte used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.0.1 mol/L Na2SO4 is the electrolyte used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction with the Pt/NiO-2 sample.The applied potential of 0.1 V vs RHE is a critical parameter in evaluating the performance of electrocatalysts in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). Specifically, this potential is utilized to compare the performance of the Fe3C/Fe2O3/Fe/C catalyst and the RuCu-FNs catalyst during the NRR. At this potential, both catalysts are assessed for their efficiency in converting nitrogen to ammonia, highlighting the importance of the applied voltage in optimizing electrocatalytic processes.0.10 eV is the limiting potential (UL) for the B-Ti-2 @C2N structure in the nitrogen reduction reaction.0.10 M HCl is the acidic electrolyte used in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) with the PTCA-rGO hybrid.0.10 m KOH is the electrolyte used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction with the modified hematite electrocatalyst.0.10 M Na2SO4 serves as the electrolyte in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) involving different electrocatalysts. Specifically, it is utilized with the TiO2/Ti electrocatalyst, as well as in conjunction with bismuth-based metal-organic frameworks. This electrolyte plays a crucial role in facilitating the NRR process, influencing the overall efficiency and yield of ammonia production in these systems.0.12 vol% water in LiClO4-ethyl acetate is the electrolyte used for the FeOx-coated carbon fiber paper cathode in nitrogen reduction reactions.0.13 mA cm(-2) is the partial current density achieved by Ru SAs/N-C during nitrogen reduction.0.16 V is the low overpotential at which Mo-COF demonstrates effective nitrogen reduction reaction performance.The applied potential of 0.17 V is significant for both MoB and MnB, as it indicates their selectivity over hydrogen evolution reactions. At this low overpotential, an NH3 Faradaic efficiency of approximately 21% was achieved, highlighting the effectiveness of these materials in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction while minimizing competing hydrogen evolution.0.172 Œºg¬∑h^(-1)¬∑cm^(-2) is the average ammonia yield achieved by Fe(1.0)HTNs in the assembled Zn-N2 aqueous batteries.0.18 V represents the low overpotential achieved by vanadium (V) single atom anchoring on a phosphorene monolayer, highlighting its significant catalytic activity in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This low overpotential indicates that the V single atom is an effective electrocatalyst, facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia with enhanced efficiency. The anchoring of the V atom on the phosphorene substrate plays a crucial role in optimizing the reaction conditions, thereby contributing to improved performance in NRR applications.0.2 M LiClO4 is the electrolyte used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction system for ammonia production.0.2 M PBS is another electrolyte used for the nitrogen reduction reaction with Ni2P nanoparticles.0.2 mA cm(-2) is the applied current density at which the Ni@MX electrocatalyst achieves its NH3 yield rate.0.2 V is the applied potential for the nitrogen reduction reaction using Ti3C2 at 20 degrees C.-0.20 V is the applied potential at which the Mo2C@3DUM-C electrocatalyst achieves its Faradaic efficiency of 9.5%.The limiting potential of -0.23 V is associated with the nitrogen reduction reaction when using FeMo/g-C3N4 as an electrocatalyst.)<|COMPLETE|>The term "0.24 eV" refers to two distinct contexts within electrochemical reactions involving different catalysts. Firstly, it represents the free energy increase for the potential-limiting step of the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) where N2* is converted to NNH* on the Ni-4-TiO2(101) catalyst. This indicates that a 0.24 eV energy barrier must be overcome for this specific transformation to occur, highlighting the catalyst's role in facilitating the NRR. Secondly, "0.24 eV" also denotes the lower reaction energy barrier for the -FeO(OH,F) nanorod compared to -FeOOH, suggesting that the -FeO(OH,F) nanorod is more favorable for certain electrochemical processes due to its reduced energy requirement. Together, these insights emphasize the importance of catalyst selection and design in optimizing electrochemical reactions, particularly in the context of nitrogen reduction.0.24 V is the applied potential for NbB, indicating its selectivity over hydrogen evolution reactions.0.25 +/- 0.03 mA cm(-2) is the maximum NH3 current density achieved by the Cu catalyst at -0.5 V vs RHE.0.25 V is the extremely low onset potential at which W1C3 exhibits the best performance for N2 reduction.The entity "0.25 V@5 mA cm(geo)(-2)" refers to a specific condition observed during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). It indicates a lower voltage loss, which is particularly noted in the absence of ethanol during the reaction. This lower voltage loss is also observed in the membrane electrode assembly (MEA) configuration, suggesting that the presence of ethanol may negatively impact the efficiency of the NRR process. Overall, the data highlights the importance of reaction conditions, such as the absence of ethanol, in optimizing the performance of electrochemical systems for nitrogen reduction.0.26 V is the limiting potential for the nitrogen reduction reaction involving W@Bis as the electrocatalyst.0.27 V is the limited potential at which Nb@GD demonstrates excellent performance for nitrogen reduction reactions.0.29 mu g h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield after 16 h of chronoamperometry tests, indicating poor durability of the system.0.29 V is the low overpotential for nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) achieved by the Fe-85 NC electrocatalyst.0.3 V vs RHE is the applied potential used for the nitrogen reduction reaction with Ti3C2Tx.0.3 wt % is the loading of Pt in the Pt-NiTiO3 electrocatalyst.The entity "0.31 mW cm(-2)" refers to the peak power density achieved by the Zn-N-2 battery. This power density is attained using two different cathode catalysts: CoPi/HSNPC and exfoliated NbS2. Specifically, the Zn-N-2 battery 