al highlights its potential as an electrocatalyst in optimizing nitrogen reduction reactions, thereby enhancing ammonia (NH3) yield and faradaic efficiency.0.4 V is the potential required by the two-electrode system to achieve an NH3 yield rate of 24.7 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1).0.4 wt % is the loading of Pd in the Pd-NiTiO3 electrocatalyst.0.40 eV is the lowest NRR energy barrier for TiO2 (101)/Ti3C2Tx, indicating its efficiency in the nitrogen reduction reaction.0.40 V is the applied potential for NbTe2, indicating its selectivity over hydrogen evolution reactions.0.41 V is the limiting potential for nitrogen reduction reactions involving Nb as a dopant.0.42 V is the onset potential for the N2-to-NH3 conversion via the distal mechanism with Mo-G/F.0.43 V is the limiting potential for nitrogen reduction reactions involving Mo as a dopant.0.44 V is the applied potential for TaB, indicating its selectivity over hydrogen evolution reactions.0.45 eV is the energy barrier for the conversion of *NH2 to *NH3 in the nitrogen reduction reaction, indicating a critical step in the process.0.45 V is the applied potential for the Fe-B/NC catalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.0.45 V vs RHE is the applied potential for the Fe-B/NC catalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The entity "0.46 MU G H(-1) CM(-2)" refers to the average ammonia (NH3) production rate achieved with a modified hematite nanostructure and the modified hematite electrocatalyst. This production rate indicates the effectiveness of the modified hematite in facilitating the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, highlighting its potential as a viable electrocatalyst for ammonia synthesis.The entity "0.49 eV" refers to two distinct electrochemical contexts. First, it represents the free energy increase for the potential-limiting step of the nitrogen reduction reaction, specifically the transition from NH* to NH2*, on the Ni-13-TiO2(101) catalyst. This indicates a significant energy barrier that must be overcome for effective nitrogen reduction to occur on this catalyst. Second, "0.49 eV" also denotes the low activation energy observed for the NiCe bimetallic alloy catalyst during the third hydrogenation step of the nitrogen reduction process. This suggests that the NiCe alloy facilitates the reaction more efficiently in this step compared to other catalysts, potentially leading to improved ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency. Overall, the value of 0.49 eV highlights important energetic characteristics of both the Ni-13-TiO2(101) and NiCe catalysts in the context of electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions.0.49% is the maximum Faradaic efficiency achieved at 25 degrees C with RuO2/PANI/N-MWCNT as the cathode.0.5 m is the concentration of K2SO4 solution used in the nitrogen fixation process.)<|COMPLETE|>0.5 m anhydrous lithium perchlorate is the electrolyte used for V-doped TiO2 nanorods during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).0.5 M LiClO4 serves as a crucial electrolyte in various electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions, demonstrating its versatility across multiple electrocatalysts. It is utilized in conjunction with copper nanoparticles supported on reduced graphene oxide (Cu NPs-rGO), as well as with surface modified reduced graphene oxide. Additionally, 0.5 M LiClO4 is employed in the nitrogen reduction reaction involving palladium phosphide (PdP2) supported on reduced graphene oxide, and in reactions utilizing beta-FeOOH and Co3HHTP2 as electrocatalysts. The electrolyte also facilitates the nitrogen reduction process under ambient conditions and is used with the -FeO(OH,F) nanorod, highlighting its effectiveness in enhancing the performance of various electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction applications.0.5 mol dm-3 indicates the concentration of NaOH used in the alkaline medium for the nitrogen reduction reaction with the Fe2P catalyst.0.5 mol dm-3 NaOH is the alkaline medium used for the nitrogen reduction reaction with the Fe2P catalyst.0.5 V vs RHE is the applied potential used for the nitrogen reduction reaction with Fe3O4@MoS2 as the catalyst.0.52% is the Faradaic efficiency of the nitrogen reduction reaction catalyzed by undoped G.The entity "0.53 V" represents a critical limiting potential value that indicates favorable conditions for nitrogen reduction reactions. Specifically, at this potential, the Mo@C6N6 sheet facilitates the distal pathway for nitrogen reduction, while the V/Fe-PTC catalyst allows for the efficient reduction of gas-phase N2 to NH3 through an enzymatic or distal mechanism. This low overpotential of 0.53 V is significant as it highlights the efficiency of these electrocatalysts in promoting the nitrogen reduction reaction, ultimately enhancing ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency.0.54 eV is the desorption free energy of NH3 for the FeFe catalyst, indicating its durability.The entity "0.55 V" serves as a critical threshold in the context of nitrogen reduction reactions. It is identified as the limiting potential for reactions that involve rhenium (Re) and osmium (Os) dopants, indicating that this voltage represents the maximum potential at which these doped systems can effectively facilitate the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia (NH3). Additionally, "0.55 V" is also recognized as the onset potential for the conversion of nitrogen (N2) to ammonia (NH3) via the distal mechanism when utilizing molybdenum-doped graphene/hybrid (Mo-G/H) materials. This dual significance underscores the importance of the 0.55 V potential in optimizing electrochemical processes aimed at enhancing ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency in nitrogen reduction reactions.0.55 V vs RHE is the applied potential for La2Ti2O7 during the nitrogen reduction reaction.0.566 mu mol NH3 (9.64 mu g) is the amount of ammonia produced in 24 hours hydrolysis at -2.3 V using the titanocene based electrocatalytic system.The entity "0.57 eV" represents a critical parameter in the context of electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It signifies the energy barrier for the NRR, which is essential for optimizing the reaction conditions to favor nitrogen fixation while minimizing the competing hydrogen evolution reaction (HER). Additionally, 0.57 eV denotes the desorption free energy of ammonia (NH3) for the CoCo catalyst, highlighting its durability and effectiveness in sustaining the reaction over time. Furthermore, this value is identified as the limiting potential for electrochemical nitrogen fixation using the Fe3@C2N catalyst, indicating its operational threshold in the NRR process. Collectively, these aspects underscore the significance of 0.57 eV in enhancing the performance and efficiency of various electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction applications.0.57 V is the onset potential for the potential determining step in the nitrogen reduction reaction using NiN4/Cr anchored graphene.The entity "0.58 V" represents a significant applied potential in the context of electrochemical reactions. It indicates the selectivity of the Nb@S catalyst over hydrogen evolution reactions, suggesting that at this potential, the catalyst effectively favors the desired reaction pathway rather than competing hydrogen evolution. Additionally, 0.58 V is identified as the overpotential for the potential-limiting step in the nitrogen reduction reaction, specifically the transition from NH* to NH2* on the Ni-13-TiO2(101) catalyst. This dual role of 0.58 V highlights its importance in optimizing the performance of electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction processes, as it influences both selectivity and reaction kinetics.0.58% is the Faradaic efficiency of the chromium nitride catalyst during the ENRR.0.59 eV is the reaction energy barrier for the -FeOOH electrocatalyst.)<|COMPLETE|>0.6 eV is used as a screening criterion for good activity in nitrogen reduction reactions.0.6 M LiClO4 with 0.5 vol% ethanol in tetrahydrofuran is the electrolyte used in the lithium-mediated nitrogen reduction system.0.6 V is the applied potential at which the Cr2O3-rGO catalyst achieves a faradaic efficiency of 7.33%.)<|COMPLETE|>0.6 V vs RHE is the applied potential used for the nitrogen reduction reaction with Ag nanosheets.The applied potential of 0.60 V serves a dual purpose in electrochemical reactions involving HfTe2 and V/Fe-PTC. For HfTe2, this potential indicates a notable selectivity over hydrogen evolution reactions, suggesting that it effectively minimizes unwanted side reactions while promoting the desired electrochemical processes. In the context of V/Fe-PTC, 0.60 V represents a low overpotential at which nitrogen gas (N2) can be efficiently reduced to ammonia (NH3). This characteristic highlights the catalyst's effectiveness in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), thereby enhancing ammonia yield and overall faradaic efficiency.0.64 V is the applied potential for HfSe2, indicating its selectivity over hydrogen evolution reactions.The entity "0.65 eV" represents two critical aspects of the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction facilitated by the Nb-sTCNQ catalyst. Firstly, it denotes the free energy change associated with the desorption of ammonia (NH3) produced during the reaction, indicating the energy required for NH3 to detach from the catalyst surface. Secondly, it signifies the rate-limiting barrier for the protonation of adsorbed nitrogen hydride (NH) on the catalyst surface, which is a crucial step in the nitrogen reduction process. Together, these values highlight the energetic challenges involved in optimizing the performance of the Nb-sTCNQ catalyst for efficient ammonia synthesis.0.68 eV is the activation barrier for the nitrogen reduction reaction when using Mo/BP as the catalyst.The applied potential of 0.7 V is utilized in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) for two distinct catalytic systems. Firstly, it is the potential applied to hollow NiCo-layered double hydroxide (LDH) nanocages, which serve as an electrocatalyst in this 