he Faradaic efficiency of Ti-FeP/CC at an optimal voltage of -0.3 V.The term "10.8%" refers to the faradaic efficiency achieved by two distinct electrocatalysts under different conditions. Firstly, the Bi nanodendrites electrocatalyst demonstrated a faradaic efficiency of 10.8% at applied potentials of -0.60 V and -0.55 V. Secondly, the Fe-MoS2/CC electrode also exhibited a faradaic efficiency of 10.8%, but this was measured at -0.1 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode. Both instances highlight the performance of these electrocatalysts in facilitating electrochemical reactions, specifically in the context of nitrogen reduction, where faradaic efficiency is a critical parameter for evaluating their effectiveness.10.9% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by C@NiO@Ni microtubes during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).10.93 mu g h-1 cm-2 is the NH3 yield of Ti-FeP/CC during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.10.94 % is the faradaic efficiency of the 1T-MoS2@Ti3C2 composite at -0.3 V vs RHE.100 % Faradaic efficiency indicates the high selectivity of the Fe4@g-C6N6 catalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.The data indicates that a Faradaic efficiency of 100% was achieved during nitrogen reduction reactions by multiple catalysts, specifically PC6-Fe-4 and PC6-Cr-3, as well as by the Pd-GDY catalyst in neutral media. This remarkable efficiency suggests that these catalysts are highly effective in converting nitrogen to ammonia, indicating their potential for optimizing electrochemical nitrogen reduction processes.100% of its original activity indicates the stability of the Pd3Cu1 alloy as an electrocatalyst after 18 hours of operation.)<|COMPLETE|>100.7 lg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved with the center dot H generated by sulfite and H2O under UV light.1004.1 m(2) g(-1) is the specific surface area of the Fe/Fe3O4/PC-800 composite catalyst, indicating its morphology.107.9 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield produced by CoPc nanotubes