n catalyst for N2 reduction.The entity "13.2%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved by various electrocatalysts under specific conditions. It is noted that the FeMoO4 electrocatalyst exhibits a Faradaic efficiency of 13.2% at a potential of -0.3 V. Similarly, iron oxyhydroxide nanosheets also achieve a Faradaic efficiency of 13.2% at a slightly lower potential of -0.4 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). Additionally, B/N-doped carbon nanofibers (B/N-CNF) reach the same Faradaic efficiency of 13.2% at a more negative potential of -0.5 V. This consistency in Faradaic efficiency across different electrocatalysts highlights the potential for optimizing electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions through careful selection of materials and operating conditions.The entity "13.3 %" refers to the Faradaic efficiency observed in two distinct electrochemical systems. Firstly, it represents the Faradaic efficiency of alpha-Fe2O3@mTiO2-400 during the nitrogen reduction reaction, indicating its effectiveness in converting nitrogen to ammonia under specific conditions. Secondly, the same percentage denotes the Faradaic efficiency of anatase TiO2/C composites when operated at a potential of -0.2 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). This suggests that both materials exhibit a similar level of efficiency in their respective nitrogen reduction processes, highlighting their potential as effective electrocatalysts in ammonia synthesis.13.3 +/- 0.4% is the Faradaic efficiency of Rh-Se NCs, showing a substantial increase compared to previous values.13.30% is the Faradaic efficiency of Ti3C2Tx QDs at -0.50 V during the nitrogen reduction reaction.13.36% is the Faraday efficiency achieved by the pAu/NF electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.13.4% is the optimal Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Cu clusters on ZrO2 nanofibers at -0.6 V.An optimal pH of 13.5 is identified as favorable for the nitrogen reduction reaction.13.5% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Ru-CNT GDE during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.13.6 mu g mg(cat)(-1) h(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved with copper nanoparticles loaded on oxygen-deficient TiO2 at -0.5 V versus RHE.13.74 lg center dot h-1 center dot mg-1 is the highest ammonia yield rate achieved by LiNbO3 cubes at the best potential.13.79% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by the metal-free catalyst (BCN) during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).The 13.8% Faradaic efficiency is associated with the performance of a 3D amorphous BiNi alloy electrocatalyst in the nitrogen reduction reaction. This efficiency reflects the effectiveness of the electrocatalyst in converting nitrogen to ammonia, highlighting its potential in electrochemical applications aimed at nitrogen fixation. The reported value indicates a specific performance metric for this electrocatalyst, underscoring its relevance in the study of nitrogen reduction processes.13.8% is the Faradaic efficiency (FE) achieved under 0.1 M KOH for the Cu single atom catalyst.13.9 +/- 1.0 % is the Faradaic efficiency of NbSe2 at -0.4 V vs RHE.134.08 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the maximum ammonia yield obtained from the carbon quantum dots, which is significantly higher than that of other metal-free materials.138.6 mW h g(zn)(-1) is the energy density achieved by the Zn-N-2 battery using CoPi/HSNPC as the cathode catalyst.14 s(-1) is the catalytic rate constant (k(cat)) for nitrogenase, indicating the reaction rate for nitrogen reduction.14% is the Faradaic efficiency of BNFC at -0.4 V vs. RHE.14.0 Œºg h^(-1) cm^(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved by anatase TiO2/C composites in the NRR process.)<|COMPLETE|>14.0 Œºg h^(-1) cm^(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved by anatase TiO2/C composites in the NRR process.14.022 ¬µg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by N-C@NiO/GP at -0.2 V vs RHE.14.03% is the Faradaic efficiency of C18@CoP/TM in 0.1 M Na2SO4, reflecting its effectiveness in NRR.14.1 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia yield of TiC/C at 0.1 M HCl during nitrogen reduction reaction.14.2% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Fe, MoN/C hollow porous nitrogen-doped carbon nanorods during nitrogen reduction.14.28 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the ammonia yield rate for Pd-NiTiO3 electrocatalysts.14.33 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the highest ammonia yield rate achieved at -0.2 V using TiO2-delta N delta.14.4 mu g.h(-1).mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield of the boron-doped TiO2 catalyst in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.14.5 % is the Faradaic efficiency of 450-NiMnO3 at -0.1 V vs. RHE, indicating its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.14.5 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of Pd-rGO during the N-2 reduction reaction.14.54% is the Faradaic efficiency of the La-doped TiO2 catalyst at an applied potential of -0.70 V.14.6% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the FeOOH quantum dots decorated graphene sheet in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).14.62% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by Ti3+-TiO2-x/TM in 0.1 M Na2SO4.The entity "14.7%" refers to the faradaic efficiency observed in various electrocatalysts during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). Specifically, it is reported that FeCoOOH hollow nanocapsules (HNCs) exhibit a faradaic efficiency of 14.7%. Additionally, the nPd/NF catalyst also demonstrates a faradaic efficiency of 14.7%, which is noted to be lower than that of the nPdB/NF catalyst. Furthermore, the MnMoO4 nanorods-reduced graphene oxide catalyst achieves the same faradaic efficiency of 14.7% during the NRR. This consistency in faradaic efficiency across different catalysts highlights a common performance metric in the context of nitrogen reduction, although the specific structural and compositional characteristics of each catalyst may influence their overall effectiveness and optimization potential in electrochemical applications.14.8% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by PdNCs@CNFs with a current density of 0.028 mA cm(-2).14.85 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia yield of the VO2 hollow microspheres catalyst under specific conditions.14.88 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by the Ag/TiO2 catalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.14.88 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by the TiO2 electrocatalyst composited with Ag.The entity "14.9%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved with the Pt/TiO2-x nanofibrous aerogel during the nitrogen reduction reaction. Specifically, this efficiency of 14.9% is noted at a potential of -0.35 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). This indicates that the Pt/TiO2-x nanofibrous aerogel demonstrates a measurable performance in the electrochemical conversion of nitrogen, highlighting its potential as an effective electrocatalyst in this reaction.14.95 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by the atomic Fe and Mo metal dimer catalyst at -0.4 V.143 ¬± 3 ¬µg h(-1) mgcat(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of the ZnMn2O4 electrocatalyst derived from spent primary ZnC battery.143.38 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) cat is the NH3 yield rate for the NRR using CoPc nanotubes.1453 cm(-1) is a frequency associated with H-N-H bending in the N2Hy species during nitrogen reduction.146 mV is the lower onset overpotential found for the Fe-ReS2@N-CNF catalyst compared to pristine ReS2.149 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved with the Rh atomic layer-decorated SnO2 heterostructure.15 h is the duration over which the stability of the NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency of Ru88Pt12 is preserved during electrocatalysis.15 homonuclear dual-atom catalysts (DACs) are explored for their potential in nitrogen reduction reactions.The 15 hour continuous N-2 reduction is a reaction condition used to determine the stability of the NiFe@MoS2 catalyst.15.13 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield of TiO2-rGO at -0.90 V vs. RHE, indicating its effectiveness in ammonia production.15.18 +/- 0.78 mu g h(