ncy in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction, highlighting its potential for effective ammonia synthesis under specified electrochemical conditions.15.7 ŒºgNH3 h-1 mgcat.-1 is the NH3 yield rate of BNC-NSs at -400 mV vs. RHE in 0.05 M H2SO4 electrolyte.15.8% is the Faraday efficiency achieved by the D-FeN/C electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.15.83 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield of the LiMn2O4 electrocatalyst at -0.50 V vs RHE.15.95% is the faradaic efficiency of the boron carbide nanosheet electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).150 mV is the low overpotential at which palladium hydride achieves a high ammonia yield rate and Faradaic efficiency.150 mV is the low overpotential at which palladium hydride achieves its NH3 yield and Faradaic efficiency.150-350 atm is the pressure range typically used in the industrial Haber-Bosch process for ammonia synthesis.155.52 mu W cm-2 is the power density achieved by the Zn-N2 battery in the context of nitrogen reduction.158.66 mu mol center dot cm(-2) center dot h(-1) is the overall yield of catalytic nitrate reduction using the Cu/Cu2O@C catalyst.15N isotopic labeling is an experimental method used to confirm the source of nitrogen in produced ammonia.16 Œºg h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 production yield of the Fe-CuS/C catalyst at -0.4 V-RHE.16 nm is the approximate size of the RuFeCoNiCu nanoparticles, indicating their morphology.16% is the faradaic efficiency for rounded Ag nanoparticles.16.0 +/- 1.1% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Fe/Cu catalyst in an alkaline electrolyte of 0.1 M KOH.16.02 Œºg¬∑h^(-1)¬∑mg(cat)^(-1) is the ammonia yield achieved by TiS2 NSs at -0.6 V vs RHE.16.07% is the Faradaic efficiency of the electrocatalyst at -0.45 V versus RHE in 0.1 m HCl.16.10 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by O-v-TiO2@C/Cu at -0.60 V vs RHE.16.2 +/- 0.8 mu g h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield for the nickel-thiolate cluster [Ni-6(PET)(12)].16.2 Œºg/h cm(2) i