tion performance.18 +/- 3% is the maximum Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Cu catalyst at -0.3 V vs RHE.18% is the faradaic efficiency achieved with the optimal catalyst at -0.35 V vs RHE.18.16 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield of Fe-2(MoO4)(3) nanoparticles in 0.1 M sodium sulfate at -0.6 V vs RHE.18.28 ¬± 1.6 ¬µg h(-1) mg(1)cat. is the NH3 yield of FeTPPCl, indicating its effectiveness in nitrogen reduction reactions.18.3 mu A center dot cm(-2) is the partial current density corresponding to the ammonia production rate of the Ti3C2Tx MXene electrocatalyst.18.3 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of the optimized Pt3Fe nanowires during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The porous Ni91.5Bi8.5 electrocatalyst demonstrates an ammonia yield of 18.35 Œºg h-1 mg-1. This performance metric highlights the catalyst's effectiveness in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction, making it a noteworthy candidate for applications in electrochemical ammonia synthesis.18.5 % is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the U-L Co3O4 electrocatalyst in the ENRR, indicating its effectiveness in producing NH3.18.56% is the Faradaic efficiency of PdH0.43 NRs, indicating its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.18.6 +/- 0.8% is the faradaic efficiency observed with the Fe single-atom catalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.18.6 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by the NiO/G electrocatalyst during nitrogen reduction reactions.The entity "18.79%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the electrocatalyst Au/Fe-2(MoO4)(3) during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (eNRR). This specific Faradaic efficiency of 18.79% is noted to occur at an applied potential of -0.4 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). This indicates that under these conditions, the Au/Fe-2(MoO4)(3) catalyst demonstrates a measurable performance in converting nitrogen to ammonia, highlighting its potential effectiveness in electrochemical applications related to ni