ecific configuration of 2D-Fe2C12 that shows enhanced performance in NRR due to cooperative interactions of Fe sites.1T-MoS2@Ti3C2 is a composite catalyst that exhibits outstanding NRR activity with a faradic efficiency of 10.94% and a NH3 yield rate of 30.33 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1).The Mo-doped W18O49 catalyst achieves an NH3 yield that is about two times higher than that of pristine W18O49.2.0 mA cm(-2) is the current measured during the nitrogen reduction reaction at an applied potential of 0 V.2.0 V is the applied potential used in the ammonia synthesis test with the hybrid composite material (HCM) cathodes.2.19 mu g/cm(2).h is the NH3 yield rate of the MXene/TiFeOx-700 catalyst, equivalent to 21.9 mu g/mg(cat).h.2.1x10(-9) mol/cm(2) s is the highest reported NH3 yield rate achieved using the Ru-CNT GDE in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.2.25% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved with the VN nanosheet array electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.2.26% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Nb2O5/CC catalyst in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).2.3 mu mol h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved by the Ru@MXene catalyst in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).2.32 ¬µg h(-1) cm(-2) is the stable NH3 yield produced at -0.2 V vs RHE using Au single sites stabilized on N-doped porous carbon.The NH3 yield rate of 2.35 X 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(GsA)(-2) is achieved with alpha-Fe nanorods under ambient conditions.The entity "2.41 +/- 0.14 mu mol h(-1) cm(geo)(-2)" represents the mean ammonia production rate achieved in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction. This rate is specifically noted to be obtained in the membrane electrode assembly (MEA) configuration, indicating its relevance to the performance of the electrochemical system in producing ammonia.2.45 x 10(-9) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the production rate of NH3 achieved in the electrochemical reduction of purged N2 gas.2.45x10(-10) mols(-1) cm(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved at -0.40 V vs RHE with the optimal catalyst.2.48 x 10(-10) mol(-1) s(-2) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved by the VN/CC electrocatalyst.2.50 % is the faradaic efficiency of the TiO2/Ti electrocatalyst at -0.7 V vs reversible hydrogen electrode.2.54 ¬± 0.16 Œºg(NH3) cm(-2) h(-1) is the average ammonia yield of the Bi NS at -0.8 V vs. RHE.2.6% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by Fe3O4/Ti in the nitrogen reduction reaction.The entity "2.7 X 10(-7) mmol mg(-1) s(-1)" represents the ammonia formation rate at an applied potential of -0.577 V. This value indicates the efficiency of the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction under the specified conditions, highlighting the performance of the catalyst in facilitating ammonia production.2.74 % is the faradaic efficiency after 16 h of chronoamperometry tests, indicating poor durability of the system.2.76% is the faradaic efficiency of the MoS2@Fe(OH)(3) catalyst in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.2.81 x 10(-5) mu mol center dot s(-1) center dot cm(-2) is the ammonia production rate achieved by the Ti3C2Tx MXene electrocatalyst.2.9 mu g mg(cat).(-1) h(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by CN/C-600 at -0.3 V versus RHE.20 degrees C is the temperature at which the nitrogen reduction reaction using Ti3C2 is performed to achieve a specific yield and efficiency.20 g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the maximum ammonia yield rate achieved in the study using gold-based catalysts.The entity "20 h" refers to the duration over which various electrocatalysts demonstrate stability and performance in nitrogen reduction reactions. Specifically, CoPc-C3N4 exhibits stability for 20 hours during these reactions, while the N-C@NiO/GP electrocatalyst showcases excellent electrocatalytic activity and structural stability over the same period. Additionally, the 2D C3N4-NV electrocatalyst was also tested for stability in nitrogen reduction reactions for 20 hours. This consistent duration across different electrocatalysts highlights the importance of stability in optimizing their performance for ammonia synthesis.20 hours is the duration for which the electrochemical durability of the catalyst is preserved during the nitrogen reduction process.20.03 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield of the hybrid TiO2 and juncus effusus-derived carbon microtubes catalyst.20.09 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of Fe-2(MoO4)(3) nanoparticles in 0.1 M hydrochloric acid.20.4 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved with palladium hydride as the electrocatalyst.20.4 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved with Ti3C2Tx MXene nanosheets at -0.4 V vs RHE.The entity "20.4%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved in various electrocatalytic systems for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). Specifically, this efficiency is attained by the Fe-SnO2 electrocatalyst, which demonstrates effective performance in catalyzing NRR. Additionally, the optimized morphology of Fe-based nanoparticles also achieves a Faradaic efficiency of 20.4%, indicating that structural enhancements can significantly influence catalytic performance. Furthermore, mesoporous Rh-Te alloy films (mRhTe/CP) exhibit the same Faradaic efficiency of 20.4% during nitrogen reduction reactions, highlighting the potential of different materials and morphologies in achieving comparable catalytic efficiencies in NRR. Overall, the consistent Faradaic efficiency of 20.4% across these diverse electrocatalysts underscores the importance of material selection and structural optimization in enhancing nitrogen reduction performance.20.50 % is the energy efficiency of the NRR process using Rh nanoparticles anchored on CNT.20.59 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield of the Pt/NiO-2 sample at -0.2 V vs. RHE, indicating its effectiveness in nitrogen reduction reactions.20.7 % is the faradaic efficiency achieved by the yolk-shell microcage electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.The entity "20.80 ¬µg h‚Åª¬π mg‚Åª¬π" represents the ammonia (NH3) yield associated with two different electrocatalytic systems for nitrogen reduction. Specifically, this yield is observed for Bi nanoparticles supported on carbon materials (Bi NPs@CRs) as well as for a bismuth metal-organic framework (Bi-MOF) electrocatalyst, both measured under specific applied potentials. This indicates that both systems exhibit the same NH3 production rate, highlighting their potential effectiveness in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions.20.82% is the faradaic efficiency observed with P-doped graphene during the nitrogen reduction reaction.20.9% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Ag-Cu-NS/CP catalyst in 0.1 M Na2SO4 electrolyte.The MnO (200) surface morphology has a smaller adsorption energy toward N than that of H, influencing its catalytic activity in nitrogen reduction.200 mmol g(-1)h(-1) is the ammonia yield obtained in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) using bismuth and potassium cations.204 mu g h-1 mgcat-1 is the NH3 yield rate achieved by C-BN@600 at -0.2 V vs RHE.21 +/- 1.9% is the faradaic efficiency achieved at -0.2 V vs RHE under alkaline conditions, indicating the effectiveness of the electrocatalyst.21% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved at a low overpotential of 0.17 V.21.12% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by Cu@NC nanocubes.21.29 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of the Ni@MX electrocatalyst, indicating its effectiveness in ammonia production.21.36 kJ mol(-1) is the activation energy for the nitrogen reduction reaction involving Mo-PTA.21.42 mu g mg(cat)(-1) h(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved using Au-Fe3O4 nanoparticles for NRR.21.60 +/- 2.35% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the modified MoSx catalyst in nitrogen reduction reactions.21.7 % is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Ag@AgP2@Ni-CoP catalyst at -0.3 V vs RHE.21.7 +/- 1.8% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Fe(II)Cu(II)Fe(III)-LDH catalyst.21.79% is the Faradaic efficiency of Co@NC at -0.9 V vs Ag/AgCl, indicating its superior performance in the NRR process.21.86 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate observed after 100 consecutive cycles with the Fe-TPPHZ catalyst.21.9 % is the Faradaic efficiency achieved in HCl aqueous solution under ambient conditions for the Ag-based electrocatalyst.21.99% is the faradaic efficiency achieved with the FeP2 catalyst at -0.40 V vs RHE.219.72 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia yield rate achieved by the GDY/Co2N electrocatalyst in acidic conditions.22 mu g mg(cat)(-1)h(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by Fe2O3 nanoparticles in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).22.14 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved with Co3HHTP2 at -0.40 V vs RHE.22.18 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the maximum NH3 yield achieved with the NiCo oxide nanoflower structure on the CNS.22.23% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the optimized MoS2 electrocatalyst.22.26 % is the faradaic efficiency of the Fe/Fe3O4/PC-800 composite catalyst in nitrogen reduction reactions.22.27 Œºg h^(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by the BHGO catalyst in the NRR process.22.27 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield of the BHGO catalyst, indicating its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.22.4 Œºg(NH3)¬∑h‚Åª¬π¬∑mg(cat)‚Åª¬π is the NH3 formation rate of the Mn2O3-MnO/Mn3O4 catalyst at -0.3 V vs. RHE.22.68 mu g h(-1) mgcat(-1) is the NH3 production rate achieved by the Ni/Zn-NPC electrocatalyst at -1.0 V.22.7 Œºg h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield of the electrocatalyst N-NiO/CC.22.7 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by the S-mAu film/NF electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.22.7% is the faradaic efficiency of the electrocatalyst (V,Ni,Mn)(3)O4-x/Nb2O5-y, indicating its effectiveness in the nitrogen reduction reaction.22.8 % is the Faradaic efficiency accomplished by the PdFe3 nano-alloy electrocatalyst at a low overpotential.22.9 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield of the Pd3Cu bimetallic catalyst.23.06 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield of the La-doped TiO2 catalyst, indicating its effectiveness in nitrogen reduction reactions.23.15% is the Faradaic efficiency of the NiCoP/CoMoP/Co(Mo3Se4)(4) @C/NF electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.23.23 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield of CoS/MoS2, indicating its high efficiency in ammonia synthesis.23.31% is the outstanding Faradaic efficiency achieved by the three-phase electrocatalytic system.23.31% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the three-phase electrocatalytic system using FeMoSe2.23.32 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by beta-FeOOH in 0.5 M LiClO4.23.34% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by Ni/NiFe-OH at -0.15 V vs RHE.23.48 % is the Faradaic efficiency of the NRR process using Rh nanoparticles anchored on CNT.23.6 mW cm(-2) is the power density achieved by the Fe-Cu DAC in the Zn-NO2 battery system.23.8% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by the Mn2O3-MnO/Mn3O4 catalyst.23.84 % is the Faradaic efficiency obtained by the mAu3Rh/NF electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction.2300 cycles is the number of cycles the assembled rechargeable zinc-air batteries (ZABs) sustained with minimal degradation in performance.235.0 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved using the fluidized electrocatalysis system with Co3O4 nanoparticles.24 h refers to the duration for which the active species from Fe2O3-CNT samples remain stable during electrocatalytic flow tests.)<|COMPLETE|>24,526 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by the Fe-Cu DAC at -0.6 V.24-fold improvement in Faradaic efficiency is observed when using a tailored microenvironment with a transition metal-based catalyst compared to the bare one.24-fold improvement in Faradaic efficiency is achieved with the tailored microenvironment compared to the bare catalyst.The entity "24.12 ¬µg h‚Åª¬π mg(cat)‚Åª¬π" represents the ammonia (NH3) yield achieved in two distinct electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). First, this yield was attained 