posite catalyst at -0.5 V under ambient conditions.250 degrees C is the maximum temperature at which the high-entropy alloys RuFeCoNiCu nanoparticles were prepared.26.0 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the ammonia production rate achieved by the NiCoS/C nanocages in the electrocatalytic NRR.26.01 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) cat. is the NH3 yield achieved by Ti2O3 in an acidic electrolyte.26.16 mu g cm(-2) h(-1) is the NH3 yield of the Ni@Nb2C MXene-based catalyst.26.25 mu g h-1 mgcat.-1 is the ammonia yield rate of the optimized Ru0.15Cu0.85 NS catalyst.26.29 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia yield achieved by the mAu3Rh/NF electrocatalyst.26.3% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Fe2O3/Fe3O4-CeO2 electrocatalyst in nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).26.32 mu g mg(-1) cat. is the NH3 yield achieved by the TiO2/Ti3C2Tx hybrid catalyst.26.4 mu g h-1 cm-2 is the NH3 formation rate achieved on Pt-HEX during the nitrogen reduction reaction.26.44 mu g mgcat-1h-1 is the NH3 yield achieved by the optimized morphology of the Fe-based nanoparticles in nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) catalysis.26.5 +/- 0.8 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of FeMo/NC at -0.3 V in neutral electrolyte.The data indicates that a Faradaic efficiency of 26.5% is achieved with two different electrocatalysts: the Au-25-Cys-Mo catalyst and the Zn-Fe electrocatalyst, both of which are utilized for nitrogen reduction reactions. This efficiency reflects the effectiveness of these catalysts in converting electrical energy into chemical energy during the nitrogen reduction process, highlighting their potential in optimizing ammonia production through electrochemical methods.26.57 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia yield achieved by the boron carbide nanosheet electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).26.7% is the Faradaic efficiency for Pt-Y in the nitrogen reduction reaction.26.91 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by Rh nanoparticles anchored on CNT during NRR.27 single transition metals are used in the study of SACs supported by N(O)-dual-doped graphene for nitrogen electroreduction.The entity "27%" refers to the faradaic efficiency achieved in two distinct electrochemical processes involving different electrocatalysts. Firstly, it represents the faradaic efficiency of 27% for Pd-NiTiO3 electrocatalysts when operating at a potential of 0.097 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). Secondly, the same value of 27% is noted for the conversion of nitrogen (N2) to ammonia (NH3) using the [Fe-S-2-Mo] electrocatalyst. This indicates that both electrocatalysts demonstrate the same level of efficiency in their respective nitrogen reduction reactions, highlighting their potential effectiveness in ammonia synthesis.27.0% is the Faradaic efficiency of the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction using Mo3Fe3C at 0.05 V vs RHE.27.1% is the faradaic efficiency achieved at -0.3 V (vs. RHE) with UV irradiation, indicating the effectiveness of the center dot H in the NRR process.27.16 +/- 2.22 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by Co3O4 at -0.49 V vs. RHE in 0.1 M Na2SO4 solution.27.2 Œºg h^(-1) mg(cat)^(-1) is the NH3 yield of alpha-Fe2O3@mTiO2-400 at -0.5 V vs RHE.27.3 % is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Au nanoparticle-embedded ZnSe photo-electrode.27.3 mu g center dot h(-1) center dot mg(cat.)^{-1} is the ammonia yield rate achieved by the FeOOH quantum dots decorated graphene sheet in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).27.3 mu g center dot h^{-1} center dot mg(cat.)^{-1} is the ammonia yield rate achieved by the FeOOH quantum dots decorated graphene sheet in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).)<|COMPLETE|>27.3 mu g h(-1) mg(cat).(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by S-G in the nitrogen reduction reaction.27.3% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Sb/Nb2CTx electrocatalyst.27.5% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by FeNiPBA nanocrystals in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).27.7% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by CoPc nanotubes in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.27.9 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of the FeNi-Co@CM electrode in 1.0 M KOH.270.9 Œºg h^(-1) mg(cat)^(-1) is the record-high NH3 yield rate achieved by the synthesized Ag sites.28.1 kJ mol^(-1) is the calculated activation energy for nitrogen reduction on the Mo2C@3DUM-C electrocatalyst.28.1 kJ mol^(-1) is the activation energy calculated for nitrogen reduction on the Mo2C@3DUM-C electrocatalyst.28.2 % is the Faradaic efficiency of DR ZnS-rGO at the specified applied potential.28.43 lig cm-2 h-1 is the ammonia yield achieved by the Ag catalyst with Bi0 sites at high pressure.28.5 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of the iron oxyhydroxide nanosheets electrocatalyst, indicating its high efficiency.28.64 +/- 0.18% is the Faradaic efficiency (FE) achieved by the optimized FeNi2S4/NiS electrocatalyst.28.9 Œºg h-1 cm-2 is the ammonia yield rate of the nanoporous CuMn (np-CuMn) alloy electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.The entity "28.9%" refers to two distinct metrics related to electrocatalytic performance. Firstly, it represents the Faradaic efficiency of molybdenum-based single atom catalysts at a specified applied potential, indicating the effectiveness of these catalysts in converting electrical energy into chemical energy during electrochemical reactions. Secondly, it denotes the production energy efficiency associated with the electrocatalytic activity of an alloy composed of gold, silver, and palladium, specifically at a temperature of 850¬∞C. Both metrics highlight the performance of different catalytic systems in electrochemical processes, emphasizing the importance of optimizing catalyst composition and conditions to enhance efficiency in nitrogen reduction reactions.287.2 +/- 10.0 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved using a traditional carbon-based catalyst in the electrochemical ammonia synthesis.289 mu g center dot h(-1) center dot mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 formation rate obtained from the Fe-0.50-Bi2WO6 catalyst at -0.75 V vs. RHE.289 Œºg¬∑h‚Åª¬π¬∑mg(cat)‚Åª¬π is the NH3 yield rate obtained from the Fe-0.50-Bi2WO6 catalyst.29 +/- 19 pmolNH3 s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield rate achieved under specific reaction conditions.29 ns is the average lifetime of excitons in the optimized catalyst, indicating its efficiency in charge separation.29.0 mu g h-1 mg-cat.1 is the NH3 yield of nPdB/NF in 0.1 M Na2SO4.29.07 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of the Fe-TPPHZ catalyst, indicating its efficiency in ammonia production.29.07 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia production rate achieved by the PdFe3 nano-alloy electrocatalyst.29.3% is the optimized faradaic efficiency achieved by the Fe-Ru single atom catalyst during nitrogen reduction reactions.29.6% is the Faradaic efficiency for NH3 production achieved by Ru SAs/N-C.2D C3N4-NV is a metal-free catalyst with a large specific surface area and abundant nitrogen vacancies for electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction.2D materials are a class of materials highlighted for their promising properties in nitrogen reduction reactions due to their structural versatility.2D Mo-Pc is a promising electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) due to its low onset potential and strong Mo-N interactions.2D NRR electrocatalysts refer to two-dimensional materials that have been reported for nitrogen reduction reactions, often compared to the performance of CuAg/Ti(3)C(2)MXene.)<|COMPLETE|>The 2D pi-conjugated polymer (2DCP) is a specialized type of two-dimensional material that serves as a supported single-atom catalyst (SAC). It is utilized in conjunction with other materials to significantly enhance the performance of nitrogen reduction reactions. By supporting SACs, 2DCP improves their catalytic properties, thereby optimizing the efficiency and yield of ammonia production in electrochemical processes.2D transition metal borides (MBenes) are novel catalysts used to boost nitrogen reduction to ammonia while inhibiting the hydrogen evolution reaction.2D-Fe2C12 is a newly-reported electrocatalyst with high activity and selectivity for nitrogen reduction reactions.2D-materials refer to materials that have a thickness of a few atoms and exhibit unique properties due to their two-dimensional structure, often used in the context of catalysis and material science.3 to 5% of the world's natural gas is consumed in ammonia synthesis, indicating its significance in global ammonia production.3-10 nm refers to the size of the Pt nanoparticles used to dope NiO nanosheets, which improves their electrocatalytic performance.A 3-fold increase in current density contributed by nitrogen reduction is observed in high-pH electrolyte compared to low-pH electrolyte.3.0 mu g NH3 g(cat)(-1) h(-1) is the NH3 yield of Zif-8@Ti3C2 at -0.55 V/RHE.3.0 Œºg(NH3)h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved with engineered surface oxygen vacancies of TiO2.3.0% is the Faradaic efficiency of the nitrogen reduction reaction catalyzed by Mn3O4.The entity "3.01 x 10(-10) mol s(-1) cm(-2)" represents the ammonia (NH3) yield achieved by the MoN nanosheets array on carbon cloth, as well as the yield obtained from the MoN NA/CC electrocatalyst. This value indicates the performance of the MoN-based electrocatalyst in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction, highlighting its effectiveness in producing ammonia under the specified conditions.3.04 mu mol h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 production rate achieved by Cu/Ti3C2 at -0.5 V vs RHE.3.08 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the ammonia yield for bare NiTiO3 electrocatalysts.3.08 x 10(-10) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield rate of the Ni12P5/FeP4 electrocatalyst, indicating its effectiveness in ammonia production.3.19 mg h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved from the quaternary system of the electrocatalyst at a specific applied potential.3.25 +/- 0.08 mu g cm(-2)h(-1) is the NH3 yield of bismuth-based metal-organic frameworks at -0.7 V (vs. RHE).3.26% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Pd3Cu bimetallic catalyst at an overpotential of -0.25 V.3.3 % is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by TiO2-rGO in nitrogen reduction reactions.3.32 x 10(-)(10) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the yield rate of NH3 produced by the TiN-PE electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.3.34% is the faradaic efficiency achieved with Co3HHTP2 at -0.40 V vs RHE.3.4% is the Faradaic efficiency of the boron-doped TiO2 catalyst in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.3.4x10(-6) mol cm(-2) h(-1) is the NH3 production rate of the metal-organic framework-derived nitrogen-doped nanoporous carbon electrocatalyst.3.5 wt % refers to the loading of Fe single atoms in the Fe-N/C electrocatalyst.3.5% is the Faradaic efficiency of NH3 production from the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) using Fe2O3 nanoparticles at -0.5 V vs RHE.3.51 x 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved by Ti3+-TiO2-x/TM at -0.55 V vs. the reversible hydrogen electrode.3.52 % is the faradaic efficiency of the Mn3O4@rGO electrocatalyst at -0.85 V.3.52% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved at -0.85 V during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.The entity "3.6 V" refers to the cell voltage applied during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction. This specific voltage is significant as it is the level at which a mean ammonia production rate and Faradaic efficiency were achieved in the proposed membrane electrode assembly (MEA) configuration. Thus, 3.6 V plays a crucial role in optimizing the performance of the electrochemical process for nitrogen reduction, influencing both the yield of ammonia and the efficiency of the reaction.3.60 % is the Faradaic efficiency of the Ag NPs-rGO catalyst at -0.7 V vs RHE.3.61 x 10(-10) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield of ReS2/CC at -0.4 V versus RHE.3.61 x 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved by CoP3/CC at -0.20 V vs RHE.3.665