 performance indicates the stability of the amorphous PdCoCu NSs after a long test period.)<|COMPLETE|>5.05 x 10(-10) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved by the Co-Ni-S/Ni foam catalyst.5.14 Œºg cm^(-2)h^(-1) is the NH3 yield at 0.05 V vs RHE, indicating the performance of the electrocatalyst at this potential.5.19% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the aerophilic-hydrophilic heterostructured electrode in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).5.2 mg/h cm(2) is the NH3 yield rate of the Cu-nanosphere electrode when reducing NO3- in a nitrate environment at -1.3 V.5.22% is the Faradaic efficiency of the gold-based catalysts for ammonia production.5.25% is the estimated energy efficiency of the nitrogen reduction reaction at the current Faradaic efficiency of 11.56%.5.3 % is the faradaic efficiency of oxygen vacancy-rich NiCo2O4 on hollow N-carbon polyhedra.The entity "5.3 ¬µg(NH3) h(-1) mgcat.(-1)" represents the ammonia (NH3) yield rate achieved with a Mo-doped W18O49 catalyst under specific electrochemical conditions. This yield rate of 5.3 ¬µg(NH3) h(-1) mgcat.(-1) is observed at an applied potential of -0.5 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) in a 0.1 M sodium sulfate (Na2SO4) electrolyte. This data highlights the performance of the Mo-doped W18O49 catalyst in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction, indicating its potential effectiveness in ammonia synthesis under the specified conditions.5.45 x 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by structured polyaniline as an efficient metal-free NRR electrocatalyst.The faradaic efficiency improved from 5.5% to 18.1% after surface modification of Ti3C2Tix MXene at -0.7 V (vs. RHE).5.50% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by TiS2 NSs in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.5.51% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the MXene/Mn3O4 composite catalyst during nitrogen reduction reactions.5.6 x 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield of Fe3O4/Ti in th