RHE). This efficiency is consistent across different descriptions, confirming that the porous structure of the Ni91.5Bi8.5 catalyst contributes to its performance in electrochemical reactions.51.2 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield of DR ZnS-rGO in 0.1 M HCl.51.4 % is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the FeOOH-150 catalyst at a specific applied potential.52.8 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield obtained from the hollow NiCo-LDH nanocages at -0.7 V.53% is the nitrite selectivity achieved in the ternary state of the cathode assembly during the nitrate-reduction reaction (NRR).53.12 mu g(NH3) h-1 mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by the Fe SAC/N-C catalyst.53.2 +/- 1.8 % is the highest faradaic efficiency achieved by Fe-Fe3O4 in nitrogen reduction reactions.53.3 mu g(NH3) h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved under 0.1 M KOH for the Cu single atom catalyst.53.6 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by RuCu-FNs at -0.1 V vs RHE.53.99% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by the atomically dispersed metal-nitrogen-carbon catalyst with optimized plasma etching treatment.54.5 +/- 1.1% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved in the electrochemical ammonia synthesis using a traditional carbon-based catalyst.54.8% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by F-doped carbon for NH3 production during N2 electroreduction.540 systems were screened for catalysts for N2 reduction, leading to the identification of 10 promising candidates.55.6 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by the Ir/RGO electrocatalyst during nitrogen reduction reactions.55.9 nmol h(-1) mg(-1) is the weight-normalized activity of gamma-Fe2O3 when coated onto porous carbon paper for N2 reduction.56.9 % is the maximum Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Lewis acid pair catalyst at a peak potential of -0.1 V vs. RHE.56.9 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the ammonia yield rate achieved by the nanoporous NiSb alloy electrocatalyst for nitrogen fixation.57.1 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by RuFeCoNiCu at a low overpotential.57.17 +/- 4.14 % is the Faradaic efficiency observed in electrochemical tests on NiS catalysts.571 +/- 0.1 mu g h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield from bismuthene nanostructures in the NRR.58.3 Œºg h(-1) mg(-1) is the ammonia yield rate achieved under ambient conditions with the N2 microextractor (NME) system.58.5 mg g(Ag)(-1) h(-1) is the ammonia yield for Ag triangular nanoplates at a low overpotential.58.5 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the highest ammonia production rate achieved by the NiCoS/C nanocages with increasing potential.58.60 % is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the GDY/Co2N electrocatalyst during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.58.82 mu g h-1 mg-1cat is the NH3 yield rate for the NO3RR using CoPc nanotubes.59.7 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of V2O3/VN-2 during the nitrogen reduction reaction.599 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved at -0.5 V with the Fe-Mn-Ga oxide electrocatalyst.6 M KOH is the alkaline electrolyte used for the electrocatalytic N2 reduction reaction under alkaline conditions.6.02 mu mol center dot cm(-2) center dot h(-1) is the NH3 yield of the Cu/Cu2O@C catalyst for nitrogen reduction.6.04 % is the faradaic efficiency (FE) of the NH3 production using the modified hematite electrocatalyst at -0.9 V vs. Ag/AgCl.The entity "6.04%" refers to the faradaic efficiency achieved in two distinct electrochemical systems. In the first instance, a composite material designated as O-v-TiO2@C/Cu attained a faradaic efficiency of 6.04% when operated at a potential of -0.55 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). In a separate study, a modified hematite nanostructure also achieved the same faradaic efficiency of 6.04%, but this was measured at a different potential of -0.9 V versus the silver/silver chloride (Ag/AgCl) reference electrode. This indicates that both systems exhibit comparable faradaic efficiencies, albeit under different electrochemical conditions.6.1 +/- 0.9% is the Faradaic efficiency obtained on the best-performing sample at -0.1 V versus RHE.6.1% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by the CeO2 nanoparticle decorated carbon skeleton at -0.6 V vs. RHE.The entity "6.2 %" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved in two distinct electrochemical systems. Firstly, it represents the Faradaic efficiency of CoFe2O4 nanoclusters anchored on reduced graphene oxide during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). Secondly, it also denotes the Faradaic efficiency of a TiO2 electrocatalyst composited with silver (Ag) at a potential of -0.70 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). Both systems demonstrate a Faradaic efficiency of 6.2 %, highlighting the performance of these electrocatalysts in facilitating nitrogen reduction processes.6.2% is the faradaic efficiency of the Ag/TiO2 catalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.6.23% is the optimum Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Co-Ni-S/Ni foam catalyst.6.25 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by undoped G in the nitrogen reduction reaction.The Faradaic efficiency of 6.25% is achieved by Al-doped Co3O4 in 0.1 M KOH.6.29% is the Faradaic efficiency of alpha-CuPc at a different applied potential.6.3 mu g(NH3)h(-1)mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved with the yttrium catalyst at low overpotentials.6.3% is the faradaic efficiency of Cu3P@NC at -0.1 V vs. RHE during nitrogen reduction reaction.6.31 mu mol h(-1) cm(-2) is the optimal NH3 yield achieved by the MoN/CFC electrode.The NH3 yield rate of 6.48 x 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is achieved by Al-doped Co3O4 in 0.1 M KOH.6.5% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved with engineered surface oxygen vacancies of TiO2 during nitrogen reduction.6.5 +/- 0.2% is the measured Faradaic efficiency in the lithium-mediated nitrogen reduction system.6.6% is the faradaic efficiency of NS-Ti3C2Tx in 0.05 M H2SO4 during nitrogen reduction reactions.6.7% is the Faradaic efficiency of beta-FeOOH in 0.5 M LiClO4.6.8 % is the faradaic efficiency of WO3 at -0.12 V.6.8 +/- 0.4 mg/h/cm is the NH3 yield rate attained by the Fe/Cu catalyst in an alkaline electrolyte of 0.1 M KOH.6.8 +/- 3.3% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Ni3S4 catalyst for NRR.6.9% is the Faradaic efficiency of the PTCA-rGO hybrid at -0.50 V vs. RHE.6.92% is the Faradaic efficiency obtained with the MXene@NiCoB composite in the nitrogen reduction reaction.The entity "60 DEGREES C" refers to a specific reaction condition utilized in various ammonia synthesis tests involving hybrid composite materials. At this temperature, the NH3 production rates are notably enhanced, reaching 7.3 x 10^(-6) mol cm^(-2) h^(-1). Additionally, 60 degrees C serves as the evaluation temperature for the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction using Fe3C/Fe2O3/Fe/C composites. Furthermore, this temperature is also employed in experiments involving Ti3C2, where it contributes to achieving improved yield and efficiency in the nitrogen reduction reaction. Overall, 60 degrees C is a critical temperature that positively influences the performance of different electrocatalysts in ammonia synthesis.60 h is the duration over which the reduction current density remains almost constant, indicating the stability of the electrocatalyst.60% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Fe2P catalyst during electrolysis at -0.4 V vs. RHE.60-h is the duration during which the durability of the NbSe2 electrocatalyst was tested without stable decay of Faradaic efficiencies and NH3 yield rates.60.3 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the ammonia yield of the MnMoO4 nanorods-reduced graphene oxide catalyst.60.5 % is the Faradaic efficiency of SC-PBDT-TT in the nitrogen reduction reaction.60.68 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield of the amorphous PdCoCu NSs during nitrogen reduction reactions.60.87 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of MoSe2/Ti3C2 composite at -0.55 V vs RHE.600.4 +/- 23.0 mu g h(-1) mg(Ag)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved using AgNDs in the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction.607.35 mg h(-1) mg(NCNT)(-1) is the NH3 production rate achieved by NCNT@MIL-101(Fe) in the nitrogen reduction reaction.61 +/- 1 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia yield rate achieved by the electrocatalyst using phosphotungstic acid.61 ¬± 1 ¬µg h(-1) mg(cat).(-1) is the ammonia yield rate achieved by the electrocatalyst using phosphotungstic acid, indicating its high efficiency.61.5 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of the Ag-Cu-NS/CP catalyst at an applied potential of -0.4 V.62.1% is the Faradaic efficiency for CN/C-600 at various applied potentials.62.9 +/- 2.7 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia yield rate achieved with the Fe single-atom catalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.62.94 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by Ti3C2Tx QDs as NRR catalysts.63 mV dec(-1) is the Tafel slope for the Fe-ReS2@N-CNF catalyst indicating its electrochemical activity.64.76 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield of the Fe-HCS electrocatalyst, indicating its high efficiency.65.2 mu g h-1 mu gcat-1 is the NH3 yield rate for Pt-Y in the nitrogen reduction reaction.66% is the Faradaic efficiency obtained in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) using bismuth and potassium cations.66.3 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of BNQDs@Nb2CTx at -0.4 V.67.6 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 production rate achieved by the Lewis acid pair catalyst under specific conditions.67.8% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by Au electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions after increasing their electron density.68.7 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by V-alpha-Fe2O3 at a specific applied potential.68.7 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield of 17 % V-alpha-Fe2O3 at -0.2 V (vs. RHE).69.2 +/- 2.5 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of the diatomic Pd-Cu catalyst, indicating its effectiveness in ammonia production.The seven-cycle test is a stability test for the three-phase Au/o-CFP electrocatalyst, showing negligible fluctuation in NH3 yield and Faradaic efficiency.7.01 % is the faradaic efficiency of Ti3C2 at 0.2 V and 20 degrees C.7.2% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by RuCu-FNs during the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction.7.3% is the Faradaic efficiency of the electrocatalyst N-NiO/CC.The entity "7.3%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved in two distinct electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions. Specifically, it represents the Faradaic efficiency of the Ni@Nb2C MXene-based catalyst, which is utilized in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, as well as the optimized Pt3Fe nanowires, which also demonstrate a Faradaic efficiency of 7.3% in the same type of reaction. This indicates that both catalysts exhibit the same level of efficiency in converting nitrogen to ammonia, highlighting their potential in optimizing nitrogen reduction processes.†␀␀␀␀␀␀z†␀␀␀␀␀␀°␀␀␀␀␀␀°␀␀␀␀␀␀ï°␀␀␀␀␀␀R¢␀␀␀␀␀␀ë¢␀␀␀␀␀␀b§␀␀␀␀␀␀·§␀␀␀␀␀␀ß␀␀␀␀␀␀\ß␀␀␀␀␀␀Î®␀␀␀␀␀␀ ™␀␀␀␀␀␀´␀␀␀␀␀␀h´␀␀␀␀␀␀h´␀␀␀␀␀␀Ë´␀␀␀␀␀␀‡≠␀␀␀␀␀␀ÄØ␀␀␀␀␀␀ÄØ␀␀␀␀␀␀ÄØ␀␀␀␀␀␀¸∞␀␀␀␀␀␀é≥␀␀␀␀␀␀µ␀␀␀␀␀␀Äµ␀␀␀␀␀␀Äµ␀␀␀␀␀␀ø∑␀␀␀␀␀␀∏␀␀␀␀␀␀‰∫␀␀␀␀␀␀@ª␀␀␀␀␀␀◊Ω␀␀␀␀␀␀◊Ω␀␀␀␀␀␀*æ␀␀␀␀␀␀*æ␀␀␀␀␀␀Ù¿␀␀␀␀␀␀D¡␀␀␀␀␀␀î√␀␀␀␀␀␀î√␀␀␀␀␀␀î√␀␀␀␀␀␀î√␀␀␀␀␀␀î√␀␀␀␀␀␀ƒ␀␀␀␀␀␀‹«␀␀␀␀␀␀‹«␀␀␀␀␀␀Z»␀␀␀␀␀␀Z»␀␀␀␀␀␀Z»␀␀␀␀␀␀íÀ␀␀␀␀␀␀íÀ␀␀␀␀␀␀íÀ␀␀␀␀␀␀Œ␀␀␀␀␀␀!œ␀␀␀␀␀␀ìœ␀␀␀␀␀␀ô–␀␀␀␀␀␀ô–␀␀␀␀␀␀~”␀␀␀␀␀␀ ‘␀␀␀␀␀␀ ‘␀␀␀␀␀␀'’␀␀␀␀␀␀'’␀␀␀␀␀␀Å÷␀␀␀␀␀␀Jÿ␀␀␀␀␀␀Jÿ␀␀␀␀␀␀ûÿ␀␀␀␀␀␀Ÿ␀␀␀␀␀␀‹␀␀␀␀␀␀Îﬁ␀␀␀␀␀␀Gﬂ␀␀␀␀␀␀Àﬂ␀␀␀␀␀␀v·␀␀␀␀␀␀Œ‚␀␀␀␀␀␀5„␀␀␀␀␀␀—„␀␀␀␀␀␀wÂ␀␀␀␀␀␀ÊÂ␀␀␀␀␀␀È␀␀␀␀␀␀È␀␀␀␀␀␀…Í␀␀␀␀␀␀˙Î␀␀␀␀␀␀îÌ␀␀␀␀␀␀Ô␀␀␀␀␀␀Ô␀␀␀␀␀␀Ô␀␀␀␀␀␀Ô␀␀␀␀␀␀óÔ␀␀␀␀␀␀óÔ␀␀␀␀␀␀
