tions (NRR). Specifically, it indicates that Mo2C/C demonstrates a Faradaic efficiency of 7.8%, showcasing its capability in effectively converting nitrogen into ammonia. Similarly, the NiO/G electrocatalyst also exhibits a Faradaic efficiency of 7.8% during NRR. This consistency in Faradaic efficiency across both catalysts highlights their comparable performance in the electrochemical conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, although they are different materials.7.9% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved in the gas-phase cell for ammonia synthesis.7.94 +/- 0.2% is the Faradaic efficiency of bismuthene nanostructures in the nitrogen reduction reaction.70 +/- 24% is the Faradaic efficiency observed during the nitrogen reduction reaction at the specified applied potential.70.9 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia yield achieved by the chlorine-doped reduced graphene oxide at an applied potential of -0.3 V vs RHE.71.4 mu g h-1 mu gcat-1 is the NH3 yield rate for Pt-La in the nitrogen reduction reaction.714 mA h g(-1) is the energy density achieved by the Zn-N-2 battery using exfoliated NbS2 as the cathode.The electrocatalyst Fe3O4@MoS2 exhibits an NH3 yield rate of 73.24 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) in the nitrogen reduction reaction. This performance metric highlights the effectiveness of Fe3O4@MoS2 in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, making it a noteworthy candidate for further research and optimization in electrochemical applications.The entity "73.74 %" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved in a study focused on electrochemical synthesis processes. This efficiency indicates the effectiveness of the reaction in converting electrical energy into chemical energy, specifically in the context of nitrogen reduction reactions. The reported Faradaic efficiency of 73.74 % was attained using a novel covalent organic polymer in conjunction with a transition metal-based catalyst, highlighting the potential of this combination to enhance the performance of electrochemical synthesis.75.2% is the retention rate of the NH3 yield after 100 cycles with the Fe-TPPHZ catalyst.76.53% is the Faradaic efficiency of the electrocatalyst V-N-2.76.97% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Cu/Cu2O@C catalyst for nitrate reduction.78.4 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by Mo2N at -0.3 V.79% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the vanadium tetroxide nanowire electrocatalyst at -0.2 V.The NH3 yield of the new catalyst is reported to be 8 times better than that of Pd/C, which yields 4.8 Œºg mg(cat)^(-1)h^(-1).8% is the significant current efficiency for ammonia formation achieved in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction system.8.02% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by MnO particles during the electrocatalytic hydrogenation of N-2 to NH3.8.08 x 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by MoS2 in nitrogen reduction reactions.8.09 g h^(-1) mg(cat)^(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by the PCN catalyst.8.1 % is the Faradaic efficiency of BNC-NSs at -400 mV vs. RHE in 0.05 M H2SO4 electrolyte.The Fe2Mo3O8/XC-72 electrocatalyst exhibits a Faradaic efficiency of 8.2% during the nitrogen reduction reaction at a potential of -0.3 V. This efficiency indicates the effectiveness of the electrocatalyst in converting nitrogen to ammonia under the specified electrochemical conditions.8.22% is the Faradaic efficiency of the electrocatalyst Fe3O4@MoS2.8.22% is the Faradaic efficiency for Fe3O4@MoS2 in the nitrogen reduction reaction.8.24 x 10-9 mol. s-1.cm-2 is the NH3 yield rate of the Fe@MXene electrocatalyst, indicating its efficiency in producing ammonia.8.28 % is the faradaic efficiency achieved after the first 1 h chronoamperometry test with the modified hematite electrocatalyst.8.29% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Fe/Fe3O4 catalyst for NH3 production, indicating its effectiveness.8.4% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Fe@MXene electrocatalyst during nitrogen reduction reactions.8.40 x 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield rate obtained with the VN nanosheet array at -0.50 V.8.42% is the Faradaic efficiency of the TiO2/Ti3C2Tx hybrid catalyst at -0.60 V vs RHE.8.5 % is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the Co3O4@NCs electrocatalyst at -0.2 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode, indicating its effectiveness in producing NH3.The entity "8.5%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency of the VS4-140 electrocatalyst, which is measured at a potential of -0.59 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). This efficiency indicates the effectiveness of the VS4-140 in facilitating the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, specifically in the context of ammonia (NH3) production. The consistent mention of 8.5% in both descriptions underscores the reliability of this performance metric for the VS4-140 electrocatalyst under the specified conditions.The Fe3C@C electrocatalyst demonstrates an NH3 yield rate of 8.53 Œºg/(h¬∑mg(cat)) during the nitrogen reduction reaction. This value reflects the ammonia production rate of the Fe3C@C composite, indicating its effectiveness in facilitating the electrochemical conversion of nitrogen to ammonia.8.56 mu g h(-1) mg cat(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved at -0.2 V versus RHE in the nitrogen reduction process.8.57% is the faradaic efficiency achieved with the FeP-rGO hybrid.8.63 mu g NH3 mg(cat)(-1) h(-1) is the yield rate of ammonia produced by the Fe-MoS2 catalyst.8.7 mg(NH3) mg(Fe)(-1) h(-1) is the NH3 yield of the [Fe-S-2-Mo] electrocatalyst.8.8 +/- 1.3 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the rate of NH3 formation on the best-performing sample at -0.1 V.8.9 % is the faradaic efficiency of Ru88Pt12 electrocatalyst at -0.2 V.The entity "8.9 +/- 1.7 %" represents the Faradaic efficiency achieved in the membrane electrode assembly (MEA) configuration for ammonia production. This efficiency is specifically noted during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, which occurs at a cell voltage of 3.6 V. The reported value indicates a moderate level of efficiency in the conversion process, highlighting the performance of the electrochemical system under the specified conditions.An overpotential of 80 mV is noted as a condition under which an NH3 Faradaic efficiency as high as 9.8% is obtained.80% is the nitrite selectivity achieved in the binary state of the cathode assembly during the nitrate-reduction reaction (NRR).80.1% is the record-high Faradaic efficiency achieved under ambient conditions with the N2 microextractor (NME) system.80.4 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the ammonia production yield achieved with the Fe-ReS2@N-CNF catalyst.800 K is one of the temperatures indicating the thermodynamic stability of the studied materials for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).The Zn-nitrate battery system demonstrates a faradaic efficiency of 81.3% for ammonia production during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NORR). This efficiency indicates the effectiveness of the system in converting electrical energy into chemical energy, specifically in the synthesis of NH3.814 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved at -0.6 V vs RHE with the Fe-Mn-Ga oxide electrocatalyst.82.7 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate obtained with the Fe-SnO2 electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.83 +/- 1% is the Faradaic efficiency for the Mo-PTA@CNT electrocatalyst at -0.1 V versus RHE.836 mV vs. RHE is the half-wave potential for the oxygen reduction reaction (ORR) activity of BNFC, indicating its performance in electrochemical reactions.85 % is the Faradaic efficiency of the Cu-nanosphere electrode when reducing NO3- to yield NH3 at -1.3 V.85 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of NiPc nanorods.85% +/- 2% is the Faradaic efficiency achieved by the electrocatalyst using phosphotungstic acid.85% ¬± 2% is the faradaic efficiency of the electrocatalyst using phosphotungstic acid at -0.1 V vs. RHE.85.43% is the best Faradaic efficiency achieved at an applied potential of -0.4 VRHE using LiNbO3 cubes.85.45 mg h^{-1} m^{-2} is the highest yield rate achieved with a configured flow cell in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).86.91 +/- 9.29 mu g h-1 cm-2 is the NH3 production rate observed in electrochemical tests on NiS catalysts.88.7 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield of the Fe-B/NC catalyst at 0.45 V vs RHE.89.5 +/- 6.0 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of NbSe2 at -0.45 V vs RHE.The enhancement of the intrinsic N2RR activity by up to 9-fold indicates a significant improvement in the performance of the Fe/Fe3O4 catalyst.9.02% is the Faradaic efficiency of -FeO(OH,F) nanorod at the specified applied potential.9.03 % is the faradaic efficiency of Ti3C2 at 0.2 V and 60 degrees C.9.04 % is the initial Faradaic efficiency value before enhancement in the study involving the synthetic plasma-enhanced bimetallic catalyst.The entity "9.1%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved by various electrocatalysts during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). Specifically, Fe-2(MoO4)(3) nanoparticles, O-KFCNTs in a 0.1 M HCl solution at -0.80 V vs RHE, and the TiN-PE electrocatalyst at -0.6 V vs RHE all demonstrate a Faradaic efficiency of 9.1%. This indicates that these materials exhibit similar performance levels in terms of their ability to convert nitrogen into ammonia under the specified conditions, highlighting their potential as effective electrocatalysts in NRR applications.9.15% is the Faradaic efficiency of the bioinspired Fe3C@C composite for nitrogen reduction.9.16 x 10(-11) mol s(-1).cm(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved with the TiO2/Ti electrocatalyst.9.16% is the Faradaic efficiency of Ti2O3 in the nitrogen reduction reaction.9.17 % is the optimal Faradaic efficiency achieved at -0.1 V using TiO2-delta N delta.9.26% is the faradaic efficiency of the Nb2O5 nanofiber electrocatalyst at -0.55 V vs. RHE during nitrogen reduction reactions.9.28% is the faradaic efficiency of the Fe-N/C-CNT catalyst at -0.2 V vs RHE, reflecting its effectiveness in the NRR process.9.3 g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 formation rate of the defect-rich fluorographene nanosheet electrocatalyst.The entity "9.3%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved in two distinct electrochemical systems. Firstly, Ti3C2Tx MXene nanosheets exhibit a Faradaic efficiency of 9.3% when operated at a potential of -0.4 V vs RHE. Secondly, a composite of MoSe2 and Ti3C2 demonstrates the same Faradaic efficiency of 9.3%, but at a slightly higher potential of -0.25 V vs RHE. This indicates that both materials, despite differing in composition and operating conditions, achieve the same level of efficiency in their respective nitrogen reduction reactions.9.37% is the faradaic efficiency of V-NiS2 at an applied potential of -0.35 V.9.42 mu g h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by the pAu/NF electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.9.49% is the faradaic efficiency of Pd nanoparticles modified with tannic acid during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).9.5% is the Faradaic efficiency of the Mo2C@3DUM-C electrocatalyst for NH3 production at -0.20 V.9.63 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the ammonia formation rate achieved by electrospun zirconia nanofibers during nitrogen reduction reactions.9.66 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by the Fe ion grafted on MoO3 nanorods during the nitrogen reduction reaction.9.73% is the faradaic efficiency of the FePc/O-MWCNT catalyst after 2 hours of electrocatalytic reaction.The Faradaic efficiency of CuAg/Ti(3)C(2)MXene is 9.77%, showing its performance in the NRR process.9.8% is the faradaic efficiency of the NiFe2O4/RGO catalyst at -0.5 V vs. RHE.The entity "9.83%" refers to the Faradaic efficiency achieved by multiple electrocatalysts during nitrogen reduction reactions. Specifically, it represents the Faradaic efficiency of the FeNi@CNS catalyst, the nanoporous CuMn (np-CuMn) alloy electrocatalyst at -0.3 V vs RHE, and the NiO@TiO2 electrocatalyst, all under ambient conditions. This consistent value of 9.83% indicates a notable performance level for these catalysts in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction, particularly at the specified potential of -0.3 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE).9.9% is the energy efficiency achieved in the liquid-phase electrochemical cell.)<|COMPLETE|>The entity "90%" refers to the remarkable performance metrics achieved during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) using a coated metal-organic framework (MOF) layer and platinum-reduced electrocatalysts (PtRENCs). Specifically, a selectivity of 90% was attained, demonstrating the catalyst's efficiency in converting nitrogen to ammonia. Additionally, this performance metric reflects a retention rate of 90% for ammonia yield after 12 hours of continuous NRR tests with PtRENCs, indicating sustained catalytic activity. Furthermore, the stable NRR activity of over 90% across multiple repetitive cycles underscores the effectiveness and reliability of the catalyst in facilitating nitrogen reduction.92.1% is the faradaic efficiency of the Pd-doped TiO2 nanoarray electrode for NH3 production.92.23% is the maximum Faradaic efficiency for NH3 production achieved by the Fe-Cu DAC at 20 mA cm(-2).93% is the Faradaic efficiency for nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) at an applied potential of 0 V.95.12% is the faradaic efficiency for the NO3RR using CoPc nanotubes.95.8% is the faradaic efficiency of Zn/NC NSs at -0.70 V vs RHE.97% is the record-high Faradaic efficiency achieved in the nitrogen reduction reaction under the adjusted three-phase interface conditions.97.18 +/- 7.13 mu g hour(-1) cm(-2) is the maximum production rate of ammonia from the electrochemical reduction of N2.99% is the selectivity achieved by the Ni-0.3(FeCoCuPd)(0.175) electrocatalyst relative to the secondary hydrogen evolution reaction.99.6% is the nitrate conversion efficiency achieved by the Pd-doped TiO2 nanoarray electrode.99.88% is the Faradaic efficiency for NH3 production using the Fe-Cu DAC at -0.6 V.The reaction condition of <65 degrees C indicates the low temperature at which the electrochemical synthesis of NH3 was demonstrated using gamma-Fe2O3.The acidic electrolyte is used in the electrocatalytic reaction involving the FePc/O-MWCNT catalyst.Acidic electrolytes are used to provide protons (H+) in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), but can lead to low selectivity due to H2 formation.Active sites refer to the catalytic sites that are exposed and available for reaction in electrocatalysts.Silver (Ag) is a chemical element recognized for its high-performance capabilities as an electrocatalyst in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is often composited with titanium dioxide (TiO2) to enhance the performance of these reactions. Ag is included among various materials studied for NRR, demonstrating its versatility in different catalytic forms. Notably, it is utilized in the form of nanodots (AgNDs) as a catalyst in aqueous solutions, and it plays a crucial role in the synthesis of single-atom sites characterized by Ag-N-4 coordination, which significantly improves NRR performance.

Additionally, Ag is incorporated into trimetal palladium-based nanosheets, contributing to the overall efficacy of nitrogen reduction. It is also involved in the creation of a porous bromide-derived film (BD-Ag/AF), which serves as an efficient electrocatalyst for the reduction of nitrogen gas (N2). Furthermore, Ag is part of a transition-metal-embedded graphdiyne monolayer (TM@GDY) and has been screened for anchoring on this monolayer to facilitate electrocatalytic N2 reduction. Overall, Ag is a key transition metal studied for its potential in ammonia synthesis through nitrogen reduction, showcasing its multifaceted role in advancing electrochemical catalysis.Ag nanodots (AgNDs) are utilized as the catalyst in a non-loading electrocatalysis system specifically designed for nitrogen reduction reactions. Their role as a catalyst is crucial in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, highlighting their potential in electrochemical applications aimed at sustainable ammonia production.Ag nanoparticles-reduced graphene oxide hybrid (Ag NPs-rGO) is a novel Ag-based catalyst designed for high-efficiency electrocatalytic performance in N2-to-NH3 conversion.Ag-doped Cu nanosheets grown on carbon paper (Ag-Cu-NS/CP) are synthesized as efficient electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions.Ag-N-4 coordination refers to the specific arrangement of silver atoms that enhances the performance of the electrocatalyst in NRR.Ag/AgCl is a reference electrode used in electrochemical measurements, particularly in the context of applied potentials.)<|COMPLETE|>Ag@AgP2@Ni-CoP is a core-shell nanowire catalyst synthesized for effective nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Al, or aluminum, is recognized as one of the main group metal elements that play a significant role in the electrochemical production of ammonia. Its properties and characteristics make it a subject of interest in the field of electrochemistry, particularly in the context of optimizing nitrogen reduction reactions for enhanced ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency.Alkaline aqueous solution is the type of electrolyte used in the electrochemical synthesis of NH3 on Fe-CuS/C catalysts.Alkaline electrolyte is the type of electrolyte used for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) with the D-FeN/C electrocatalyst.Alkaline medium is the reaction condition under which the optimized Ru0.15Cu0.85 NS catalyst exhibits superior stability.Alkaline solution is a reaction condition that affects the kinetics of the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and the overall performance of the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.alpha-CuPc is an electrocatalyst that demonstrated NRR activity in 0.1 N HCl electrolyte solution.Alpha-Fe nanorods are used as NRR cathodes, enhancing the nitrogen reduction reaction activity.Alpha-Fe2O3 is a chemical compound that serves as a foundational material in the synthesis of the alpha-Fe2O3@mTiO2 nanocomposite, which is utilized for nitrogen reduction applications. Additionally, alpha-Fe2O3 is the precursor for V-alpha-Fe2O3, a modified variant designed to enhance the activity of the nitrogen reduction reaction. This modification aims to optimize the performance of the catalyst in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, thereby improving overall efficiency in electrochemical processes.alpha-Fe2O3@mTiO2-400 is a composite catalyst that exhibits a large NH3 yield and high Faradaic efficiency in the nitrogen reduction reaction.The ambient atmosphere refers to the environmental conditions under which the nitrogen reduction reaction is conducted.Ambient condition refers to the standard environmental conditions under which the BHGO catalyst operates during the nitrogen reduction reaction.Ambient conditions encompass the environmental parameters under which electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) are conducted, specifically for the fixation of nitrogen into ammonia. These conditions typically involve room temperature and atmospheric pressure, making the process more sustainable and efficient compared to traditional methods like the Haber-Bosch process. The significance of ambient conditions lies in their ability to facilitate the electrochemical conversion of N2 to NH3, allowing for effective electrocatalyst performance. Various studies highlight that conducting NRR under these mild conditions not only enhances the yield of ammonia but also promotes the use of alternative catalysts, such as Pd/gamma-MnO2 and Mn3O4, thereby contributing to a more environmentally friendly approach to ammonia synthesis. Overall, ambient conditions are crucial for optimizing the electrochemical nitrogen reduction process, making it an attractive and sustainable option for ammonia production.Ambient electrocatalytic nitrogen fixation is a method for synthesizing ammonia under ambient conditions, which is considered a green technology.Ambient electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) is a method for synthesizing ammonia under mild conditions, contrasting with traditional methods.Ambient reaction conditions refer to the mild conditions under which the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction can be conducted.Ammonia synthesis refers to the process of producing ammonia through the nitrogen reduction reaction, which is the focus of the study.The ammonia yield of 41.69 mu g h(-1)mg(cat.)(-1) indicates the performance of the CB[7]-K-2[B12H12]@Au electrocatalyst in ammonia production.1.43 mg(NH3) cm(-2) h(-1) is the ammonia yield rate achieved during the tandem plasma-electrocatalysis strategy at -0.33 V vs. RHE.3.0 mg(NH3) cm(-2) h(-1) is the ammonia yield rate achieved during the tandem plasma-electrocatalysis strategy at -0.63 V vs. RHE.The ammonia yield rate of 5.302 mu g h-1 cm-2 indicates the performance of the TiO2@N-C electrocatalyst in ammonia production.Amorphism refers to the lack of a defined crystalline structure in electrocatalysts, which can affect their catalytic properties.Amorphous PdCoCu NSs demonstrate excellent nitrogen reduction reaction activity at low overpotentials with an NH3 yield of 60.68 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) and a Faradaic efficiency of 42.93%.Amorphous trimetal Pd-based nanosheets (PdCoM with M = Cu, Ag, Fe, Mo) are synthesized for efficient nitrogen fixation and exhibit high electrocatalytic activity.The applied potential is a variable that influences the kinetics of both NRR and HER in electrocatalytic processes.Artificial ammonia synthesis refers to the production of ammonia through methods like electrochemical nitrogen reduction, as opposed to traditional methods.The associative distal mechanism is a proposed pathway for the nitrogen reduction reaction as confirmed by theoretical calculations.)<|COMPLETE|>Atmospheric pressure refers to the specific pressure condition under which various catalytic processes for ammonia synthesis occur. In the context of the Fe2O3/TiO2 composite catalyst, atmospheric pressure is the operational environment that facilitates the synthesis of ammonia. Similarly, for the electrochemical synthesis of NH3 utilizing gamma-Fe2O3, atmospheric pressure is also the pressure condition under which the reaction is conducted. This indicates that both catalytic systems rely on atmospheric pressure to optimize their performance in ammonia production.Atomic dispersion refers to the distribution of Fe sites in the Fe-B/NC catalyst, which is critical for its performance in nitrogen reduction.Attenuated total reflection surface-enhanced infrared absorption spectrum (ATR-SEIRAS) is a technique used to verify the reaction path in nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Gold (Au) is a chemical element that plays a significant role in the field of electrochemistry, particularly in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) aimed at producing ammonia (NH3). As a noble metal catalyst, Au is utilized as an electrode in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions, contributing to the efficient synthesis of NH3. Its application extends to the modification of nanowire surfaces, which enhances electrocatalytic performance for NRR. 

In the context of NRR, Au is employed in innovative configurations such as the three-phase Au/o-CFP electrocatalyst, which has been evaluated for its effectiveness in electrochemical nitrogen reduction. Additionally, Au is one of the transition metals studied for its potential to improve NRR performance, particularly through methods like the self-assembled monolayer technique. The metallic form of Au is produced via the self-reduction of Au3+ ions during the preparation of Au@Ti3C2 samples, showcasing its versatility and importance in advancing electrocatalytic processes for ammonia synthesis.Au nanowire (Au NW) is an electrocatalyst modified with tannic acid to enhance its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Au-25-Cys-M is a novel electrocatalyst fabricated for nitrogen reduction reactions under ambient conditions.Au-Ag-Pd-850 is a trimetallic electrocatalyst that achieves the highest electrocatalytic NRR activity under ambient conditions.Au-Fe3O4 nanoparticles are heterogeneous catalysts that exhibit high efficiency for nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) due to their synergistic effect.Au/C is a noble metal catalyst evaluated for electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (ENRR) to produce ammonia.Au/Fe-2(MoO4)(3) is a modified electrocatalyst that significantly enhances the activity and selectivity for the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (eNRR). This catalyst demonstrates exceptional performance, achieving a nitrogen-to-ammonia conversion rate of 7.61 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) and a Faradaic efficiency of 18.79% when operated at -0.4 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode in a 0.2 m Na2SO4 electrolyte under ambient conditions. These characteristics position Au/Fe-2(MoO4)(3) as a promising candidate for efficient ammonia production through electrochemical methods.Au3+ ions are used in the preparation of Au@Ti3C2 samples by electrostatic adsorption on the surface of Ti3C2 nanosheets.Au3Rh is a bimetallic electrocatalyst synthesized as a mesoporous film on Ni foam, exhibiting high Faraday efficiency and NH3 yield for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).B is a chemical element studied for co-doping with Ti to enhance nitrogen reduction reaction activity.B, S-mRh/NF is a boron and sulfur co-doped mesoporous Rh film on Ni foam developed for electrochemical ammonia synthesis.B-0.9(FCNZY)(0.2) exhibits significantly higher NH3 yield and Faradaic efficiency compared to B(FCNZY)(0.2).B-Ti-2 @C2N is a co-doped structure that shows high electrocatalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.B-x(FCNZY)(0.2) is a high-entropy perovskite oxide reported as an electrocatalyst for eNRR with enhanced activity due to nonstoichiometric metal elements.B/N-CNF is a metal-free co-doped porous carbon nanofiber that exhibits superior electrocatalytic performance for the NRR.Bamboo-like carbon nanotubes (BLCNTs) are used to bridge Co nanocrystals in the yolk-shell microcage design for nitrogen reduction reactions.BD-Ag/AF is a porous bromide-derived Ag film that acts as an efficient electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).The Berthelot method is a commonly employed technique for measuring ammonia concentration in various electrolytes.)<|COMPLETE|>beta-FeOOH nanorods are reported as an efficient and durable NRR electrocatalyst.BHGO is a broken holey graphene oxide catalyst that has been synthesized using a facile high-temperature etching method. This innovative preparation technique endows BHGO with enhanced electron transfer capabilities and a greater abundance of active sites, making it particularly effective for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). The unique structural characteristics of BHGO contribute to its superior performance in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, highlighting its potential as a promising electrocatalyst in electrochemical applications.Bismuth (Bi) is a chemical element that plays a significant role in the development of Bi-based catalysts for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). It is utilized in various forms, including as a dopant in cerium oxide (CeO2) nanocubes, where it enhances the NRR activity. Additionally, Bi is incorporated into bismuth-based metal-organic frameworks, which are also employed for NRR applications. As one of the main group metal elements, Bi is crucial in the electrochemical production of ammonia, contributing to advancements in catalyst design and performance optimization for this important reaction.Bi NPs@CRs are Bi nanoparticles in situ confined in carbon rods, used as an electrocatalyst for ammonia synthesis.Bi NS is a two-dimensional mosaic bismuth nanosheet fabricated via an in situ electrochemical reduction process, exhibiting favorable NH3 yield and Faradaic efficiency.Bi(0) nanoparticles are the reduced form of bismuth that exhibit excellent NRR performance.BI-MOF is recognized as a high-efficiency electrocatalyst for the artificial synthesis of ammonia (NH3), demonstrating favorable selectivity in this process. Additionally, BI-MOF serves as a metal-organic framework precursor, which is instrumental in the fabrication of bismuth (Bi) nanoparticles specifically for ammonia synthesis. This dual role highlights BI-MOF's significance in enhancing the efficiency and selectivity of electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions, making it a valuable material in the field of catalysis and electrochemistry.Bimetallic NiCo boride nanoparticles are electrocatalysts that demonstrate high-performance nitrogen reduction electrolysis.Bimodal nanoporous structure refers to the structural design of PdCu alloys that enhances their performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Binding energy is a critical factor in determining the interaction strength between the catalyst and nitrogen in the NRR process.Bismuth is a chemical element recognized for its superior intrinsic activity in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), outperforming many transition metals. It is a key component of the PdBi2 compound and plays a significant role in the electrochemical decomposition process. As a semiconducting element, bismuth serves as a promising catalyst for ambient electrocatalytic N2 reduction reactions, enhancing the efficiency of ammonia (NH3) production. The structural characteristics of bismuth species, which are anchored on the 4-fold hollow site of the electrocatalyst, facilitate the activation and hydrogenation of nitrogen, further contributing to its effectiveness in NRR applications.Bismuth-based materials are electrocatalysts that exhibit unique nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) properties due to their electronic structures and poor hydrogen evolution activity.Bismuthene nanostructures are produced for use in the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Boron and nitrogen dual-doped carbon nanospheres (BNC-NSs) show exceptional electrocatalytic activity toward the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).BNFC is a ternary-doped carbon material synthesized from cigarette butts, demonstrating enhanced electrocatalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). This electrocatalyst achieves an NH3 yield rate of 41 ¬µgNH3 h-1 mgcat.-1 and a faradaic efficiency of 14% when operated at -0.4 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) in a 0.05 M H2SO4 electrolyte. The unique composition and structure of BNFC contribute to its effective performance in catalyzing the NRR, making it a promising candidate for sustainable ammonia production.BNQDs are a type of electrocatalyst that, in combination with Nb2CTx, enhance nitrogen activation and reduction reactions by creating unique interfacial B sites for catalytic activity.BNQDs@Nb2CTx is an efficient NRR catalyst that presents optimum activity with an NH3 yield rate of 66.3 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) and a Faradaic efficiency of 16.7%.Boron carbide nanosheet acts as a metal-free electrocatalyst for high-performance electrochemical nitrogen-to-ammonia fixation at ambient conditions.Boron-doped carbon nanosphere is a metal-free electrocatalyst that effectively activates the N=N triple-bonds and suppresses the hydrogen evolution reaction during the eNRR process.Boron-doped TiO2 acts as an efficient non-noble-metal electrocatalyst for electrochemical nitrogen reduction to ammonia at ambient conditions.The Bosch process is a method for synthesizing ammonia, but it faces challenges such as low NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency due to the lack of effective catalysts.C is a chemical element that forms a Lewis acid site when bonded with F, influencing the electrochemical reactions.C-doped TiO2 nanoparticles are efficient electrocatalysts for the nitrogen reduction reaction, achieving notable NH3 yield.C18@CoP/TM is an electrocatalyst that enhances the activity of CoP nanoarray for N2-to-NH3 conversion.C3N is the support material for the single-atom catalysts investigated in the study.C4N4 is a structural framework that can be doped with transition metals to enhance nitrogen reduction reaction activity.C6N6 is a triazine-based porous sheet that can act as a next-generation photocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.C9N4 is a substrate used to anchor transition metal single atom centers for catalyzing nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).C@NiO@Ni microtubes are identified as a high-efficiency electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) in 0.1 M KOH electrolyte.CaCO3 is a chemical compound used as a template for the synthesis of porous carbon materials.Carbon cloth is the substrate used to support the VN nanowire array for nitrogen reduction reactions.Carbon fiber paper serves as a substrate for the WO3 nanobelt catalyst, facilitating the electrochemical reactions.Carbon nanotubes (CNTs) play a significant role in enhancing the efficiency of nitrogen electroreduction processes. They are utilized in conjunction with covalent organic frameworks (COFs) to improve overall performance in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). Additionally, CNTs serve as a support structure for iron oxide (Fe2O3) nanoparticles, which further enhances the catalytic activity of these nanoparticles in the NRR. This dual functionality of CNTs not only contributes to the structural integrity of the catalyst system but also optimizes the electrochemical performance, leading to improved ammonia (NH3) yield and faradaic efficiency.Carbon quantum dots are a metal-free catalyst that effectively activates N2 to reduce it to ammonia under ambient conditions, showing excellent catalytic performance in 0.1 M HCl.Carbon-encapsulated mixed-valence Fe-7(PO4)(6) is an electrocatalyst for high-efficiency NRR, showing superior performance compared to other Fe-based catalysts.Catalyst-support interactions describe the relationship between the catalyst and its support material, which can significantly influence catalytic performance.Catalytic material refers to substances used to enhance the rate of the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) in electrochemical processes.The catalytic performance pertains to the effectiveness of a catalyst in facilitating the nitrogen reduction reaction, which is quantitatively assessed through metrics such as yield and efficiency. In particular, the catalytic performance of carbon quantum dots can be significantly improved by manipulating the oxygen content of the samples at various temperatures. This optimization of oxygen content is crucial for enhancing the overall performance of the catalyst in the nitrogen reduction process.CB[7]-K-2[B12H12]@Au is an advanced electrocatalyst designed for electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions. It features highly dispersed and ultrafine gold (Au) nanoparticles that are anchored to a stable substrate, which significantly enhances its performance in nitrogen reduction. The incorporation of potassium ions (K(+)) further optimizes the catalytic activity, contributing to improved efficiency in the production of ammonia (NH3). This unique combination of structural characteristics and ionic enhancement positions CB[7]-K-2[B12H12]@Au as a promising candidate for advancing nitrogen reduction technologies.Cd is a chemical element that is part of the studied materials for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).CEO2 refers to bi-doped CeO2 nanocubes, which serve as effective electrocatalysts exhibiting high activity for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This chemical compound plays a crucial role in stabilizing Fe2+ cations within bimetallic nanotubes, enhancing their performance in NRR. Additionally, CeO2 nanoparticles are integrated onto a three-dimensional carbon skeleton, which is proposed as a promising and stable electrocatalyst for the conversion of nitrogen (N2) to ammonia (NH3). Collectively, these characteristics highlight the potential of CeO2-based materials in optimizing electrochemical processes for ammonia synthesis.CeO2-rGO is a non-noble-metal electrocatalyst that exhibits excellent selectivity in the nitrogen reduction reaction.Cetyltrimethylammonium bromide (CTAB) is a surfactant used to modify the surface of Ti3C2Tix MXene to increase its hydrophobicity.Chemical vapor deposition is the synthesis method used to create the molybdenum carbide (Mo2C) catalyst for nitrogen reduction.Chromium nitride is an active and selective electrocatalyst for the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (ENRR) demonstrated in a Nafion-based membrane electrode assembly.Chronoamperometry is a technique used to investigate the durability of the electrochemical system in nitrogen reduction reactions.Chronoamperometry (CA) is a method used to evaluate the electrochemical performance of materials in nitrogen reduction reactions.Cigarette butts are the source material used to synthesize the ternary-doped carbon (BNFC) through carbonization.Citrate is a complexing agent used to mitigate the effect of interfering metal ions during ammonia quantification.Cl-doped ultrathin GDY is a metal-free electrocatalyst that efficiently drives the nitrogen reduction reaction.CN/C is a highly selective electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions, specifically N-defective carbon nitride grown on carbon paper.CN/C-600 is an electrocatalyst synthesized at 600 degrees Celsius, achieving a high NH3 production rate.CNT, or carbon nanotubes, play a multifaceted role in enhancing ammonia production through electrocatalysis. They serve as a catalytic center in the confined electrocatalysis strategy, which is crucial for improving ammonia yield. Additionally, CNTs are utilized for immobilization in the Mo-PTA@CNT electrocatalyst, facilitating the effective integration of active materials. They also act as a substrate for anchoring Rh nanoparticles, which significantly enhances their catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). Furthermore, CNTs provide an excellent substrate for the growth of Co3O4, which not only improves electronic conduction properties but also boosts nitrogen (N2) adsorption, thereby contributing to the overall efficiency of the ammonia production process.Cobalt (Co) is a versatile chemical element and transition metal that plays a significant role in various catalytic processes, particularly in the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). It is a key component of the bimetallic catalyst Co-Ni-S/Ni foam, which is instrumental in regulating the adsorption of intermediates during the N2 activation process. Co is also utilized in the construction of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) that enhance electrocatalytic performance for nitrogen reduction.

In addition to its use in MOFs, Co is involved in the synthesis of g-C3N4 monolayers, which are important for facilitating nitrogen reduction reactions. Furthermore, Co serves as a foundational element in the development of single-atom catalysts, particularly when compared to Fe-1-N-C, and is integral to the construction of single cluster catalysts (SCCs) specifically designed for NRR.

Co's potential as an electrocatalyst has been evaluated, highlighting its high activity in nitrogen reduction reactions. It is one of the 3d metals considered as a candidate electrode material for these reactions. Additionally, Co is a constituent element in high-entropy alloys (HEAs) that are being studied for their efficacy in nitrogen reduction. Lastly, Co has been screened for its ability to anchor on monolayer graphdiyne, further emphasizing its relevance in the field of electrocatalysis for N2 reduction.Co doping is a method used to enhance the performance of electrocatalysts in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Co nanocrystals encapsulated in N-doped carbon framework are part of a yolk-shell microcage design for enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction performance.Co-2@PC6 is a bimetallic catalyst that has been screened as the best candidate for nitrogen reduction reaction with high Faradaic efficiency.Co-N-x-C is a hybrid electrocatalyst derived from a metal-organic framework, featuring built-in Co-N-x active sites for nitrogen reduction.The Co-Ni heteronuclear complex exhibits exceptional nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) activity, attributed to the synergistic effect of its double atomic catalytic sites. This unique structural characteristic enhances its catalytic performance, making it one of the most effective catalysts for NRR. The combination of cobalt and nickel in this complex not only maximizes the catalytic efficiency but also contributes to the highest observed NRR activity, underscoring the importance of heteronuclear interactions in electrochemical catalysis.Co-Ni-S/Ni foam is a bimetallic electrocatalyst that effectively reduces N2.Co3 @Gra is an electrocatalyst that shows high catalytic activity with a limiting potential of -0.64 V.Co3HHTP2 is a conductive metal-organic framework that acts as an efficient catalyst for ambient electrochemical N2-to-NH3 fixation.Co3O4 is a cost-efficient electrocatalytic material that plays a significant role in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) for ammonia (NH3) synthesis. It is often coated on carbon nanotubes (CNT) to enhance its catalytic performance. Additionally, Co3O4 is doped with aluminum (Al) to further improve its efficacy in NRR applications. This electrocatalyst is fabricated as ultrafine nanoparticles using the laser ablation in liquid (LAL) technique, which contributes to its efficiency in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia. Overall, Co3O4 demonstrates promising characteristics as an electrocatalyst for sustainable ammonia production.Co4N/Co-2 C@rGO is a high-efficiency electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) that operates under ambient conditions for ammonia synthesis.Co@N-doped carbon (NC) is a catalyst obtained via pyrolysis of a metal-organic framework precursor (ZIF-67) that shows high Faraday efficiency for NRR.Cobalt is a chemical element used as a single atom catalyst in the Lewis acid pair catalyst designed for nitrogen reduction reactions.Cobalt phosphonates are the precursors used to derive the hollow cobalt phosphate microspheres (CoPi/HSNPC).Cobalt pyrophosphate micro-flowers are synthesized for use as an electrocatalyst in nitrogen reduction reactions.CoCo is a metal dimer that can selectively adsorb and activate N-2 for efficient conversion in nitrogen reduction reactions.COF-Fe/MXene is an electrocatalyst that enhances nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) performance by repelling water molecules to inhibit the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER).CoFe2O4 nanoclusters are used as a catalyst for enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) performance.CoFeB is an electrocatalyst that is part of a hierarchical 3D nanostructure for NRR.The competitive hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is a critical reaction condition that competes with nitrogen reduction, significantly impacting the overall efficiency of the process. It poses a substantial challenge in achieving high ammonia yield and Faradaic efficiency during nitrogen reduction reactions. Understanding and mitigating the effects of HER is essential for optimizing electrochemical systems aimed at enhancing ammonia production.Computational predictions are theoretical analyses that forecast the behavior and selectivity of catalysts in the nitrogen reduction reaction.CoP3 is an electrocatalyst used in the ambient electrocatalytic N2-to-NH3 conversion process.Cobalt(II) phthalocyanine (CoPc) is a stable beta-cobalt phthalocyanine that serves as an effective electrocatalyst for the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) and the nitrate reduction reaction (NO3RR). Notably, CoPc nanotubes have been highlighted for their competence in facilitating these reactions, showcasing their potential in enhancing nitrogen fixation processes and nitrate reduction in electrochemical applications.CoPc-C3N4 is a 1D/2D heterostructure electrocatalyst designed for electrochemical nitrogen reduction to NH3.CoPi/HSNPC is a hollow cobalt phosphate microsphere electrocatalyst employed for nitrogen reduction and oxygen evolution reactions under alkaline conditions.The unique monolithic core-shell configuration with a porous structure provides abundant accessible active sites and enhances charge-transfer properties.Core-shell structures refer to the morphology of MOF-derived nitrogen-doped carbon/Co3O4 nanocomposites, which enhances their electrochemical activity for nitrogen fixation.CoS/MoS2 is a heterojunction catalyst where CoS nanoparticles are anchored on MoS2 nanosheets, reported as a high-efficiency NRR catalyst.Cobalt tetraphenylporphyrin (CoTPP) is a model catalyst that exhibits good electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction activity.Covalent organic frameworks (COFs) are synthesized to create a hybrid with carbon nanotubes (CNTs) for efficient nitrogen electroreduction.Chromium (Cr) is a versatile transition metal that plays a significant role in various electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is utilized in the construction of single cluster catalysts (SCCs), which are designed to enhance the efficiency of nitrogen reduction processes. Additionally, Cr is a key component of transition-metal-embedded graphdiyne monolayers (TM@GDY), which are also investigated for their potential in nitrogen reduction reactions. Furthermore, Cr is one of the constituent elements in high-entropy alloys (HEAs) that are being studied for their effectiveness in synthesizing ammonia through nitrogen reduction. Its ability to be anchored in nitrogen-vacancy stabilized graphitic carbon nitride/graphene heterojunctions further contributes to its enhanced activity in NRR. Overall, Cr's diverse applications in various catalytic systems underscore its importance in advancing nitrogen reduction technologies.Cr-1 is a single metal atom supported on pyrrolic-N-3-G evaluated for its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Cr2N is the pure phase identified by X-ray diffraction of the chromium nitride catalyst, recognized as the active phase in ENRR.Cr2O3-rGO is a hybrid catalyst that achieves a high NH3 yield of 33.3 Œºg h(-1) mg(-1)(cat) at -0.7 V vs RHE.CrN is a phase of chromium nitride that shows negligible ENRR rate when synthesized without Cr2N species on the surface.CrNxOy is a species present on the surface of the chromium nitride catalyst, identified through X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS).CrOx is another species present on the surface of the chromium nitride catalyst, identified through X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS).Copper (Cu) is a versatile chemical element and transition metal recognized for its significant role in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). It serves as an optimal catalyst due to its active sites that facilitate the reduction of nitrogen (N‚ÇÇ) to ammonia (NH‚ÇÉ), thereby enhancing the overall efficiency of the process. Cu is integral to various catalytic systems, including bimetal alloy catalysts where it accelerates hydrogen desorption, leading to improved Faradaic efficiency.

In addition to its standalone catalytic properties, Cu is a key component in several advanced materials and structures. It is part of the Cu-nanosphere film, which demonstrates high electrocatalytic activity for NRR, and is utilized in the formation of copper single atoms within porous nitrogen-doped carbon networks, enhancing electrocatalytic performance. Cu also features in iron-based catalysts and trimetal Pd-based nanosheets, both of which are employed in nitrogen reduction applications.

Furthermore, Cu is involved in the preparation of CuTPP, a compound exhibiting electrocatalytic capabilities for NRR. Its use extends to nanosheet forms, which achieve high ammonia formation rates and Faradaic efficiency. Cu is also explored in the context of single cluster catalysts (SCCs) and as part of transition-metal-embedded graphdiyne monolayers, where it is screened for its potential to anchor and enhance electrocatalytic activity.

Moreover, Cu is a constituent of high-entropy alloys (HEAs) and is utilized in self-assembled monolayer methods to improve NRR performance. Its anchoring capabilities on monolayer graphdiyne and integration into MoS‚ÇÇ further illustrate its importance in advancing electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction technologies. Overall, Cu's multifaceted roles in various catalytic frameworks underscore its significance in optimizing ammonia synthesis through electrochemical processes.Cu NA/CF is a Cu-based nanowire array catalyst that possesses the highest Faradaic efficiency of 18.2% for NH3 production.Cu NA/CF is a Cu-based nanowire array catalyst used for nitrogen reduction reactions.Cu SAs/TiO2 refers to atomically dispersed Cu single atoms supported on TiO2 nanosheets, which achieve enhanced NRR activity and Faradaic efficiency under light irradiation.Cu(OH)2 NA/CF is a Cu-based nanowire array catalyst used for nitrogen reduction reactions.Cu-based materials are recognized as catalytic active materials for nitrogen reduction reactions, although they often exhibit inferior activities for hydrogen evolution.Cu-II-MOF on carbon cloth (JUC-1000/CC) acts as a versatile cathode and anode for both NRR and electro-oxidation of sodium gluconate.The Cu-nanosphere film is an electrocatalyst with a high surface area and dominant Cu(200) facet, showing high activity for NRR and NO3RR.Cu-Pt-Pd is a ternary composition of the cathode assembly studied for its reactivity in the nitrate-reduction reaction (NRR).Cu/Cu2O@C is a MOF-based derivative catalyst prepared for nitrogen and nitrate reduction to synthesize ammonia.Cu/Ti3C2 is a composite catalyst that demonstrates excellent selectivity for nitrogen reduction under environmental conditions.Cu1.81S is a metal sulfide nanocatalyst used for nitrogen reduction reactions with specific surface geometries.Cu3N NA/CF is a Cu-based nanowire array catalyst used for nitrogen reduction reactions.Cu3P NA/CF is a Cu-based nanowire array catalyst used for nitrogen reduction reactions.Cu3P@NC is an electrocatalyst prepared via low-temperature pyrolysis-phosphating of Cu-MOFs, exhibiting high ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency in nitrogen reduction reactions.Cu@NC nanocubes are synthesized for electrochemical nitrogen reduction, enhancing ammonia synthesis ability.The phase-separated CuAg alloy is an electrocatalyst that exhibits excellent performance for nitrogen reduction reactions due to its rich interfacial Cu defective sites.CuAg/Ti(3)C(2)MXene is a catalyst that exhibits remarkable performance for electrochemical synthesis of ammonia under ambient conditions.Cuboctahedral iron nanoclusters (NCs) are investigated for their catalytic activity in nitrogen reduction reactions, showing higher NRR activity compared to the Fe(111) surface.CuO NA/CF is a Cu-based nanowire array catalyst that achieves a high NH3 yield rate for nitrogen reduction.CuO NA/CF is a Cu-based nanowire array catalyst that achieves the highest NH3 yield rate of 1.84 x 10(-9) mol s(-1) cm(-2) for nitrogen reduction.CuSn is a bimetallic alloy catalyst that can reduce N2 to ammonia with a relatively low limiting potential and activation barrier.D-& pi;* feedback bonds formed by 4d orbitals of Mo and & pi;* orbitals of N2 are crucial for the activation of nitrogen in the NRR.)<|COMPLETE|>d-2 pi* coupling is a mechanism that enhances the interaction between the catalyst and nitrogen molecule, facilitating the conversion of N2 to NH3.The d-band center is a crucial descriptor in the context of electrocatalysis, particularly for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). It relates to the binding energy of nitrogen, which is a key factor in predicting the catalytic performance of various catalysts. The position of the d-band center significantly influences how a catalyst interacts with nitrogen molecules, thereby affecting the efficiency and yield of ammonia production during the NRR. Understanding and optimizing the d-band center can lead to improved faradaic efficiency and overall performance of electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction processes.The d-band center theory is used to predict the catalytically active center in the high-entropy perovskite oxide.d-block metals are transition metals that suffer from inferior selectivity in nitrogen reduction reactions due to competition from H-2 evolution.D-FeN/C is a high-performance NRR catalyst consisting of atomically dispersed iron single sites embedded in porous nitrogen-doped carbon nanofibers.Defect engineering is a strategy used to enhance the nitrogen reduction reaction performance of metal-free polymeric carbon nitride (PCN) catalysts.Defect-engineered 2D-materials are used to create specific environments for nitrogen adsorption, enhancing catalytic activity in nitrogen reduction reactions.Defect-rich fluorographene nanosheet is synthesized via fluorination of graphene and used for nitrogen reduction reaction electrocatalysis.Delta G (H) is a thermodynamic evaluation standard for the hydrogen evolution reaction, used for comparison in selectivity.Delta G (N-2) is a thermodynamic evaluation standard for selectivity in nitrogen reduction reactions.Delta G(*NNH) values indicate the thermodynamic feasibility of the nitrogen reduction reaction involving the formation of *NNH.Delta G(max) refers to the maximum Gibbs free energy change that influences the catalytic performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Density functional theory (DFT) is a quantum mechanical computational method employed to explore the electronic structure of many-body systems. In the context of electrochemistry, DFT is utilized to investigate the nitrogen reduction activity of transition metal decorated bismuthene (TM@Bis). This approach allows researchers to predict the performance of catalysts by analyzing their electronic properties and interactions, thereby providing valuable insights into optimizing the nitrogen reduction reaction for enhanced ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency.Density functional theory (DFT) is a computational method extensively employed to analyze and predict the catalytic mechanisms and performance of electrocatalysts in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). It serves as a crucial tool for assessing the electrochemical reduction of nitrogen, allowing researchers to evaluate the catalytic activity of various catalysts. DFT calculations facilitate the investigation of the reaction pathways and the kinetics of proton transfer processes involved in NRR, thereby providing insights into optimizing electrocatalyst design and enhancing ammonia (NH3) yield and faradaic efficiency. Through these analyses, DFT plays a vital role in advancing the understanding of the fundamental processes governing nitrogen reduction and the development of more effective catalytic systems.Density functional theory (DFT) calculations are utilized to investigate the catalytic mechanisms and performance of various electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR), specifically focusing on the FeOOH(110) surface. These calculations provide valuable insights into the underlying processes that govern the efficiency and effectiveness of the NRR, enabling researchers to better understand how different electrocatalysts can be optimized for improved ammonia (NH3) yield and faradaic efficiency. Through the application of DFT, the intricate interactions and reaction pathways involved in the NRR can be elucidated, facilitating advancements in the development of more effective catalytic materials.DFT (Density Functional Theory) is used for theoretical calculations to explore the catalytic mechanism in nitrogen reduction reactions.)<|COMPLETE|>Density functional theory (DFT) calculations are used to investigate the catalytic activity and mechanism of TM embedded nitrogen-doped phosphorene.The distal associative route is identified as the preferable nitrogen reduction reaction pathway for certain catalysts.Dithionite is a reductant used in the study of nitrogenase to analyze electron flow.Dopants are elements added to electrocatalysts to modify their electronic structure and improve their performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.DR ZnS-rGO is a high-efficiency electrocatalyst for ambient N2-to-NH3 conversion, supported on reduced graphene oxide.Dual-site catalysts involve two different metals incorporated into a two-dimensional C3N-C2N heterostructure, providing isolated active sites for nitrogen reduction.ECSA, or electrochemical surface area, is a measure that indicates the amount of active surface area available for the nitrogen reduction reaction.)<|COMPLETE|>Edge sites refer to the active sites on the surface of the Bi NS that enhance its electrocatalytic activity for the NRR.Edge and corner sites in electrocatalysts can serve as active sites for nitrogen adsorption and reaction.Effective nitrogen reduction current is a measure used to describe the activity of the electrode materials in nitrogen reduction reactions.Electrochemical active surface area (ECSA) is a measure of the surface area of an electrode that is active in electrochemical reactions, relevant for evaluating the performance of electrocatalysts.)<|COMPLETE|>Electrochemical ammonia synthesis is a method where H atoms come from water instead of H2, aimed at reducing N2 to NH3.Electrochemical N-2 fixation is a method for synthesizing ammonia using renewable electricity.)<|COMPLETE|>The electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (eNRR) is an environmentally-friendly alternative to the Haber-Bosch process for ammonia production.The electrochemical reduction pathway is a method that facilitates nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) in an energetically favorable manner.Electrochemical stability refers to the ability of an electrocatalyst to maintain performance over time during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Electrochemically active surface area refers to the surface area of the catalyst that is available for the electrochemical reaction, impacting the overall efficiency of the process.Electron transfer (ET) is a process that sets the applied bias needed for nitrogen reduction reactions.ENRR, or electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, is a process aimed at producing ammonia (NH3) from nitrogen (N2) through electrocatalytic means. This method, often referred to as electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (eNRR), has garnered significant attention for its potential to synthesize ammonia under mild conditions, making it an attractive alternative to traditional methods. The eNRR process is specifically focused on the electrochemical conversion of nitrogen gas into ammonia, highlighting its relevance in the field of sustainable chemistry and energy.Electrocatalytic proton-coupled electron transfer (ePCET) is a method that utilizes an ePCET mediator to facilitate the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) while mitigating hydrogen evolution reaction (HER).Excellent stability indicates the durability of the electrocatalyst during repeated use in nitrogen reduction reactions.)<|COMPLETE|>F is a chemical element that plays a significant role in the electroreduction of nitrogen (N2) by enhancing the binding strength of N2 when doped into carbon materials. This enhancement is particularly effective when F is introduced into a three-dimensional (3D) porous carbon framework, which facilitates the electroreduction process. The incorporation of F into such carbon structures not only improves the interaction with nitrogen molecules but also contributes to optimizing the overall efficiency of nitrogen reduction reactions, making it a valuable component in the development of advanced electrocatalysts for ammonia (NH3) production.Faradaic efficiency is a critical metric in electrochemistry, quantifying the effectiveness of an electrocatalyst in converting electrical charge into chemical change, specifically in the context of electrochemical reactions. It is expressed as a percentage, representing the ratio of charge utilized for the desired reaction to the total charge passed through the system. In the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), Faradaic efficiency indicates how effectively the charge is used to produce ammonia (NH3).

Various electrocatalysts exhibit differing Faradaic efficiencies. For instance, the Fe2Mo3O8/XC-72 electrocatalyst demonstrates a Faradaic efficiency of 8.2% at an applied potential of -0.3 V. In contrast, the Cu single-atom catalysts supported on TiO2 achieve a higher Faradaic efficiency of 12.88%. Notably, the Co4N/Co-2 C@rGO catalyst shows an impressive Faradaic efficiency of 24.97%, highlighting its superior performance in the NRR process. These values underscore the variability in electrocatalyst performance and the importance of optimizing conditions to enhance NH3 yield and overall efficiency in electrochemical reactions.10.53% is the Faradaic efficiency of FeMoO(4) during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The Faradaic efficiency of 10.96% shows the effectiveness of the 2D C3N4-NV electrocatalyst in the nitrogen reduction reaction.13.30% is the Faradaic efficiency of hydroxyl-rich Ti3C2Tx QDs at -0.50 V.16.23% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by Fe-TCPP, demonstrating its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.The Faradaic efficiency of 17.6% at -0.3 V vs RHE shows the effectiveness of the Co-N-x-C electrocatalyst in the nitrogen reduction reaction.18.1% is the Faradaic efficiency of FeTe2/RGO during the nitrogen reduction reaction, demonstrating its performance.The Faradaic efficiency of 2.46% at -0.1 V vs RHE reflects the effectiveness of the TiO2@N-C electrocatalyst in the nitrogen reduction reaction.The Faradaic efficiency of 29.53% at -0.4 V vs RHE reflects the effectiveness of the CB[7]-K-2[B12H12]@Au electrocatalyst in the nitrogen reduction reaction.4.8% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by Ag nanosheets in nitrogen reduction reactions.The maximum Faradaic efficiency of 43.37% is achieved by cobalt pyrophosphate micro-flowers at a potential of -0.2 V.5.87% is the Faradaic efficiency of K2Ti4O9 during the nitrogen reduction reaction.51.05 +/- 0.16% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by TiO2/Ag/Cu7S4@SeCC, indicating its high performance among non-precious metal-based NRR electrocatalysts.6.17% is the Faradaic efficiency of ITO/G at -0.40 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE).7.2% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by RuCu-FNs, demonstrating its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.The Faradaic efficiency of 8.4% at -0.05 V vs RHE reflects the effectiveness of the Pd/gamma-MnO2 electrocatalyst in the nitrogen reduction reaction.9.1% is the faradaic efficiency achieved by Ti3C2Tx, demonstrating its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Faradaic efficiency (FE) is a critical metric for evaluating the effectiveness of electrocatalytic processes in electrochemical reactions. In the provided data, FE values demonstrate a range of efficiencies achieved under different conditions. Notably, one study reports a maximum FE of 21.99%, indicating a significant level of efficiency in the electrocatalytic process. Additionally, another measurement shows an FE of 11.56 ¬± 0.85% at an applied potential of -1.19 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), suggesting moderate efficiency under these specific conditions. Remarkably, an even higher FE of 48.64% is attained at a very low overpotential of -0.1 V vs RHE, highlighting the potential for enhanced performance in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions when optimized conditions are applied. Overall, these findings underscore the variability in Faradaic efficiency based on the applied potential and reaction conditions, which are crucial for optimizing NH3 yield and overall performance in electrocatalytic systems.The faradaic efficiency of 1.84% at -0.7 V vs. RHE indicates the performance of C-doped TiO2 nanoparticles in the NRR.The faradaic efficiency of 37.3% at -0.3 V vs. RHE reflects the effectiveness of the WO3/CFP electrode in the nitrogen reduction reaction.Faraday efficiency is a critical parameter in electrochemical reactions, quantifying the effectiveness of the conversion of charge into chemical products. It is observed that Faraday efficiency tends to decrease as the applied potentials increase, indicating a potential trade-off between reaction kinetics and efficiency. In the context of the nitrogen reduction reaction, the Fe3C@C electrocatalyst demonstrates a Faraday efficiency of 9.15%, highlighting its performance in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia under specific conditions. This efficiency value underscores the importance of optimizing reaction parameters to enhance the overall yield and effectiveness of electrochemical processes.The Faraday efficiency of 13.78% is achieved by P-doped V2O3/C at -0.25 V.The Faraday efficiency of 14.83% at -0.3 V (vs. RHE) indicates the efficiency of the TA-Au NWs in the nitrogen reduction reaction.The Faraday efficiency of 6.06% is achieved by original V2O3/C at -0.25 V.Faraday efficiency of 23.84% is the efficiency achieved by the mAu3Rh/NF electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.Faradic efficiency is a measure of the effectiveness of the electrocatalyst in converting reactants to products, reported as 36.9% for the heterostructure catalysts.Iron (Fe) is a versatile chemical element and transition metal that plays a crucial role in various electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It serves as an active site in multiple catalytic systems, including the synthesized bimetallic Fe-Ru single atom catalyst, the Fe2Mo3O8/XC-72 electrocatalyst, and the Fe-C@C3N catalyst, which exhibits superior NRR performance. Fe is integral to the Fe(111) surface and the Fe-N4 configuration, both of which are favorable for nitrogen activation and ammonia synthesis.

In addition to its role in single-atom catalysts, Fe is involved in the construction of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) and the modification of electrodes for enhanced catalytic activity. It is also a component of various nanocomposites, such as the FeNi@CNS and FeP2 nanoparticle-reduced graphene oxide hybrids, which are utilized for nitrogen fixation. Fe's presence in the trimetal Fe-Mn-Ga oxide electrocatalyst and the bimetallic MoFe compound catalyst further underscores its significance in NRR.

Fe is known to mediate N2RR electrocatalysis in diverse complexes and is a key active center in molecular catalysts with well-defined FeN4 structures. Its role extends to enhancing the magnetism and activation of N2 for electroreduction, particularly on Fe(110) surfaces. Additionally, Fe is part of atomically dispersed metal dimer species and transition-metal-embedded graphdiyne monolayers, both of which are studied for their catalytic performance in nitrogen reduction.

Overall, Fe is a critical element in the development of efficient catalysts for nitrogen reduction, contributing to the advancement of technologies aimed at ammonia synthesis and nitrogen fixation.Fe atoms are identified as the active centers for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) in FeMoO(4) nanorods.Fe ion grafted on MoO3 nanorods is an electrocatalyst that enhances electron harvesting ability and selectivity during the nitrogen reduction reaction.Fe nanodot-decorated MoS2 nanosheets are used as an efficient and flexible electrode for the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.Fe SAC/N-C is a highly efficient electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions, achieving a high Faradaic efficiency and NH3 yield rate.Fe&Ni@Mo2CS2 is a diatomic catalyst predicted to have excellent performance in electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe(111) is a periodic surface studied for its catalytic activity in nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Fe(II)Cu(II)Fe(III)-LDH is an electrocatalyst that shows improved NRR performance due to the introduction of ferrous iron.Fe-0.50-Bi2WO6 is an advanced electrocatalyst that demonstrates an exceptional electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction performance, achieving an ultrahigh ammonia (NH3) yield rate. This remarkable performance is attributed to a straightforward iron (Fe) doping strategy, which enhances its catalytic properties. Specifically, Fe-0.50-Bi2WO6 exhibits a formation rate of 289 Œºg¬∑h‚Åª¬π¬∑mg(cat)‚Åª¬π when operated at a potential of -0.75 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). This combination of innovative synthesis and optimized reaction conditions positions Fe-0.50-Bi2WO6 as a promising candidate for efficient nitrogen reduction applications.Fe-1 is a single metal atom supported on pyrrolic-N-3-G evaluated for its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe-1-N-C is a single-atom catalyst that shows high faradaic efficiency and ammonia yield in the nitrogen reduction reaction.Fe-1/g-C3N4 is a single-atom catalyst used for electrochemical nitrogen reduction.Dual-atom Fe-2 species on g-C3N4 are investigated for their catalytic properties in nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe-2(MoO4)(3) nanoparticles are a non-noble-metal electrocatalyst inspired by nitrogenase enzymes for electrochemically converting nitrogen into ammonia.Fe-2@Ir(100) is an electrocatalyst with high catalytic activities for nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe-85 NC is an octahedral-shaped iron nanocluster electrocatalyst that exhibits a low overpotential of 0.29 V for NRR.Fe sites supported on boron, nitrogen co-doped carbon (Fe-B/NC) is a catalyst synthesized for nitrogen reduction reaction with atomic dispersion.Fe-C@C3N is an electrocatalyst that achieves the best NRR performance and follows the enzymatic mechanism.Fe-Cu DAC is a dual-atom catalyst with Fe-Cu atomic pair sites that achieves a remarkable NH3 yield and high Faradaic efficiency for NH3 production.Fe-CuS/C is a metal chalcogenide catalyst that demonstrates significant activity in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), facilitating the production of ammonia (NH3). This catalyst not only plays a crucial role in enhancing NH3 yield but is also utilized as an electrode for the in-situ detection of electrochemically generated NH3. Its dual functionality underscores its importance in both catalysis and analytical applications within electrochemical systems.The Fe-doped strategy is a method used to enhance the catalytic performance of the Fe-0.50-Bi2WO6 catalyst for ammonia production.Fe-HCS is an electrocatalyst made of zero valent iron particles dispersed on hollow carbon spheres, enhancing nitrogen adsorption.Fe-Mn-Ga oxide is a trimetal electrocatalyst specifically designed for ammonia (NH3) production. It is synthesized using a straightforward one-pot hydrothermal method, which is subsequently followed by an annealing process. This preparation technique is aimed at optimizing the catalyst's performance in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions, enhancing its efficiency in converting nitrogen to ammonia.Fe-MoS2 is a novel catalyst with Fe atomically dispersed onto MoS2 nanosheets, designed to enhance nitrogen electroreduction into ammonia.Fe-N-C is a single-atom catalyst that incorporates a second transition metal to tune its electronic structure for improved NRR activity.Fe-N/C is an electrocatalyst with atomically dispersed Fe single atoms fixed on a carbon substrate, achieving high activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe-N/C-carbon nanotube (CNT) is a catalyst derived from a metal-organic framework and carbon-nanotube-based composite, featuring built-in Fe-N-3 active sites for enhanced NRR activity.Fe-N/C-CPs are modified Fe-N/C catalysts on carbon papers, effective for electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe-N4 is a configuration of a single-atom catalyst that plays a crucial role in stabilizing iron species, thereby enhancing nitrogen activation. This electrocatalyst configuration is characterized by the stabilization of atomically dispersed single iron (Fe) species, which significantly improves the efficiency of nitrogen activation processes, particularly for ammonia synthesis. The unique structural characteristics of Fe-N4 facilitate its function as an effective electrocatalyst, making it a valuable entity in the field of electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions.The Fe-Ru single atom catalyst is synthesized by anchoring metal single atoms on nitrogen-doped carbon nanorod spheres and exhibits optimized faradaic efficiency and NH3 yield for nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe-SnO2 is a Janus electrocatalyst designed for efficient nitrogen reduction and oxidation reactions.Fe-TCPP is an electrocatalyst that exhibited prominent performance with a high NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency for nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe-TMDA/GS is a double-atom Fe catalyst supported with graphene-based substrate, constructed for electroreduction of nitrogen into ammonia.Fe-TPPHZ is a well-designed two-dimensional nanosheet-like electrocatalyst that shows remarkable NRR activity at ambient conditions.Fe/Fe3O4 is an electrocatalyst prepared by oxidizing an Fe foil, exhibiting high Faradaic efficiency for NH3 production.Fe1Mo1-N is a bimetallic electrocatalyst that has demonstrated promising performance in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). Specifically, it achieved an NH3 yield rate of 46.64 Œºg mg(cat.)^(-1) h^(-1) and a faradaic efficiency of 1.12% at a potential of -0.8 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). This performance highlights its potential as an effective catalyst for NRR applications.Fe1Ni0.2Co145 is a catalyst used in the self-supporting carbon mat (FeNi-Co@CM) for electrochemical ammonia synthesis.Fe2B2 is proposed as a potential new candidate for effective nitrogen reduction reactions and NH3 synthesis.Fe2Mo3O8/XC-72 is recognized as an effective and durable electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), drawing inspiration from the natural nitrogenase process. This electrocatalyst demonstrates notable NRR activity, achieving an ammonia (NH3) yield of 30.4 Œºg h^(-1) mg^(-1) when an applied potential of -0.3 V is utilized.Fe2N is a reduced form of iron oxide that can also act as an active species in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Fe2N4@graphene is identified as the most efficient NRR catalyst with a limiting potential of -0.32 V.Fe2O3, also known as iron(III) oxide or ferric oxide, is a chemical compound that exhibits complex behavior in the context of nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). While it has been reported to have inferior NRR activity when considered alone, its performance significantly improves when combined with TiO2, demonstrating superior catalytic activity in this configuration. Additionally, Fe2O3 can be reduced to form active species that are beneficial for the NRR. This compound can be synthesized from glycerine, which contributes to its potential as a highly efficient catalyst for ammonia production. Overall, the effectiveness of Fe2O3 in NRR is influenced by its synthesis method, structural characteristics, and the presence of other materials, highlighting the importance of optimizing these factors to enhance NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency.Fe2O3/Cu is identified as an efficient electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), demonstrating a significant ability to promote the adsorption of nitrogen (N2) and exhibiting high catalytic activity. Notably, Fe2O3/Cu achieves an impressive ammonia production rate of 15.66 Œºg¬∑h^(-1)¬∑mg(cat)^(-1) when operated at -0.1 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). This combination of properties positions Fe2O3/Cu as a promising candidate for optimizing ammonia synthesis through electrochemical methods.Fe2O3/Fe3O4-CeO2 is an electrocatalyst that possesses a remarkably enhanced NH3 yield rate and Faradaic efficiency in nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Fe2O3/TiO2 is a composite catalyst used for electrochemical ammonia synthesis from nitrogen/air.Fe2P is a catalytic system that exhibits electrocatalytic activity toward nitrogen reduction in alkaline medium.Fe3 @Gra is an electrocatalyst that shows high catalytic activity with a limiting potential of -0.60 V.Fe3@C2N is a catalyst proposed for effective electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.Fe3C/Fe2O3/Fe/C is a composite electrocatalyst that exhibits enhanced ammonia formation rate and faradaic efficiency in NRR.Fe3C@C is a bioinspired composite designed as an efficient electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions. This innovative material mimics the natural vein structure, featuring multichannels that enhance its performance in catalyzing the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia. The unique structural characteristics of Fe3C@C contribute to its effectiveness, making it a promising candidate for optimizing electrochemical nitrogen reduction processes.Fe3O4 is a chemical compound that plays a significant role in the field of electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is utilized as an electrocatalyst, particularly in the form of Fe3O4@MoS2, which combines Fe3O4 with MoS2 nanosheets to enhance catalytic performance. This compound is also a component of bimetallic nanotubes and Fe-based oxide composite nanoparticles, both of which exhibit remarkable activity and selectivity for nitrogen reduction. The synthesis of Fe3O4 nanoparticles has been optimized to achieve high Faradaic efficiency and ammonia yield, making it a valuable material in the development of efficient electrocatalysts for NRR applications.Fe3O4/Ti is a spinel nanorod electrocatalyst that acts efficiently for nitrogen reduction reaction under ambient conditions.Fe3O4@MoS2 is an electrocatalyst that exhibits excellent catalytic performance with an NH3 yield rate of 73.24 Œºg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) at 0.5 V vs. RHE.Fe3P is an electrocatalyst that is modified with octadecanethiol to enhance its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe4 clusters fixed on g-C6N6 are explored for their catalytic properties in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).Fe4 clusters are fixed on g-C6N6 and are used for nitrogen reduction reactions.Fe@MXene is a novel non-precious cathode catalyst consisting of Fe nanoparticles loaded on two-dimensional MXene nanosheets, achieving high Faradaic efficiency and NH3 yield.FeAg nanoclusters dispersed on Si nanowire are used as a synthetic plasma-enhanced bimetallic catalyst in the study.FeCoOOH HNCs are constructed via controlled Co doping and serve as an effective electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).FeFe sites are another type of electrocatalyst compared to FeNi heteronuclear sites for N-2 activation in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Fe-doped titanium dioxide hollow nanospheres (FeHTNs) are designed for nitrogen reduction reactions with abundant oxygen vacancies.FeM-GDYs are Fe-based transition metal double-atom catalysts supported on graphdiyne, showing improved activities for nitrogen reduction.FeMo-N is an electrocatalyst supported on nickel foam, fabricated through a hydrothermal-pyrolysis method, used for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).FeMo/g-C3N4 is a heteronuclear dual-atom catalyst that was screened as an effective electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.FeMo/NC is a catalyst designed with iron-molybdenum sub-nanoclusters and single atoms on porous nitrogen-doped carbon for nitrogen reduction reaction.FeMoNxC is an electrocatalyst that facilitates the selective electroreduction of nitrogen to ammonia, exhibiting high catalytic activity.FeMoO(4) nanorods are explored as an efficient electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) in 0.1 M Na(2)SO(4) solution.FeMoO4 is emerging as a promising candidate for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) in neutral solutions, demonstrating both effectiveness and durability. Specifically, vulcanized FeMoO4 has been engineered as an electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction, showcasing outstanding catalytic performance in neutral media. This positions FeMoO4 as a significant material in the pursuit of efficient and sustainable ammonia production through electrochemical methods.FeN(3) is an electrocatalyst embedded in graphane and fluorographene, investigated for its nitrogen reduction reaction performance.FeNi-Co@CM is a self-supporting carbon mat used as an electrode for electrochemical ammonia synthesis.FeNi-PBA is an electrocatalyst designed to facilitate N-2 activation in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).FeNi2S4/NiS is a hetero-interface electrocatalyst that enhances the adsorption of N-2 molecules while inhibiting H atoms adsorption for improved NRR performance.FeNi@CNS is a nanocomposite catalyst designed for efficient nitrogen fixation under ambient conditions, demonstrating high NRR performance.FeOOH is an effective electrocatalyst for the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (eNRR), particularly when paired with Lewis acid-base pairs, which enhances its catalytic performance. This compound exhibits good electrochemical stability, making it suitable for prolonged use in electrochemical applications. Additionally, FeOOH demonstrates excellent selectivity for nitrogen reduction reactions under ambient conditions, indicating its potential for efficient ammonia production in practical settings. Overall, FeOOH stands out as a promising candidate for optimizing nitrogen reduction processes due to its favorable properties and performance characteristics.FeOx-coated carbon fiber paper is an electrocatalyst used in nitrogen reduction reactions.Ti-doped iron phosphide (FeP) is an electrocatalyst used for electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.FeP/C is an electrocatalyst that, when doped with Pt, shows improved performance in nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).FeP2-rGO is a hybrid electrocatalyst that shows lower performance compared to FeP2-rGO hybrid in nitrogen reduction.FePc is a molecular electrocatalyst grafted on an O-MWCNT surface to improve recyclability and enhance electrocatalytic ability for NRR.FeRh is identified as the best catalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions due to its low limiting potential and high selectivity.FeS2 is a catalyst synthesized via a one-step hydrothermal method for nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).FeTe2 is an electrocatalyst characterized by its abundant surface-terminated and low-coordinate iron (Fe) sites, which facilitate the activation of the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This unique structural feature allows FeTe2 to achieve a low limiting potential of -0.84 V, indicating its effectiveness in promoting the NRR. Furthermore, FeTe2 is recognized as an efficient and durable electrocatalyst, making it a promising candidate for applications aimed at enhancing ammonia (NH3) yield and faradaic efficiency in electrochemical processes.FeTe2 nanoparticles supported on reduced graphene oxide (FeTe2/RGO) demonstrate significant activity in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), characterized by a notable combination of ammonia (NH3) yield and Faradaic efficiency. The incorporation of FeTe2 onto the reduced graphene oxide substrate enhances the overall performance of the electrocatalyst, making it a promising candidate for efficient nitrogen fixation processes.FeTPPCl is a molecular iron catalyst that exhibits promising NRR activity with the highest NH3 yield of 18.28 ¬± 1.6 ¬µg h(-1) mg(1)cat. and faradaic efficiency of 16.76 ¬± 0.9 % at -0.3 V vs. RHE in neutral electrolytes.First-principles calculations are computational methods used to predict the performance of catalysts in nitrogen reduction reactions.The flower-like (Fe,Mn,Ga)(3)O4-x morphology refers to the structural appearance of the trimetal oxide electrocatalyst.Fluorographene is a functionalized carbon material that accelerates electrocatalytic N(2) fixation.Graphitic carbon nitride (g-C3N4) is a versatile substrate that plays a crucial role in supporting dual-atom Fe-2 species and transition metal catalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions. This carbon nitride material enhances the performance of electrocatalysts, particularly when combined with cobalt (Co), as it demonstrates lower Delta G(*NNH) values, indicating improved thermodynamic efficiency for nitrogen reduction. Additionally, g-C3N4 can be engineered with nitrogen vacancies, which are utilized in single-atom heterostructures to facilitate the conversion of nitrogen (N2) into ammonia (NH3). Overall, g-C3N4 serves as a significant electrocatalyst in optimizing nitrogen reduction processes, contributing to enhanced ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency.g-C3N4 with nitrogen vacancies is the morphology of the single-atom heterostructures used for N2 to NH3 conversion.g-C6N6 is a material that serves as a support for Fe4 clusters, significantly enhancing the performance of nitrogen reduction reactions. This support role is crucial for optimizing the efficiency and effectiveness of the nitrogen reduction process, which is essential for ammonia synthesis. By utilizing g-C6N6 in conjunction with Fe4 clusters, researchers aim to improve the overall yield and faradaic efficiency of the nitrogen reduction reaction, making it a valuable component in the development of advanced electrocatalysts for this purpose.Gallium (Ga) is a chemical element classified as a post-transition metal, recognized for its significant role in the field of electrochemistry, particularly in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) for ammonia (NH3) production. It is a key component of the trimetal Fe-Mn-Ga oxide electrocatalyst, which is utilized to enhance the efficiency and selectivity of ammonia synthesis. In this context, Ga is often synthesized as well-isolated single atoms, which contribute to improved selectivity in NRR processes. As one of the main group metal elements, Ga is extensively studied for its potential to optimize electrochemical ammonia production, making it a critical focus in the development of advanced electrocatalysts.Gamma-Fe2O3 is an electrocatalyst utilized in the electrochemical synthesis of ammonia (NH3), exhibiting variable activity that is influenced by different experimental conditions. This metal oxide plays a significant role in the development of a free-standing three-dimensional (3D) hybrid electrode specifically designed for the nitrogen reduction reaction. The unique structural characteristics of gamma-Fe2O3 contribute to its effectiveness in facilitating the electrochemical processes necessary for efficient NH3 production.gamma-Fe2O3-NC/CF is a hybrid electrode that enhances electrocatalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reaction due to cation vacancies.gamma-MnO2 is an electrocatalyst that is compared with Pd/gamma-MnO2 and Pd/C in terms of catalytic performance for nitrogen reduction reactions.gamma-MnO2 is a support material for Pd nanoparticles, enhancing their catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.gamma-MnO2-supported Pd nanoparticles (Pd/gamma-MnO2) exhibit excellent catalytic activity for the electroreduction of N2 to NH3.The gas diffusion electrode (GDE) is a catalyst testing platform that improves detection challenges in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions.Gas diffusion electrodes are used in the context of nitrogen reduction reactions to evaluate the performance of high-entropy alloy (HEA) catalysts.Gas storage refers to the ability of CNT to store gases, which enhances the nitrogen reduction reaction efficiency.GCP-K methodology is a computational method used to predict reaction free energies and barriers as a function of applied potential.The GDE cell design is a catalyst testing platform that improves detection challenges in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions.GDY/Co2N is a freestanding 3D graphdiyne-cobalt nitride electrocatalyst with a highly active interface for the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.Gibbs free energy (Delta G) is a thermodynamic parameter that influences the reaction conditions for nitrogen reduction reactions.The Gibbs free energy change is a critical reaction condition for determining the potential-determining step in the nitrogen reduction reaction.Gibbs free energy variation is a thermodynamic parameter analyzed to understand the potential determining step of the nitrogen reduction reaction.)<|COMPLETE|>Gibbs free-energy changes for desorption of the second ammonia are important for understanding the efficiency of the NRR process.Glucaric acid is the product of the electro-oxidation of sodium gluconate in the two-electrode system with Cu-II-MOF.GO is a graphene oxide that serves as a comparison to BHGO in terms of electrocatalytic performance.Graphane is a carbon allotrope used as a substrate for the FeN(3) electrocatalyst in nitrogen reduction reactions.Graphdiyne (GDY) is a model substrate used to support Fe-based transition metal double-atom catalysts for nitrogen reduction.Mo@GDY is an electrocatalyst that shows high stability, high electrical conductivity, and excellent catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.Graphenylene-based triple-atom catalysts are investigated for their potential in nitrogen reduction reactions.gt-C3N4 is a support material for various transition metal atoms investigated as electrocatalysts for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).H poisoning refers to the detrimental effect of hydrogen on the performance of electrocatalysts during nitrogen reduction reactions.The H-2 evolution reaction (HER) is a competing reaction that affects the selectivity and yield of nitrogen reduction reactions.H-CrOx/C-550 is an amorphous metal oxide catalyst characterized by a hierarchically porous structure, which contributes to its superior electrocatalytic performance in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) under ambient conditions. This unique structural feature enhances its effectiveness in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, making H-CrOx/C-550 a promising candidate for applications in electrochemical nitrogen reduction.H-N2 interactions are significant for the distal mechanism in nitrogen reduction reactions, influencing the performance of the catalyst.H/F termination refers to the edge termination of zigzag graphene nanoribbons, influencing their catalytic properties in nitrogen reduction.H2 is the hydrogen gas that can compete with nitrogen in the nitrogen reduction reaction.H2O, or water, is used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction as a source of hydrogen atoms.H3O+ (hydronium ions) appear in high-pH electrolyte and facilitate nitrogen dissociation during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The Haber-Bosch process is a conventional and industrial-scale method for synthesizing ammonia (NH3) that operates under harsh conditions, specifically at high temperatures and pressures. This process is characterized by its significant energy consumption and substantial CO2 emissions, contributing to environmental pollution. It involves the direct dissociation of adsorbed nitrogen (N2) to produce ammonia. In contrast, the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) is being developed as a more sustainable alternative to the Haber-Bosch process, aiming to reduce the environmental impact associated with traditional ammonia production methods.The Haber-Bosch reaction is an industrial process for ammonia production that is energy-intensive and produces significant CO2 emissions.HCl, specifically in a concentration of 0.1 M, serves as the acidic electrolyte in various electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) aimed at evaluating the performance of different catalysts. This electrolyte is utilized for ammonia (NH3) electrosynthesis under ambient conditions, facilitating the NRR process with several catalysts, including MnO2-Ti3C2Tx, Co4N/Co-2 C@rGO, and OV-Ti2O3. Additionally, HCl is employed in the NRR process using a TiB2 film as the electrocatalyst. The consistent use of 0.1 M HCl across these studies highlights its importance in optimizing the electrochemical conditions for enhanced NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency in nitrogen reduction reactions.High-entropy alloys (HEAs) are a class of materials predicted to be efficient and sustainable for the electrochemical reduction of nitrogen, utilizing density functional theory and deep neural networks for optimization.HEM refers to hydroxide exchange membrane, a type of electrolyzer used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.Hematite is an electrocatalyst whose nanostructure surface can be modified to improve nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) performance by introducing more oxygen vacancies.Hematite nanostructure is modified to improve the performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.HER, or hydrogen evolution reaction, is a significant competitive reaction that occurs alongside the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), impacting the overall efficiency and selectivity of ammonia production. It poses a challenge in the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction process, as it competes with the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (ENRR) on all tested catalysts. The presence of HER can influence the performance of catalysts, making it crucial to consider its effects when optimizing conditions for nitrogen reduction. Overall, HER plays a critical role in determining the efficiency of ammonia synthesis by affecting the balance between hydrogen and ammonia production during the electrochemical processes.Heterointerfaces are formed by modifying the NiTiO3-based catalysts with ultra-small metal nanocrystal co-catalysts, improving charge separation and N2 activation.HEX (hexanethiol) is used in a self-assembled monolayer strategy to enhance the selectivity and activity of metal electrocatalysts for NRR.The Heyrovsky-like mechanism is a pathway in the nitrogen reduction reaction that involves interfacial water molecules.Hf is one of the transition metals that can be anchored in MoS2 to enhance its electrocatalytic activity for NRR.HfSe2 is an electrocatalyst that exhibits impressive selectivity over HER with a specific overpotential.HfTe2 is an electrocatalyst that exhibits impressive selectivity over HER with a specific overpotential.HGO is a holey graphene oxide that serves as a comparison to BHGO in terms of electrocatalytic performance.Hierarchical N doped carbon nanotubes represent a unique morphology that enhances the performance of the NRR catalyst.High active site density is a characteristic of the Pd3Cu1 alloy that contributes to its effectiveness in nitrogen reduction reactions.The high density of surface-exposed sites in the Mn2O3-MnO/Mn3O4 catalyst contributes to its outstanding electrochemical performance.High overpotential is a condition that limits the applicability of nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) in industry.High pressure is a condition that contributes to the further reaction of N2 fixation in the nitrogen reduction process.)<|COMPLETE|>High selectivity refers to the ability of the electrocatalyst to preferentially produce ammonia over other products during the nitrogen reduction reaction.High temperature and pressure are conditions under which the traditional Haber-Bosch process operates for ammonia production.High-curvature tips are a morphological feature of the U-L Co3O4 electrocatalyst that contribute to the generation of a strong local electric field for enhanced nitrogen reduction.)<|COMPLETE|>High-index facets refer to the specific surface orientations of Pt3Fe nanowires that enhance their electrocatalytic performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.High-index facets (HIFs) are specific surface structures of Pt-RE alloy concave nanocubes that enhance their catalytic performance in the nitrogen reduction reaction.High-pH electrolyte is used to enhance the performance of nitrogen reduction reactions by optimizing the local reaction environment.High-spin refers to the electronic configuration of iron in the Fe&Ni@Mo2CS2 catalyst that promotes nitrogen activation.High-temperature reduction route is a synthesis method used to produce bismuthene nanostructures for the nitrogen reduction reaction.Hollow bimetallic CoFe2O4 nanocube is an electrocatalyst derived from a prussian blue analogue, modified by a thin carbon layer and rich oxygen vacancies.Hollow carbon spheres are the structure on which zero valent iron particles are dispersed to form the Fe-HCS electrocatalyst.Hollow nitrogen-doped carbon polyhedra is the morphology of the substrate that enhances the performance of the oxygen vacancy-rich NiCo2O4 electrocatalyst.The hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is a significant competing reaction that can occur during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This competition affects the overall efficiency of the NRR process, as HER can limit the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia. Consequently, optimizing conditions to minimize HER is crucial for enhancing the efficiency of ammonia production through NRR.The hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is a significant competing reaction that impacts the efficiency, selectivity, and overall performance of electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). HER can hinder the effectiveness of these catalysts by leading to inefficiencies and often dominates the process, particularly under high overpotential conditions. To achieve higher Faradaic efficiency in NRR, it is crucial to suppress HER, as its occurrence can adversely affect the desired outcomes of nitrogen reduction. Thus, understanding and mitigating the influence of HER is essential for optimizing electrocatalytic performance in nitrogen fixation applications.The hydrogenation of Pd nanorods (Pd NRs) with N,N-dimethylformamide is a synthesis method used to create palladium hydride nanorods (PdH0.43 NRs) for electrochemical ammonia synthesis.Hydrophobic carbon fiber paper (CFP) serves as a substrate for the Au nanoparticles, facilitating efficient three-phase contact points for nitrogen reduction reactions.The hydrophobic molecular layer is a surface modification that enhances the eNRR efficiency by improving active site exposure.Hydrophobic treatment refers to the process of modifying catalyst surfaces to suppress the competitive hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) during NRR.)<|COMPLETE|>Hydrophobicity refers to the property of CNT that affects the interaction with water during nitrogen reduction reactions.Hydrothermal and electrodeposition methods are the synthesis methods used to create the MoS2@Fe(OH)(3) catalyst.The hydrothermal method is used to prepare Co and Ni bimetal loaded on CNS for investigating their electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) properties.Hydrothermal synthesis is the method used to synthesize LiNbO3 cubes with regular morphology for electrocatalytic NRR.Hydrothermal treatment is the method used to synthesize Cu-doped hematite (Fe2O3) nanorod arrays for enhancing NRR performance.Hydroxyl groups on Ti3C2Tx are identified as playing a key role in enhancing the electrocatalytic performance for nitrogen reduction.)<|COMPLETE|>Impedance measurements are utilized to study the performance and efficiency of the nitrogen reduction reaction with the Fe2P catalyst.In situ Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy is a technique used to demonstrate the role of coupling Fe and Mo atoms in enhancing NRR performance.)<|COMPLETE|>The indophenol blue method is a technique used to calculate ammonia yield in nitrogen reduction reactions, compared to NMR results.Interconnected porous architectonics refers to the morphology of the pAu/NF electrocatalyst, which contributes to its superior NRR performance.The interconnected porous scaffold morphology of the 3D alloy electrocatalyst enhances its electrochemical performance.The interfacial Cu defective sites in the phase-separated CuAg alloy are crucial for its high activity in nitrogen reduction reactions.Ir, a noble metal catalyst, has been evaluated for its effectiveness in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (ENRR) aimed at producing ammonia. Additionally, Ir is one of the transition metal atoms that has been screened for its potential to anchor on a monolayer graphdiyne, which serves as a substrate for enhancing the electrocatalytic reduction of nitrogen (N2). This dual role highlights Ir's significance in advancing the efficiency and performance of ammonia synthesis through electrochemical methods.Ir/C is a noble metal catalyst evaluated for electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (ENRR) to produce ammonia.Iridium nanoparticles on reduced graphene oxide (Ir/RGO) are reported as an effective electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.The isolated Fe and Mo atoms are part of the structure that contributes to the high performance of the NRR catalyst.Isotope N-15(2) experiments are used to verify the source of nitrogen in the produced ammonia during the nitrogen reduction reaction.Isotopic and chemical specificity refers to the ability to distinguish between different isotopes and chemical forms of NH3 in the NMR method.Commercial indium-tin oxide glass (ITO/G) is used as a catalyst electrode for artificial nitrogen fixation, demonstrating excellent selectivity at mild conditions.K is one of the main group metal elements discussed for their role in electrochemical ammonia production.k(1)approximate to 10.7x10(-3)min(-1) is the rate constant for the binary state of the cathode assembly in the nitrate-reduction reaction (NRR).k(1)approximate to 28.6x10(-3)min(-1) is the rate constant for the ternary state of the cathode assembly in the nitrate-reduction reaction (NRR).K2Ti4O9 nanobelt is an effective electrocatalyst for ambient nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) under alkaline conditions.KOH, or potassium hydroxide, is utilized as a 0.1 M alkaline electrolyte in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) to assess the performance of various catalysts, including the Fe single-atom catalyst. This alkaline electrolyte plays a crucial role in facilitating the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction process, particularly under alkaline conditions. Additionally, KOH is employed in the electrochemical synthesis of ammonia (NH3) using gamma-Fe2O3, highlighting its significance in optimizing NRR performance and enhancing NH3 yield. Overall, KOH serves as a vital component in the electrochemical systems aimed at improving nitrogen reduction efficiency.La-doped TiO2 nanorods are developed as an efficient electrocatalyst for ambient NH3 synthesis.La2Ti2O7 nanosheets are reported as an efficient NRR electrocatalyst with excellent selectivity under ambient conditions.The large surface specific area (SSA) of the TiO2/Ti3C2Tx electrocatalyst is crucial for its efficiency in the NRR process.)<|COMPLETE|>Layered double hydroxide (LDH) is an electrocatalyst that can be modified to enhance NRR activity by doping with ferrous iron.Layer double hydroxides (LDHs) are a type of morphology composed of first-row transition metals that are theoretically promising for N2 electroreduction.The Lewis acid pair refers to the catalytic centers designed to suppress the competing hydrogen evolution reaction and enhance nitrogen reduction.Lewis acidity is a property that can be tuned in the MoSx model catalyst to enhance its activation ability toward N-2.Lithium (Li) is a chemical element recognized for its catalytic properties in electrochemical nitrogen reduction systems. It serves as a mediator in the process of ammonia production, playing a significant role among the main group metal elements that are studied for their effectiveness in this electrochemical reaction. The utilization of Li in these systems highlights its importance in enhancing the efficiency and yield of ammonia synthesis through electrochemical methods.LiCl-KCl-CsCl is a molten alkali-metal chloride-based electrolyte used in ammonia synthesis processes.LiClO4 serves as an electrolyte in various electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions, demonstrating versatility in its concentration and application with different catalysts. Specifically, a 0.1 M solution of LiClO4 is utilized in conjunction with the MoS2-rGO catalyst, while a higher concentration of 0.5 M LiClO4 is employed with the FeP2 catalyst. Additionally, LiClO4 is also used in a mixture with ethyl acetate and water for the nitrogen reduction process. This highlights the importance of LiClO4 in optimizing the electrochemical environment for enhancing nitrogen reduction efficiency across different catalytic systems.LiF, or lithium fluoride, is a chemical compound utilized as a chemical agent in the mixed chemical etching process. Its primary function is to adjust and optimize the composition of catalysts, specifically in the context of Ti3AlC2 MAX and Ti3C2 MXene catalysts. By tuning the composition of these materials, LiF plays a crucial role in enhancing the performance and efficiency of the catalysts in various electrochemical applications.LiFe5O8-rGO is an efficient electrocatalyst proposed for artificial N2-to-NH3 fixation under ambient conditions.LiFeO2 is explored as a potential catalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).The lightning rod effect refers to the phenomenon where the high-curvature tips of the U-L Co3O4 electrocatalyst generate a strong local electric field, enhancing the nitrogen reduction reaction.The term "limiting potential" denotes the minimum potential necessary for a catalyst to efficiently promote the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This parameter is essential as it significantly influences the performance of transition metals utilized in NRR processes. Understanding the limiting potential is crucial for optimizing electrochemical systems aimed at enhancing ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency in nitrogen reduction applications.LiMn2O4 is a noble-metal-free electrocatalyst for NH3 synthesis that performs excellently under ambient conditions.LiNbO3, or lithium niobate (LN), is a material used to synthesize cubes with oxygen-rich vacancy for electrocatalytic NRR.Linear sweep voltammetry (LSV) is a method used to evaluate the electrochemical performance of materials in nitrogen reduction reactions.The lithium-mediated process (LiNR) is considered a promising route in the field of NRR due to its outstanding ammonia yield and Faradaic efficiency.Lithium species are anchored on the 3-fold hollow site of the electrocatalyst, reducing the hydrogen reduction rate in undesired water splitting.Lotus-root-like amorphous TiO2 nanofibers possess abundant intrinsic oxygen vacancies and under-coordinated dangling bonds, enhancing N2 activation and electron transport capacity.The low limiting potential of FeTe2 indicates its efficiency in activating the nitrogen reduction reaction.Low over potentials are conditions under which the nitrogen reduction reaction is conducted, indicating the voltage required to drive the reaction efficiently.Low selectivity is a challenge faced in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) due to competition from the hydrogen evolution reaction (HER).Low-pH electrolyte is used in nitrogen reduction reactions but shows inferior performance compared to high-pH electrolyte.Low-spin refers to the electronic configuration of nickel in the Fe&Ni@Mo2CS2 catalyst that aids in ammonia desorption.Low-valent transition-metal doping is a method used to enhance the catalytic activity of electrocatalysts for NRR and HER.The MvK mechanism is a process that can be evoked in the nitrogen reduction reaction systems.Magnetron sputtering is a technique used to integrate Ni3+ and oxygen vacancies on NiO@TiO2 for improved catalytic performance.The Mars-van Krevelen (MvK) mechanism is a proposed pathway for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) involving the participation of H+.The Mars-van Krevelen mechanism is a proposed pathway for the activation of dinitrogen during the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (ENRR). This mechanism outlines the reaction pathway followed when utilizing N-15(2) as the feeding gas, as evidenced by isotopic labeling experiments. It serves as a critical framework for understanding the processes involved in nitrogen reduction, highlighting the significance of isotopic studies in elucidating the underlying mechanisms of this reaction.The Mars-van Krevelen reaction mechanism is a proposed pathway for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) on the surface of Co4N.Mass transport refers to the movement of reactants and products in the nitrogen reduction reaction, which affects the overall efficiency.MAU3RH/NF is a bimetallic mesoporous electrocatalyst specifically synthesized for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This catalyst is designed to enhance the efficiency and yield of ammonia production through electrochemical processes.MBenes are a class of two-dimensional materials that can capture nitrogen molecules in their hollow sites, enhancing their catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.Metal sulfide refers to a class of electrocatalysts, including Fe-CuS/C, that are used in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are used as precursors for the synthesis of the amorphous metal oxide catalyst H-CrOx/C-550.The micelle-assisted electrodeposition method is a synthesis method for the direct fabrication of porous Au film on Ni foam (pAu/NF) for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).MIL-125(Ti) is a metal-organic framework used as a precursor for synthesizing C-doped TiO2/C material.Manganese (Mn) is a versatile chemical element that plays a significant role in various materials and catalysts designed for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is a key component of the trimetal Fe-Mn-Ga oxide electrocatalyst, which is utilized for ammonia (NH3) production. Additionally, Mn serves as a central metal in the preparation of manganese tetraphenylporphyrin (MnTPP), a compound that exhibits electrocatalytic activity for NRR. 

As a transition metal, Mn is also employed in the construction of single cluster catalysts (SCCs) aimed at enhancing nitrogen reduction efficiency. Its incorporation into the Fe-N-C catalyst is known to improve electronic properties and overall NRR performance. Furthermore, Mn is part of the transition-metal-embedded graphdiyne monolayer (TM@GDY), which has been studied for its potential in nitrogen reduction applications. 

In the context of high-entropy alloys (HEAs), Mn is one of the constituent elements being investigated for its effectiveness in NRR. Overall, Mn is recognized as a crucial transition metal in the ongoing research and development of electrocatalysts for ammonia synthesis through nitrogen reduction.Mn-1 is a single metal atom supported on pyrrolic-N-3-G evaluated for its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Mn-2@Ir(100) is an electrocatalyst with high catalytic activities for nitrogen reduction reactions.Mn2O3-MnO is the core of a porous core-shell heterostructure designed for efficient N2 adsorption and reduction.Mn3O4 is a versatile chemical compound that plays a significant role in the development of advanced electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is utilized in the preparation of the MXene/Mn3O4 composite catalyst, enhancing the overall performance in ammonia synthesis. Additionally, Mn3O4 is a key component of the Mn3O4@rGO composite electrocatalyst, where it contributes to improved efficiency in ammonia formation. As a non-noble-metal electrocatalyst, Mn3O4 exhibits notable efficiency in NRR under ambient conditions, making it a promising alternative to traditional noble-metal catalysts. Density functional theory (DFT) calculations further support its superior NRR activity, highlighting its potential for practical applications. Furthermore, Mn3O4 serves as the shell in a porous core-shell heterostructure, which is specifically designed to facilitate efficient nitrogen (N2) adsorption and reduction, thereby optimizing the overall catalytic process.Mn3O4@rGO is a composite electrocatalyst reported for its efficiency and selectivity in ammonia formation.MnB is an electrocatalyst that demonstrates notable selectivity over HER with a specific overpotential.MnMoO4 is a bimetallic electrocatalyst that demonstrates high activity and durability for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).MnN4 is an active material used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, which delivers a high yield of NH3 when combined with NaBF4.MNO, specifically referring to MnO particles, serves as a highly active catalyst for the electrocatalytic hydrogenation of nitrogen (N‚ÇÇ) to ammonia (NH‚ÇÉ). These particles demonstrate excellent selectivity in this reaction, highlighting their potential in optimizing electrochemical nitrogen reduction processes.Fe-doped manganese oxide (MnO2) is synthesized as a cost-effective electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.MnO2-Ti3C2Tx is a proposed highly active electrocatalyst for the N2 reduction reaction with strong durability.MnO3 is an electrocatalyst that is used as a benchmark for comparison, showing inferior performance compared to the developed electrocatalyst.Molybdenum (Mo) is a versatile chemical element and transition metal that plays a significant role in various electrocatalytic processes, particularly in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) for ammonia synthesis. Mo facilitates electron transfer to tungsten (W), enhancing the adsorption and activation of nitrogen (N2) on its surface. It exhibits strong interactions with NNH species, which are crucial for the activation of N2. Mo is a key component in several electrocatalysts, including the Fe2Mo3O8/XC-72 system, Mo2C, and MoN/C hollow porous nitrogen-doped carbon nanorods, all of which are effective in nitrogen fixation.

Additionally, Mo is part of the MoSx material, which is also involved in NRR, and is incorporated into various doped catalysts that demonstrate excellent catalytic performance. It is featured in nitrogenase-inspired inorganic-based chalcogenide systems and trimetal Pd-based nanosheets, both of which are studied for their efficacy in nitrogen reduction. Mo is evaluated in binary transition metal combinations and is considered for anchoring in zigzag graphene nanoribbons to enhance NRR performance.

Furthermore, Mo is embedded in advanced materials such as the C6N6 sheet and graphdiyne monolayers, where it is screened for high catalytic activity and stability. It is also utilized in atomically dispersed metal dimer species to accelerate NRR kinetics and serves as a single-atom catalyst with a low onset potential of -0.25 V. Mo is a constituent of high-entropy alloys (HEAs) and can be anchored in Nvs-g-C3N4/graphene heterojunctions, further contributing to enhanced NRR activity. Overall, Mo is recognized as a promising electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions, showcasing a wide range of structural and functional applications in the field of electrochemistry and catalysis.Mo-1 is a single metal atom supported on pyrrolic-N-3-G evaluated for its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Mo-3@GP is an electrocatalyst that shows the best nitrogen reduction reaction performance with a low limiting potential of -0.39 V.Mo-COF is a two-dimensional conductive covalent organic framework that exhibits the highest electrocatalytic performance for nitrogen fixation among the studied candidates.Mo-doped catalysts are investigated for their catalytic performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.)<|COMPLETE|>Mo-doped W18O49 is an enhanced electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions with improved performance.The Mo-Mo homonuclear complex exhibits exceptional nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) activity, significantly enhancing the overall performance of the catalysts. Its unique catalytic properties enable it to achieve the highest NRR activity among the studied complexes, underscoring its potential in electrochemical applications.Mo-N-3-BP is an electrocatalyst that exhibits an extremely low overpotential of -0.02 V and superior selectivity for nitrogen reduction reactions.The Mo-PNP complex is a molecular catalyst explored for electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reactions.Mo-PTA is a phosphotungstic acid used in the Mo-PTA@CNT electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.Mo-PTA@CNT is an electrocatalyst designed with Mo species anchored on phosphotungstic acid and embedded in carbon nanotubes.Mo-sTCNQ is another promising electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction catalyst obtained through high-throughput screenings.Mo/BP is an efficient electrocatalyst for nitrogen fixation, exhibiting the best catalytic performance among the tested materials.Mo/gt-C3N4 is identified as the best catalyst for standing-on adsorbed N2 cases with a limiting potential of -0.82 V.Molybdenum carbide (Mo2C) is a two-dimensional electrocatalyst specifically synthesized for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). To enhance its stability and performance during the NRR, Mo2C is protected by layers of graphene, which serve to prevent leaching of the catalyst. This protective layer is crucial for maintaining the integrity of the catalyst and optimizing its efficiency in producing ammonia (NH3) from nitrogen.Mo2C/C is an advanced electrocatalyst designed for efficient nitrogen fixation via the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This catalyst comprises molybdenum carbide nanodots that are embedded within ultrathin carbon nanosheets, enhancing its performance as a catalyst candidate for NRR. The unique structural characteristics of Mo2C/C contribute to its effectiveness in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, making it a promising option for improving nitrogen fixation processes.Mo2C@3DUM-C is an electrocatalyst developed for enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction performance.Mo2CTX is a type of MXene used as an electrocatalyst for nitrogen fixation.Mo2N is a nanorod electrocatalyst reported to be highly efficient and selective for nitrogen reduction reactions.Mo3Fe3C is an active material found to have excellent nitrogen-fixing catalytic performance and inhibit hydrogen evolution reaction.Mo@C6N6 is a screened catalyst that shows high catalytic activity and selectivity towards nitrogen reduction reactions.MoB is an electrocatalyst that shows significant selectivity over HER with a specific overpotential.The modified Hummer method is used to prepare two-dimensional carbon nanosheets (CNS) for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).MOF refers to metal-organic frameworks that are used in conjunction with carbon nanotubes to enhance ammonia production in the nitrogen reduction reaction.MoFe compound is a bimetallic catalyst prepared under NH3 atmosphere, exhibiting improved electronic interactions for enhanced NRR performance.MoFe-PC is a cost-efficient electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction, synthesized by one-step pyrolysis-phosphating of bimetallic metal-organic framework (MOF) precursors.MoFe/C2N is considered a promising catalyst for eNRR due to its low limiting potential and high selectivity.Molecular dynamics simulations are used to study the kinetics and mechanisms of the nitrogen reduction reaction.Molecular dynamics (MD) simulations were used to study the effects of the hydrophobic molecular layer on proton transfer and active site exposure in the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction.Molten salt calcination is a process that follows hydrothermal synthesis to produce LiNbO3 cubes for electrocatalytic NRR.MoN is a chemical compound used in the MoN nanorods supported on carbon fabric cloth for nitrogen reduction reactions.MoN NA/CC is a high-performance electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) that operates under ambient conditions.MoN nanorods are used as an electrocatalyst supported on carbon fabric cloth for nitrogen reduction reactions.MoN nanosheets array on carbon cloth acts as a high-performance electrocatalyst for NH3 electrosynthesis under ambient conditions.MoN/C hollow porous nitrogen-doped carbon nanorods are designed as an effective catalyst for nitrogen fixation.MoN/CFC is an electrocatalyst that provides an optimal NH3 yield and Faradaic efficiency for nitrogen reduction reactions.Monolayer graphyne (GY) is a two-dimensional material used to support transition metals for enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction performance.MOS2, specifically in the form of defective MoS2 sheets decorated with single transition-metal (TM) atoms, has been extensively studied as an electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This material is recognized for its active edge sites, which play a crucial role in facilitating the electroreduction of nitrogen, while its inert surface limits reactivity. MoS2 is also a key component in hybrid systems, such as Fe3O4@MoS2, where it significantly contributes to the overall catalytic performance in NRR. In these hybrid configurations, MoS2 supports Fe3O4, enhancing the electrochemical activity and efficiency of the reaction. Additionally, MoS2 can be combined with reduced graphene oxide (rGO) to form a high-performance catalyst for nitrogen reduction, leveraging the unique properties of both materials. The synthesis of MoS2 nanostructures is often achieved through alkyl thiols modification, which further optimizes their performance in NRR applications. Overall, MoS2 demonstrates excellent selectivity and effectiveness as an electrocatalyst in the pursuit of efficient nitrogen reduction.MoS2(001) is a specific surface morphology of MoS2 that is described as inert for N-2 electroreduction.MoSe2 is a component of the MoSe2/Ti3C2 composite that serves as a reliable and effective catalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).MoSe2/Ti3C2 composite serves as a reliable and effective catalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).MOSX, specifically MoSx, is an electrocatalyst that plays a significant role in enhancing the nitrogen (N2) activation process. It achieves this enhancement through a mechanism involving sigma to d donation, which is influenced by the tuning of its Lewis acidity. This property is crucial for optimizing the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, ultimately improving the yield of ammonia (NH3) and the faradaic efficiency of the process.MoW/g-C3N4 is a heteronuclear dual-atom catalyst that can be applied to nitrogen reduction reactions as a photocatalyst.mPd-PdO/NF is a self-supported mesoporous palladium-based catalyst synthesized for nitrogen reduction reaction.MXene is a type of electrocatalyst that, when combined with Mn3O4, significantly enhances the performance of nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) by providing abundant active sites. However, it is important to note that MXene-based catalysts generally exhibit inadequate catalytic activity for NRR when considered alone, primarily due to the presence of inactive surface terminations. This duality highlights the potential of MXene in catalysis, particularly when optimized through strategic combinations with other materials like Mn3O4, to improve NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency in electrochemical processes.MXene (Ti3C2Tx) nanosheets attached to a vertically aligned metal host achieve a high faradaic efficiency of 5.78% for NH3 electrosynthesis at an ultralow potential.The MXene@NiCoB composite is an electrocatalyst that shows extensive improvements in nitrogen molecule chemisorption and charge transport.Nitrogen (N) is a chemical element that plays a significant role in various electrochemical processes, particularly in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) aimed at producing ammonia. In this context, N is co-doped with iron in porous carbon materials, enhancing the catalytic performance for NRR. The presence of nitrogen is crucial for the Fe-N4 configuration, which is instrumental in the activation of nitrogen molecules during the reaction. This configuration facilitates the efficient conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, highlighting the importance of N in optimizing electrochemical processes related to nitrogen fixation.N,P co-doped carbon is a substrate that supports Ni2P nanoparticles and regulates the electronic distribution during nitrogen adsorption, enhancing the catalyst's performance.N-15(2) is a stable isotope of nitrogen that plays a crucial role in various labeling experiments aimed at confirming the formation and source of ammonia during nitrogen reduction reactions. It is utilized to trace the origin of ammonia, ensuring that the nitrogen in the synthesized NH3 is derived from N2 present in the electrolyte. In batch cell testing, N-15(2) serves as a feed to validate the involvement of surface nitrogen in the activation of dinitrogen. Overall, N-15(2) is essential for verifying the source of nitrogen in ammonia production, providing insights into the mechanisms of nitrogen reduction reactions.N-2 is the diatomic nitrogen molecule that serves as the reactant in the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (ENRR). It is specifically reduced to produce ammonia (NH3) during the electrochemical reactions involved in this process. N-2 is the targeted nitrogen molecule for reduction in the nitrogen reduction reaction, highlighting its critical role in the synthesis of ammonia through electrochemical means.N-2 fixation is the process of converting nitrogen gas into ammonia, which is a focus of the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.)<|COMPLETE|>N-3 is a type of active site that is blocked by nitrogen-doped carbon, affecting ammonia production negatively.N-C@NiO/GP is a nanocomposite electrocatalyst that shows remarkable activity for nitrogen reduction reactions, achieving a faradaic efficiency of 30.43%.N-doped carbon is a type of electrocatalyst that is compared to the atomically dispersed NiN3 site-rich catalyst in terms of activity and selectivity for NRR.N-doped carbon nanosheets are the support material for the zinc active sites in the Zn/NC NSs electrocatalyst.N-doped carbon nanospikes are a highly textured physical catalyst that electrochemically reduces dissolved N2 gas to ammonia in an aqueous electrolyte.N-doped porous carbon serves as a support for single Ru sites, enhancing the electroreduction of N2 to NH3.N-MWCNT is a type of carbon nanotube used in the hybrid composite electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions.N2, or diatomic nitrogen, is the gaseous nitrogen molecule that serves as the primary reactant in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) aimed at producing ammonia (NH3). It is supplied to the membrane-electrode assembly cathode during the nitrogen reduction process, where it undergoes activation and reduction. The electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (ENRR) involves the conversion of N2 into ammonia, making N2 a crucial feedstock in this electrocatalytic process. Throughout the NRR, N2 is fixed and transformed into NH3, highlighting its role as the molecular form of nitrogen that is essential for ammonia synthesis in various electrochemical applications.The N2 -> N2H transformation is identified as the potential-determining step in the nitrogen reduction reaction on the MnO catalyst.N2 fixation refers to the process of converting atmospheric nitrogen (N2) into ammonia (NH3) through electrochemical reactions.The N2 reduction reaction is a process that involves the electrochemical conversion of nitrogen gas into ammonia, facilitated by various electrocatalysts.N2 solubility in the electrolyte is a critical reaction condition that affects the efficiency of the nitrogen reduction reaction.N2H is an intermediate formed during the nitrogen reduction reaction process.N2H* formation is the potential-determining step for the catalysts in the nitrogen reduction reaction.N2H4 is a byproduct that the VO2 catalyst avoids generating during ammonia synthesis.N2H4¬∑H2O is another product formed during the nitrogen reduction reaction on the NP-C-MOF-5 cathode.N2R refers to nitrogen reduction reactions, a method for synthesizing ammonia from nitrogen.N2RR, or nitrogen reduction reaction, is an electrochemical process specifically designed for synthesizing ammonia from nitrogen. This method plays a crucial role in the conversion of atmospheric nitrogen into ammonia, which is a vital component for fertilizers and various chemical processes. The N2RR process leverages electrochemical techniques to facilitate the reduction of nitrogen, highlighting its significance in sustainable ammonia production and its potential to contribute to greener chemical manufacturing practices.N3 is a nitrogen species involved in the nitrogen reduction reaction process.Sodium sulfate (Na2SO4) is utilized as a 0.1 M aqueous electrolyte in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (eNRR). This electrolyte serves as a crucial medium, providing the necessary ionic environment for the reaction to take place. It is specifically mentioned in conjunction with various catalysts, including FeS2 and Ti3C2Tx MXene, indicating its versatility and importance in facilitating the eNRR process. Overall, Na2SO4 plays a significant role in enhancing the efficiency and effectiveness of nitrogen reduction in electrochemical systems.NaBF4 is an aqueous electrolyte that acts as an N2-carrier and co-catalyst in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).The nano-CaCO3 template is a method used to optimize the morphology and pore structure of the carbon materials.Nanocube is one of the tunable morphologies of Pt3Fe nanocrystals used in nitrogen reduction reactions.Nanoporous NiSb alloy is reported as an efficient electrocatalyst for nitrogen fixation in the nitrogen reduction reaction.Nanorod is another tunable morphology of Pt3Fe nanocrystals used in nitrogen reduction reactions.Nanosponge (NS) architecture refers to the morphology of the bimetallic Ru-Cu mixture catalyst, which enhances its electrochemical active surface area.Nanowire is a tunable morphology of Pt3Fe nanocrystals that exhibits excellent selectivity and activity in nitrogen reduction reactions.Niobium (Nb) is a chemical element recognized for its role in doped catalysts that exhibit excellent catalytic performance in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is one of the transition metal atoms screened for anchoring on a monolayer graphdiyne, which is a promising material for electrocatalytic N2 reduction. Additionally, Nb can be anchored in nitrogen-vacancy sites of g-C3N4/graphene heterojunctions, contributing to enhanced NRR activity. This versatility in application highlights Nb's significance in optimizing electrochemical processes related to nitrogen reduction.Nb-1 is a single metal atom supported on pyrrolic-N-3-G evaluated for its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Nb-doped catalysts are investigated for their catalytic performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Nb-sTCNQ is a promising electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction catalyst obtained through high-throughput screenings.Nb2CTx is an electrocatalyst that works synergistically with BNQDs to improve nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) performance by lowering the reaction energy barrier.Nb2O5 is a chemical compound used to create a nanowire array for the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Nb2O5 nanofiber is a non-noble-metal electrocatalyst that shows high activity and selectivity for N2 conversion to NH3.Nb@S is an electrocatalyst that demonstrates notable selectivity over HER with a specific overpotential.NbB is an electrocatalyst that demonstrates notable selectivity over HER with a specific overpotential.Exfoliated metallic niobium disulfate (NbS2) nanosheets are developed as an electrocatalyst for electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).NbSe2 is an electrocatalyst tested for NH3 electrosynthesis, showing promising performance.NbTe2 is an electrocatalyst that shows significant selectivity over HER with a specific overpotential.NCNT is a carbon nanotube variant that serves as a catalytic center in the confined electrocatalysis strategy for enhanced ammonia production.Neutral conditions refer to the pH level under which the S-mAu film/NF electrocatalyst operates during the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Neutral electrolyte is a type of electrolyte used in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) that does not favor hydrogen evolution.NH is an intermediate in the nitrogen reduction reaction, formed during the hydrogenation process.NH2 is an important intermediate species in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), which plays a crucial role in the conversion of nitrogen (N2) to ammonia (NH3). It is formed through the hydrogenation of NH, another intermediate in this process. The presence of NH2 in the reaction pathway highlights its significance in facilitating the overall efficiency and yield of ammonia production during electrochemical nitrogen reduction.NH2NH2 is a product formed during the nitrogen reduction reaction, specifically in the associative mechanism.NH3, or ammonia, is a vital compound produced through the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), where nitrogen gas (N2) is reduced to form ammonia. This process is essential for synthesizing ammonia, which is crucial for fertilizers that support the growing global population and has various applications in chemicals and energy sources. The electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (ENRR) is a significant focus of research, as it offers a renewable alternative to traditional ammonia production methods like the Haber-Bosch process. Various electrocatalysts are employed to optimize the yield and Faradaic efficiency of NH3 production, with specific yields reported for different catalysts. Additionally, NH3 is generated in specific systems, such as the NP-C-MOF-5 cathode and the Zn-nitrate battery system, highlighting its versatility and importance in both agroindustry and as a potential energy source. Overall, NH3 is a key product of the nitrogen reduction reaction, with its synthesis being a critical area of study in electrochemistry and catalysis.The NH3 yield refers to the amount of ammonia produced by an electrocatalyst during a nitrogen reduction reaction, typically expressed in units of Œºg h^(-1) mg^(-1) or mol s^(-1) cm^(-2). Various electrocatalysts exhibit different NH3 yields, highlighting their efficiency in ammonia production. For instance, the Fe2Mo3O8/XC-72 electrocatalyst achieves an NH3 yield of 30.4 Œºg h^(-1) mg^(-1). In contrast, the NiO@TiO2 catalyst produces ammonia at a yield of approximately 10.75 Œºg h^(-1) cm(cat)(-2). Additionally, the Co4N/Co-2 C@rGO catalyst demonstrates a yield of 24.12 Œºg h^(-1) mg(cat)(-1), while another catalyst design achieves an NH3 yield of 24.47 Œºg h^(-1) mg^(-1) through a coupled activity/selectivity approach. These variations in NH3 yield underscore the importance of catalyst design and optimization in enhancing ammonia production efficiency.1.06 x 10(-10) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield of ITO/G during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The NH3 yield of original V2O3/C is 12.6 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) at -0.45 V.17.51 mu g h(-1) mg(-1) is the NH3 yield rate of FeMoO(4) during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The NH3 yield of P-doped V2O3/C is 22.4 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) at -0.35 V.22.88 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield attained by K2Ti4O9 in the nitrogen reduction reaction.36.9 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by Ti3C2Tx, indicating its effectiveness as an electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction.The NH3 yield of 37.6 mu g mg(-1) h(-1) at -0.9 V vs RHE indicates the performance of the Co-N-x-C electrocatalyst in ammonia production.39.16 +/- 2.31 mu g h(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield achieved by TiO2/Ag/Cu7S4@SeCC, indicating its effectiveness as an electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction.39.2 lg h-1 mg-1 is the NH3 yield achieved by FeTe2/RGO, indicating its effectiveness as an electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction.44.77 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield achieved by Fe-TCPP, indicating its effectiveness as an electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction.62.94 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is the NH3 yield of hydroxyl-rich Ti3C2Tx QDs during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The NH3 yield of 16.22 mu g h(-1) mg(cat.)(-1) is achieved with C-doped TiO2 nanoparticles in 0.1 M Na2SO4.NH3 yield of 26.29 mu g h-1 mg-1cat is the amount of ammonia produced by the mAu3Rh/NF electrocatalyst during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The NH3 yield of 39.2 lg h is achieved with FeTe2 nanoparticles, indicating their effectiveness in nitrogen reduction.NH3 yield rate is the rate of ammonia production, with Cu SAs/TiO2 achieving a yield of 6.26 Œºg h^(-1) mg(cat)^(-1) under specific conditions.The NH3 yield rate of 15.71 mg h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is achieved by TA-Au NWs, indicating their effectiveness in ammonia production.The NH3 yield rate of 159.6 mu g h(-1) mg(ca)(-1) is achieved by cobalt pyrophosphate micro-flowers at a potential of -0.2 V.The NH3 yield rate of 19.72 mu g center dot mg(Pd)(-1)h(-1) indicates the efficiency of the Pd/gamma-MnO2 electrocatalyst in producing ammonia.4.62 x 10(-11) mol s(-1) cm(-2) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by Ag nanosheets, indicating its effectiveness as an electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction.53.6 mu g h(-1) mg(cat)(-1) is the NH3 yield rate achieved by RuCu-FNs, indicating its effectiveness as an electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction.The NH3 yield rate of 4.3 mu g.h(-1).mg(-1) is achieved by the WO3/CFP electrode, indicating its efficiency in nitrogen reduction.The NH3 yield of 17.85 mu g h(-1)mg(cat.)(-1) indicates the performance of the 2D C3N4-NV electrocatalyst in ammonia production.Nickel (Ni) is a versatile chemical element that plays a significant role in various electrocatalytic systems aimed at enhancing the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). It is a key component of several advanced catalysts, including the FeNi heteronuclear sites, the FeNi2S4/NiS hetero-interface electrocatalyst, and the FeNi@CNS nanocomposite catalyst, which are all utilized for nitrogen fixation. Ni is instrumental in improving NRR performance through innovative methods such as the self-assembled monolayer technique.

In addition to its role in these composite catalysts, Ni is part of the bimetallic Co-Ni-S/Ni foam catalyst, which significantly enhances nitrogen activity. It serves as an active site in atomically dispersed electrocatalysts specifically designed for nitrogen reduction. The synthesis of Ni nanoparticles is also crucial for the electrocatalyst Ni@MX, while NiTiO3-based nanoelectrocatalysts leverage Ni's properties to efficiently activate and reduce nitrogen.

Furthermore, Ni is employed alongside gold (Au) nanoparticles to boost the faradaic efficiency of the NRR. It is a preferred transition metal for constructing single cluster catalysts (SCCs) and is involved in the development of single-atom catalysts, particularly in comparison to Fe-1-N-C. Ni is also part of the transition-metal-embedded graphdiyne monolayer (TM@GDY) and is evaluated as a candidate electrode material for NRR.

Moreover, Ni is a constituent element of high-entropy alloys (HEAs) studied for their potential in nitrogen reduction, and it has been screened for anchoring on monolayer graphdiyne to facilitate electrocatalytic N2 reduction. Overall, Ni's multifaceted roles in various catalytic systems underscore its importance in advancing nitrogen reduction to ammonia synthesis.Functionalized porous Ni foam substrates are used as electrocatalysts for ammonia production.Ni-0.3(FeCoCuPd)(0.175) is a high-entropy alloy that exhibits excellent performance in electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reactions with high thermal stability.Ni-13 is another cluster catalyst on TiO2(101) that demonstrates high selectivity and efficiency for nitrogen reduction reactions.Ni-4 is a cluster catalyst supported on TiO2(101) that shows efficiency in the electrocatalytic reduction of N2.Ni/NiFe-OH is a prototype electrocatalyst that enhances selectivity and catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reaction.Ni/Zn-NPC is a bimetallic co-doped nitrogen-coordinated porous carbon electrocatalyst for ammonia synthesis.Ni12P5/FeP4 is a self-supported bimetallic electrocatalyst that exhibits excellent performance in nitrogen fixation.Ni3 @Gra is an electrocatalyst that shows high catalytic activity with a limiting potential of -0.45 V.Ni3S4 is a catalyst that achieves a Faradaic efficiency of 6.8 +/- 3.3% for NRR with low-energy facets.Ni91.5Bi8.5 is a porous electrocatalyst that demonstrates significant catalytic activity for ammonia (NH3) formation in the nitrogen reduction reaction. It achieves a yield of 18.35 Œºg h-1 mg-1 and a Faradaic efficiency of 51.12% when operated at -0.3 V versus the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE). This combination of high yield and efficiency highlights the potential of Ni91.5Bi8.5 as an effective catalyst for electrochemical nitrogen reduction processes.Ni@MX is a highly efficient electrocatalyst composed of Ni nanoparticles loaded on V4C3Tx MXene, demonstrating significant NRR activity.Ni@Nb2C is a MXene-based catalyst that shows excellent electrocatalytic NRR performance.NiBi is a porous catalyst that provides abundant active sites for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Nickel telluride nanocrystals are synthesized to expose {001} and {010} facets, enhancing the performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.The NiCoP/CoMoP/Co(Mo3Se4)(4) @C/NF electrocatalyst is designed for excellent nitrogen reduction reaction performance with a high NH3 yield rate.NiFe layered double hydroxide (NiFe-LDH) is a catalyst designed to enhance nitrogen reduction reaction activity by introducing oxygen vacancies.NiFe-MoS2 is an advanced electrocatalyst synthesized using a Prussian blue analog self-templating strategy. This unique synthesis method results in a hollow structure that enhances its performance in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), facilitating the conversion of N2 to NH3. The electrocatalyst features trimetallic interactions, which further improve its catalytic properties. Specifically, NiFe-MoS2 is produced in the form of nanocubes, optimizing its electrocatalytic efficiency for NRR applications.NiFe2O4 is a spinel ferrite catalyst that exhibited appealing NRR performance with high NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency.NiMo/g-C3N4 is a heteronuclear dual-atom catalyst that can be utilized for nitrogen reduction reactions.NiN4/Cr is an electrocatalyst embedded in graphene that exhibits the best catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.Nickel oxide (NiO) is considered an effective electrocatalyst for ambient electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction due to its cost-effectiveness and adjustable eight-electron distribution in the 3d orbital.NiO nanodots supported on graphene (NiO/G) are developed as a high-performance electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions at ambient conditions.NiO@TiO2 is a transition metal oxide-based electrocatalyst that exhibits significant NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency.Nickel phthalocyanine (NiPc) is studied for its catalytic activity in the nitrogen reduction reaction.NiPS3 is a transition metal phosphorus trichalcogenide explored as an NRR electrocatalyst, showing excellent activities when exfoliated into nanosheets.NiSb2O6-x nanofiber is a Janus electrocatalyst designed to enhance selectivity in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).NiTiO3 is a nanoelectrocatalyst that has been synthesized to improve charge separation and facilitate the activation of N2.NiTiO3-based nanoelectrocatalysts are synthesized to harness the synergistic properties of perovskites for efficient nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Nitrate electroreduction (NORR) is the method proposed for NH3 production from nitrate.)<|COMPLETE|>The nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) is an electrochemical process aimed at converting nitrogen (N2) into ammonia (NH3).Nitrogen-doped and defect-rich carbon is designed as an efficient electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) to synthesize ammonia at ambient conditions.NixBy (SR) is a catalyst synthesized via sonochemical reduction that shows improved performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.NNH is an intermediate species in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) that is formed during the conversion of N-2 to NH3.NO (nitric oxide) is a nitrogen oxide that can compete with N2 in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) experiments.NO2 (nitrogen dioxide) is a nitrogen oxide that can compete with N2 in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) experiments.NO3- is a nitrogenous species that can be reduced to ammonia in the electrochemical reduction reaction (NO3RR).Non-aqueous electrolytes are utilized to suppress the competing hydrogen evolution reaction during nitrogen reduction reactions.These are types of electrolytes used in the NMR method for measuring NH3 concentration after electrocatalysis.NP-C-MOE-5 is a catalyst prepared for electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction in acid electrolytes under ambient conditions.np-CuMn is a nanoporous alloy electrocatalyst that shows improved nitrogen reduction reaction performance due to the synergy between Cu and Mn.nPd/NF is a type of electrocatalyst that serves as a comparison to nPdB/NF in terms of NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency.nPdB/NF is an interstitial boron-doped nanoporous palladium film that enhances nitrogen reduction reaction performance.NPG refers to nitrogen-doped porous graphene, which serves as a substrate for binary transition metals in nitrogen reduction reactions.The NRR, or electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, is a process focused on the efficient production of ammonia under ambient conditions. This reaction is significant in the context of sustainable chemistry, as it offers a potential alternative to traditional ammonia synthesis methods, which are often energy-intensive and environmentally detrimental. The NRR is characterized by its ability to operate at lower temperatures and pressures, making it a promising avenue for green chemistry initiatives.NS-Ti3C2Tx is a co-doped MXene catalyst that shows improved electron/ion transport capacity and catalytic active sites for NRR.The three-phase Au/o-CFP electrocatalyst exhibits excellent NRR performance with a high NH3 yield rate and Faradaic efficiency.O-KFCNTs are metal-free electrocatalysts derived from natural kapok fibers, used for nitrogen reduction reactions.O-v refers to oxygen vacancies that enhance the performance of the O-v-TiO2@C/Cu electrocatalyst.O-v-TiO2@C/Cu is a multifunctional composite catalyst designed for enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction performance.Octadecanethiol is a modification method used to enhance the performance of the Fe3P electrocatalyst in nitrogen reduction reactions.OH- is an anion in the electrolyte composition that facilitates nitrogen dissociation during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The one-pot hydrothermal method is a facile synthesis technique used to prepare the Fe-Mn-Ga oxide electrocatalyst.Os is a chemical element that mediates N2RR electrocatalysis in various complexes.OV-Ti2O3 is recognized as an active and stable electrocatalyst specifically designed for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It demonstrates exceptional performance in N2 fixation, making it a promising candidate for enhancing the efficiency of electrochemical processes aimed at nitrogen conversion. The material's stability and activity contribute significantly to its effectiveness in facilitating the NRR, underscoring its potential in applications related to sustainable ammonia production.Overpotentials are the extra voltage required beyond the thermodynamic potential to drive the NRR, influencing the reaction rate and efficiency.Oxygen vacancies are structural defects in materials that play a significant role in enhancing catalytic activity, particularly in nitrogen reduction reactions. In the context of TiO2 nanoparticles, these vacancies serve as active sites for ammonia (NH3) synthesis when utilized in the TiO2/Ti3C2Tx electrocatalyst. Additionally, in the C@CoFe2O4-x nanocube morphology, the presence of oxygen vacancies contributes to its improved catalytic performance for nitrogen reduction. Overall, the manipulation and optimization of oxygen vacancies in various materials can lead to enhanced efficiency and yield in electrochemical nitrogen reduction processes.Oxygen vacancies (OVs) in TiO2 act as active sites that enable efficient chemisorption and activation of N2 molecules.Oxygen vacancy-rich NiCo2O4 is an electrocatalyst that shows outstanding NRR performance with high production yield and faradaic efficiency under ambient conditions.Oxygen-rich vacancy is a structural feature of LiNbO3 cubes that enhances their performance in the nitrogen reduction reaction.P-doped V2O3/C is an enhanced electrocatalyst that achieves a higher NH3 yield and Faraday efficiency compared to original V2O3/C.P-electron density refers to the distribution of p-electrons in the electrocatalyst, which can influence its catalytic properties in the NRR.Palladium hydride is an electrocatalyst that has been reported to facilitate the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) under ambient conditions. This material is generated through the electrochemical dealloying of PdBi2 nanoflakes, a process that enhances its structural characteristics and, consequently, its performance in nitrogen reduction. The formation of palladium hydride significantly improves the efficiency of the nitrogen reduction reaction, making it a promising candidate for optimizing ammonia (NH3) yield and faradaic efficiency in electrochemical applications.pAu/NF is a porous Au film on Ni foam synthesized via micelle-assisted electrodeposition, exhibiting superior NRR performance.PB, or lead, is a transition metal that plays a role in the composition of PdPb nanosponges, which are utilized in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions. These nanosponges are significant for their potential to enhance the efficiency and yield of ammonia (NH3) production through electrochemical processes.The nanocomposite PC/Sb/SbPO4 exhibits high activity and excellent selectivity for the electrocatalytic conversion of N2 to NH3 in both acidic and neutral electrolytes under ambient conditions.PC6-Co-3 is a single cluster catalyst that exhibits excellent catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.PC6-Fe-4 is a single cluster catalyst that exhibits excellent catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.PCN is a metal-free polymeric carbon nitride catalyst that demonstrates effective nitrogen reduction reaction performance through defect engineering.Palladium (Pd) is a chemical element that plays a significant role in enhancing the performance of various catalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) and electroreduction processes. It is commonly doped into titanium dioxide (TiO2) to improve the catalyst's efficacy in nitrate electroreduction. Pd is integral to the development of Pd-based catalysts, which are specifically designed to optimize NRR performance. These catalysts include trimetallic Pd-based nanosheets and Pd nanoparticles, both of which are utilized to catalyze the electroreduction of nitrogen to ammonia.

In the synthesis of electrocatalysts, Pd is employed in various forms, such as PdNCs@CNFs and palladium hydride nanorods, which are tailored for electrochemical ammonia synthesis. Additionally, Pd is involved in the creation of nano-alloys, such as PdFe3, which further enhance NRR performance. The formation of heterojunction electrocatalysts, like Pd-NiTiO3, also benefits from the incorporation of Pd, which, along with its oxide domains, facilitates nitrogen activation and hydrogenation, thereby improving NRR efficiency.

Pd is recognized as a noble metal catalyst and has been evaluated for its effectiveness in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (ENRR) aimed at ammonia production. It is one of the transition metals that have been screened for anchoring on monolayer graphdiyne, a promising material for electrocatalytic N2 reduction. Overall, Pd's versatility and catalytic properties make it a crucial component in the ongoing research and development of efficient electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction.Pd based electrocatalysts are discussed for their potential in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) and their efficiency in NH3 generation.Pd nanoparticles are used as an electrocatalyst for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) and can be modified for improved performance.Pd-Ag is a bimetallic nanocatalyst used for electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) for ammonia synthesis.Pd-doped TiO2 nanoparticles serve as a high-efficiency electrocatalyst for ammonia synthesis under ambient conditions.Pd-GDY is an atomic catalyst with separated Pd atoms on graphdiyne, exhibiting excellent electrocatalytic properties for nitrogen reduction with high NH3 yield and selectivity.Pd-NiTiO3 is a heterojunction electrocatalyst that exhibits enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) activity and lower overpotential requirements.Pd/C is a noble metal catalyst evaluated for electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (ENRR) to produce ammonia.Pd/gamma-MnO2 is an electrocatalyst known for its exceptional catalytic activity in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). It demonstrates a high yield rate of ammonia (NH3) and exhibits impressive Faradaic efficiency, making it a promising candidate for applications in electrochemical nitrogen fixation.Pd/HKUST-1 is a catalyst that enhances ammonia production rate and Faradaic efficiency when introduced into the HKUST-1 framework.Pd3Cu1 is an alloy electrocatalyst specifically designed for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It exhibits remarkable stability, maintaining 100% of its original activity after 18 hours of operation. This characteristic highlights its potential effectiveness in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia, making it a promising candidate for optimizing electrochemical NRR processes.Pd@Ti3C2 is a composite material formed by loading Pd ions onto Ti3C2 MXene, which improves nitrogen reduction performance.PDA (polydopamine) is used in the synthesis of Cu@NC nanocubes, providing a protective shell during the calcination process.PdAu is a bimetallic alloy catalyst that can reduce N2 to ammonia with a relatively low limiting potential and activation barrier.PdBi2 is an electrocatalyst that, when exfoliated into nanoflakes, enhances the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction by creating vacancies in its crystal lattice for hydrogen absorption.Bimodal nanoporous PdCu alloys are designed for electro-catalytic nitrogen reduction reactions with high NH3 yield rates.PdCu3 bimetal catalyst exhibits a high Faradaic efficiency and NH3 yield in the nitrogen reduction reaction.PdH0.43 NRs are synthesized palladium hydride nanorods that exhibit excellent NH3 yield and Faradaic efficiency for electrochemical ammonia synthesis.PdNCs@CNFs is a composite material consisting of palladium nanocrystals (PdNCs) anchored on carbon nanofibers (CNFs). This innovative electrocatalyst is synthesized through a combination of electrospinning and carbonization processes, which effectively integrate the palladium nanocrystals onto the carbon fiber substrate. Notably, PdNCs@CNFs demonstrates a high Faradaic efficiency of approximately 14.8% for nitrogen reduction reactions, indicating its potential effectiveness in catalyzing the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia. This performance highlights the promising role of PdNCs@CNFs in electrochemical applications, particularly in the context of sustainable ammonia production.PdP2 nanoparticles-reduced graphene oxide (PdP2-rGO) is proposed as an efficient electrocatalyst for the N-2 reduction reaction.PdPb is a nanosponges electrocatalyst that effectively reduces N2 to NH3 under ambient conditions.PdxCuy bimetal catalysts are prepared with adjustable Pd/Cu atomic ratios for use in the nitrogen reduction reaction.PEM refers to proton exchange membrane, a type of electrolyzer used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction.pH is a measure of acidity or alkalinity that affects the performance of electrocatalysts in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).pH 2 is a specific pH level at which the lowest nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) onset occurs.The pH level of 10.5 signifies the acidity of the 0.1 M Na2SO4 solution utilized in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) process, as well as in the electrocatalytic system. This specific pH value is crucial for optimizing the electrochemical conditions, influencing the performance and efficiency of the NRR, particularly in terms of ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency.pH = 3.2 is the pH level of the solution during the nitrogen reduction reaction with the TiN-PE electrocatalyst.pH = over 14 indicates the alkaline conditions under which the nitrogen reduction reaction is conducted.pH > 14 indicates the highly alkaline conditions under which the nitrogen reduction reaction is conducted.The pH level of the electrolyte can significantly influence the efficiency of electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR).Neutral pH is the condition under which the VO2 hollow microspheres catalyst operates efficiently for the nitrogen reduction reaction.pH=7 indicates the acidity level of the electrolyte used in the nitrogen reduction reaction with the Ag/TiO2 catalyst.)<|COMPLETE|>The phase-separated morphology of the CuAg alloy contributes to its effective electrocatalytic performance in nitrogen reduction.Phosphate buffer solution is the electrolyte used for the electrochemical NH3 synthesis with the Fe/Fe3O4 catalyst.Phosphorene monolayer serves as a support for various electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction reactions, enhancing their performance.Phosphorous plays an unusual role in modulating the electrochemical N-2 reduction activity of the Fe catalyst.Phosphotungstic acid serves as a crucial platform for anchoring bismuth and lithium species, which are integral to the development of an efficient electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions. This innovative approach significantly enhances ammonia yield rates and Faradaic efficiencies, making phosphotungstic acid a valuable component in optimizing electrochemical processes aimed at nitrogen fixation.Plasma etching is a synthesis method employed to improve the performance of catalysts in nitrogen reduction reactions. This technique involves creating voids in the catalyst structure, which facilitates enhanced nitrogen transfer during the reaction. Specifically, plasma etching is utilized to increase the number of nitrogen defects on CN/C 6000, thereby optimizing its catalytic efficiency and overall effectiveness in the nitrogen reduction process.A polymeric electrolyte reactor is a method used for the electrochemical conversion of N2 to NH3, enhancing the production of hydrogen carriers.Porous carbon materials are synthesized from ZIF-8 and used as electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions.Porousness in electrocatalysts can create additional active sites and enhance nitrogen adsorption, improving catalytic performance.Potassium is a chemical element that stabilizes key nitrogen-reduction intermediates in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).Pristine ReS2 refers to the unmodified form of ReS2 used for comparison with doped catalysts.Proton-coupled electron transfer (PCET) is a strategy employed to enhance efficiency in the multi-electron/proton reduction of N2 to produce NH3.Proton-filtering covalent organic frameworks are used to control the diffusion of reactants in the nitrogen reduction process.Platinum (Pt) is a chemical element and noble metal that plays a significant role in the field of electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is utilized in various forms and configurations to enhance the efficiency and performance of electrocatalysts aimed at ammonia production. Pt is employed in isolated sites anchored on WO3 nanoplates, contributing to the electrochemical nitrogen reduction process. Additionally, it is a key component in the Pt/TiO2-x NA electrocatalyst, which is specifically designed for NRR applications.

Furthermore, Pt is used in the doping of NiO nanosheets, which improves their electrocatalytic performance for nitrogen reduction. It also participates in the formation of Pt-NiTiO3 heterojunction electrocatalysts, showcasing its versatility in enhancing catalytic activity. In the context of electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions, Pt has been evaluated for its effectiveness in producing ammonia, making it a critical element in this area of research.

Moreover, Pt is one of the transition metals included in assessments of various electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions. It has been identified as a candidate for improving NRR performance through self-assembled monolayer methods and is screened for anchoring on monolayer graphdiyne to facilitate electrocatalytic N2 reduction. Additionally, Pt can be anchored in MoS2 to further enhance its electrocatalytic activity for nitrogen reduction, highlighting its importance in advancing the efficiency of ammonia synthesis.Pt-1/g-C3N4 is a single-atom catalyst used for electrochemical nitrogen reduction.Pt-NiTiO3 is a heterojunction electrocatalyst that shows lower NRR activity compared to Pd-NiTiO3.Pt-Pd is a binary composition of the cathode assembly studied for its reactivity in the nitrate-reduction reaction (NRR).Pt-RE (RE = La, Y, Sc) alloy concave nanocubes are efficient catalysts for the nitrogen reduction reaction due to their high-index facets and electronic effects.Pt/Au is an electrocatalyst that, when modified with ZIF, achieves a Faradaic efficiency of >44 % and a high ammonia yield rate of >161 Œºg mg(cat)(-1) h(-1) under ambient conditions.Pt/C is a catalyst used in the anode for the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, facilitating ammonia production.Pt/NiO-NSs is a hybrid electrocatalyst formed by doping NiO nanosheets with Pt nanoparticles, leading to significant improvements in Faradaic efficiency and NH3 yield.Pt3Fe is an electrocatalyst that exhibits shape-dependent nitrogen reduction reaction activity with tunable morphologies.Pt3Fe nanocrystals are model electrocatalysts with tunable morphologies used for investigating nitrogen reduction reactions on different exposed facets.PTCA-rGO is a metal-free electrocatalyst proposed to enhance N2 reduction under ambient reaction conditions.Pyridinic N is a nitrogen species that enhances ammonia production and Faradaic efficiency in the MoN/C catalyst.Pyrrolic-N-3-G is a type of nitrogen-doped graphene that supports single metal atoms for enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) performance.Quantum dots refer to extremely small semiconductor particles that can enhance the nitrogen reduction reaction properties when used as catalysts.The quasi-gas-solid interface formed in donor-acceptor-based conjugated polymers (CPs) is a morphology that enhances the nitrogen reduction reaction process.R-NH3 is the yield of ammonia produced by the Fe-N/C-CPs electrocatalyst, measured at 2.27 ¬µg h(-1) mg(-1).Re-1 is a single metal atom supported on pyrrolic-N-3-G evaluated for its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Reaction interfaces are critical in determining the efficiency of the nitrogen reduction reaction, influencing the interaction between the catalyst and reactants.Reaction intermediates are transient species formed during the nitrogen reduction reaction, playing a key role in the overall process.)<|COMPLETE|>Reaction kinetics involves the study of the rates of the nitrogen reduction reaction and the factors affecting these rates.Reaction thermodynamics refers to the energy changes associated with the nitrogen reduction reaction, influencing its feasibility and efficiency.Reduced graphene oxide (RGO) is a versatile material that plays a significant role in enhancing the performance of various electrocatalysts. It serves as a support for FeTe2 nanoparticles, which improves their catalytic activity, thereby facilitating more efficient electrochemical reactions. Additionally, RGO is a key component of the Mn3O4@rGO composite, where it contributes to the enhancement of the electrocatalytic properties of the composite material. Overall, RGO's unique structural characteristics and conductive properties make it an essential element in optimizing the performance of electrocatalysts in various applications.ReS2 is a transition-metal dichalcogenide that serves as a model platform for investigating nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) and hydrogen evolution reactions (HER) catalysis. Specifically, the ReS2 nanosheet array on carbon cloth (ReS2/CC) has been employed in NRR applications, demonstrating not only high catalytic activity but also impressive durability. This combination of structural characteristics and material composition positions ReS2 as a promising candidate for optimizing electrochemical processes related to nitrogen reduction.The reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE) is a reference electrode used to measure the applied potentials in electrochemical reactions, including NRR.)<|COMPLETE|>Reduced graphene oxide (rGO) serves as a versatile support material that significantly enhances the electrical conductivity and catalytic activity of silver nanoparticles (Ag NPs) in the Ag NPs-rGO hybrid system. Additionally, rGO is utilized in combination with molybdenum disulfide (MoS2) to create a hybrid catalyst specifically designed for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), further improving its performance. This dual role of rGO in facilitating both the Ag NPs and MoS2 highlights its importance in optimizing electrochemical processes, particularly in the context of enhancing ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency in nitrogen reduction applications.Rhodium (Rh) is a chemical element recognized for its significant role in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR), particularly during the hydrogenation step that converts nitrogen (N2) to NNH. As a transition metal, Rh is known for forming ultrafine nanoparticles that demonstrate excellent performance in NRR applications. It has been extensively studied as a potential electrocatalyst for the synthesis of ammonia from nitrogen, showcasing its effectiveness in this critical reaction. Additionally, Rh is one of the transition metal atoms that have been screened for anchoring on a monolayer of graphdiyne, a structure that enhances its catalytic properties for electrocatalytic N2 reduction. Overall, Rh's unique characteristics and its integration into advanced materials make it a valuable candidate for optimizing nitrogen reduction processes.Rh atomic layer-decorated SnO2 heterostructure is a novel catalyst designed for enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction performance.Rh-Se NCs are electrocatalysts that exhibit significantly enhanced NH3 yield and Faradaic efficiency due to surface chalcogenation.RHE stands for reversible hydrogen electrode, a reference electrode used in electrochemical measurements, particularly for applied potentials in nitrogen reduction reactions.Room temperature is a critical condition for the operation of the Fe2O3/TiO2 composite catalyst in ammonia synthesis. It serves as the environment in which the nitrogen reduction reaction is conducted, significantly influencing the reaction kinetics. The performance of the catalyst and the efficiency of ammonia production are closely tied to maintaining this temperature, highlighting its importance in optimizing electrochemical processes for nitrogen reduction.Ruthenium (Ru) is a chemical element and noble metal that plays a significant role in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (ENRR), particularly in the synthesis of ammonia (NH3) from nitrogen (N2). It influences the electronic structure of iron (Fe) sites in bimetallic Fe-Ru single atom catalysts, enhancing their catalytic performance. Ru is utilized as a single-atom electrocatalyst, notably on Ru(0001) surfaces, and is also incorporated into nitrogen-doped carbon structures to facilitate nitrogen reduction reactions. Additionally, Ru is employed in high-performance catalysts such as Ru@Ti3C2 MXene, which are designed for ambient electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction. As a transition metal, Ru has been extensively studied for its electrocatalytic activity and is part of various innovative structures, including transition-metal-embedded graphdiyne monolayers, where it is screened for anchoring to optimize N2 reduction. Overall, Ru is a key element in advancing the efficiency and effectiveness of nitrogen fixation and electroreduction processes.Ru nanowires are a type of electrocatalyst that serve as a comparison to Ru88Pt12 in terms of NH3 production rate and faradaic efficiency.Ru SAs/N-C is an advanced electrocatalyst that demonstrates exceptional performance in nitrogen reduction reactions, achieving a record-high activity at -0.2 V vs. RHE. This electrocatalyst exhibits a remarkable yield rate of 120.9 Œºg(NH3) mg(cat)(-1).h(-1), significantly surpassing previously reported values for ammonia (NH3) production. The combination of its high activity and yield rate positions Ru SAs/N-C as a leading candidate for efficient electrochemical nitrogen reduction, highlighting its potential for optimizing NH3 synthesis in various applications.Ru(0001) is a benchmark surface used for comparison in the activity trends of the FeM-GDY catalysts.Ru-1/g-C3N4 is another single-atom catalyst used for electrochemical nitrogen reduction.Ru-CNT GDE is a microtubular ruthenium-carbon nanotube gas diffusion electrode applied for electrochemical NH3 synthesis.The Ru-Cu mixture catalyst in a nanosponge architecture is designed for enhanced performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.Ru/C is a noble metal catalyst that has been evaluated for its effectiveness in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (ENRR) aimed at producing ammonia. As a precious-metal catalyst, Ru/C serves as a benchmark for comparison under similar reaction conditions in nitrogen reduction processes. Its performance in these reactions is critical for optimizing ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency, making it a significant entity in the field of electrochemistry and catalysis.Ru88Pt12 is an electrocatalyst composed of Ru and Pt that exhibits a high NH3 production rate and faradaic efficiency in the nitrogen reduction reaction.Ru@Ti3C2 MXene is a high-performance catalyst reported for ambient electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction.RuCu-FNs are fusiform-like ruthenium-copper alloy nanosheets that have been specifically engineered for use in electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). These nanosheets are notable for their unique fusiform shape and are synthesized through alloy engineering techniques. They are designed to operate effectively under ambient conditions, making them a promising candidate for enhancing the efficiency and yield of ammonia production in electrochemical processes.RuFeCoNiCu is a high-entropy alloy nanoparticle electrocatalyst reported for nitrogen reduction reaction with small size and uniformity.RuO2 is a chemical compound that plays a significant role in the preparation of hybrid composite electrocatalysts specifically designed for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is highlighted in the context of a study that proposes various strategies to enhance NRR activity, indicating that RuO2 can benefit from these innovative approaches. This suggests that RuO2 not only serves as a foundational material in the development of effective electrocatalysts but also has the potential for optimization to improve ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency in electrochemical processes.Ruthenium nanoparticles are a highly efficient and durable electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reaction in acidic electrolyte.S is a chemical element that plays a significant role in various electrocatalytic systems, particularly in the context of nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is a component of the FeNi2S4/NiS hetero-interface electrocatalyst, which is designed to improve the efficiency of NRR. Additionally, S is integral to the composition of nitrogenase-like biomimetic electrocatalysts, which are engineered to mimic the natural nitrogen-fixing process. Furthermore, S contributes to the formation of defective two-dimensional materials, which have been shown to enhance catalytic activity for NRR. Overall, S is a crucial element in advancing the development of effective electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction, influencing both structural characteristics and catalytic performance.S-doped carbon nanosphere (S-CNS) is an efficient and stable catalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions.S-G is a sulfur-doped graphene electrocatalyst proposed for efficient nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) under ambient conditions.Single atom catalysts (SACs) are expected to achieve relatively high ammonia selectivity at low applied potential for nitrogen electroreduction.Sb is one of the main group metal elements discussed for their role in electrochemical ammonia production.Sb/Nb2CTx is an efficient electrocatalyst for NRR that exhibits high activity and selectivity.Scandium (Sc) is a 3d transition metal that plays a significant role in enhancing the electrocatalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It can be loaded onto a C2N monolayer, which facilitates the nitrogen reduction process, and is also considered an early transition metal for ammonia synthesis. Additionally, Sc can be anchored in MoS2, further improving its performance in NRR applications. This versatility in supporting various catalytic frameworks highlights Sc's potential in optimizing electrochemical nitrogen reduction to ammonia.SC-PBDT-TT is a semicrystalline conjugated polymer catalyst that exhibits high Faradaic efficiency and NH3 production rate in a neutral-buffered seawater electrolyte.Se is a chemical element involved in the creation of defective 2D-materials that improve the catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions.Se vacancies are structural features in the MoSe2/Ti3C2 composite that enhance its NRR activity by increasing conductivity and reducing reaction barriers for intermediate formation.Seed-assisted hydrothermal and ammonolysis methods are the synthesis methods used to prepare MoN nanorods.Single Fe atom loaded on anatase TiO2(001) is identified as a potential catalyst for photo-electrochemical ammonia synthesis.Slow N-2 diffusion is a reaction condition that hinders the efficiency of the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction, leading to competitive hydrogen evolution.Sn is one of the main group metal elements discussed for their role in electrochemical ammonia production.The porous tin heterostructure is an electrocatalyst designed for efficient ammonia synthesis from nitrogen.Sodium gluconate is a species used in the electro-oxidation reaction alongside the nitrogen reduction reaction in the two-electrode system with Cu-II-MOF.Sponge-like nitrogen-doped mesoporous interconnected hollow carbon nanofibers serve as a substrate for anchoring cobalt atoms to enhance nitrogen reduction reactions.A spontaneous process in the context of nitrogen reduction refers to a reaction that occurs without external energy input, such as the initial hydrogenation of N2 on certain metal surfaces.)<|COMPLETE|>Strong interaction refers to the significant binding strength between Mo-PTA and N2 during nitrogen reduction reactions.Strong metal-support interaction (SMSI) is a phenomenon that enhances the performance of catalysts by improving electron transfer and charge distribution.Structural integrity refers to the ability of the Pd3Cu1 alloy to maintain its original activity after prolonged operation in nitrogen reduction reactions.Sulfur-doped graphene is proposed as an efficient and stable electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction.Sulfur-doped graphene (S-G) is proposed as an efficient and stable electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction.The superhydrophobic metal-organic framework (MOF) layer is used to coat the NRR electrocatalyst to enhance selectivity and Faradaic efficiency during the nitrogen reduction reaction.The synergistic catalytic effect refers to the enhanced performance of the Pd/gamma-MnO2 electrocatalyst due to the interaction between Pd and Mn.The "synergistic effect" is a phenomenon observed in various electrocatalysts that significantly enhances their performance in the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). In the context of the Fe-B/NC catalyst, the synergistic effect between boron (B) and iron (Fe) plays a crucial role by improving the adsorption and activation of nitrogen (N2), thereby leading to enhanced NRR performance. Additionally, this effect is also noted in the combination of iron oxide (Fe3O4) and molybdenum disulfide (MoS2), which further contributes to the improved catalytic activity in the NRR. Overall, the synergistic interactions among these components are pivotal for optimizing the efficiency and yield of ammonia (NH3) production in electrochemical processes.TaB is an electrocatalyst that exhibits impressive selectivity over hydrogen evolution reactions (HER) with a specific overpotential.The tabular crystal-like (Mn,Fe)(2)O3-y morphology describes another structural form of the trimetal oxide electrocatalyst.Tafel analysis is a method used to estimate the exchange current density and other kinetic parameters of the electrocatalyst during the NRR.)<|COMPLETE|>Tafel plots are used to analyze the mechanism and dynamics of nitrogen reduction in the context of the applied electroreduction potential.Tandem catalysis is a strategy that combines a molecular complex with a co-catalyst to facilitate N-H bond formation in the N2RR.Tannic acid (TA) is used to modify the surface of Au nanowires, improving their electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction performance.TaSe2 is an electrocatalyst that shows significant selectivity over HER with a specific overpotential.TE, or Tellurium, is a chemical element known for its role in the formation of defective two-dimensional (2D) materials. These defective 2D materials can significantly enhance catalytic activity, particularly in electrochemical reactions such as nitrogen reduction. The presence of Tellurium facilitates the exposure of nitrogen molecules to transition metals, which is crucial for optimizing the efficiency of catalytic processes aimed at ammonia (NH3) production.Tetrahydrofuran / ethanol (99:1 v/v) is the solvent used in the electrochemical nitrogen reduction system.Tetraphenylporphyrin iron chloride (FeTPPCl) is a molecular iron catalyst that exhibits promising NRR activity.Theoretical calculations are used to predict the performance and mechanisms of the electrocatalysts in nitrogen reduction reactions.Thermal annealing is a process used to fabricate Bi nanoparticles in situ confined in carbon rods for ammonia synthesis.Thiourea (TU) is a synthesis method used for the preparation of NiS catalysts, influencing their catalytic performance.The three-phase electrocatalyst Au/o-CFP is designed to enhance nitrogen reduction reaction performance by facilitating contact between gas, liquid, and solid phases.Titanium (Ti) is a versatile 3d transition metal that plays a significant role in various aspects of electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It can be loaded onto C2N monolayers to facilitate nitrogen reduction, and it is a key component of TiO2, which is utilized in NRR electrocatalysts. Additionally, Ti is involved in the formulation of boron-doped TiO2 catalysts, enhancing their effectiveness in electrochemical NRR processes.

In the context of electrocatalytic systems, Ti serves multiple functions: it acts as a current collector in both fluidized and non-loading electrocatalysis systems, providing essential electrical conductivity. Furthermore, Ti is employed as a doping agent in iron phosphide to improve NRR activity and is integral to the development of NiTiO3-based nanoelectrocatalysts.

Ti has been evaluated as a candidate electrode material for ammonia synthesis, and its potential has been explored in various configurations, including anchoring on monolayer graphdiyne and within MoS2 structures to boost electrocatalytic performance. Overall, Ti's multifaceted roles in NRR highlight its importance in advancing the efficiency and effectiveness of nitrogen reduction technologies.Ti2O3 nanoparticles serve as a pure Ti3+ system that performs efficiently in NH3 electrosynthesis under ambient conditions.Ti3+ refers to titanium in a +3 oxidation state, which acts as a catalytically active site in the NRR process.Ti3+-TiO2-x is a self-doped nanowire electrocatalyst used for the nitrogen reduction reaction with excellent selectivity.Ti3AlC2 is a MAX phase material used in the development of heterostructure catalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions.Ti3AlC2 MAX / Ti3C2 MXene is a heterostructure catalyst proposed for N2-to-NH3 conversion at ambient conditions.Ti3C2 is a promising MXene material that plays a significant role in enhancing the performance of various heterostructure catalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is a key component in several composite catalysts, including the MoSe2/Ti3C2 and Ru@Ti3C2 systems, both of which demonstrate high efficiency in NRR applications. Additionally, Ti3C2 can be combined with copper to form a composite catalyst that further improves nitrogen reduction efficiency. This ultra-thin two-dimensional conductive material is also utilized in the preparation of Au@Ti3C2 samples, showcasing its versatility in electrocatalytic applications. Overall, Ti3C2 stands out as an effective catalyst for electrochemical nitrogen reduction under ambient conditions, making it a valuable material in the pursuit of sustainable ammonia production.Ti3C2 MXene is a two-dimensional conductive material used as a substrate for loading Pd ions to enhance nitrogen reduction performance.Ti3C2-B is a boron-doped titanium carbide electrocatalyst that exhibits high NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency.Ti3C2Tix MXene is a catalyst modified to increase hydrophobicity and improve NRR performance.Ti3C2Tx, a type of MXene nanosheet characterized by its surface terminations (T = F, OH), is recognized as a high-performance 2D electrocatalyst for the ambient conversion of nitrogen (N2) to ammonia (NH3). This MXene not only serves as an effective precursor and conductive substrate for the growth of TiO2 nanoparticles but also enhances the electrocatalytic properties of the resulting composite. Ti3C2Tx exhibits improved adsorption and activation of N2, which is crucial for facilitating nitrogen reduction reactions. Its structural modifications have been specifically designed to enhance catalytic reactivity, leading to significant improvements in the efficiency of the electrochemical nitrogen reduction process. Notably, Ti3C2Tx has demonstrated a high NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency under ambient conditions, with its oxygen vacancies acting as active sites that contribute to its effectiveness as an electrocatalyst.Ti3C2Tx quantum dots (Ti3C2Tx QDs) are advanced electrocatalysts derived from Ti3C2Tx MXene, designed specifically for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). These quantum dots are characterized by their abundant active sites, which enhance their efficiency in catalyzing the NRR. The synthesis of Ti3C2Tx QDs involves processes such as alkalization and intercalation, which contribute to their improved performance in ammonia production. Overall, Ti3C2Tx QDs represent a promising approach to optimizing NRR catalysis, leveraging their unique structural properties and active site availability.TiC/C nanofibers act as an efficient one-dimensional electrocatalyst for N2-to-NH3 conversion, demonstrating high ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency.TiMo/g-C3N4 is a heteronuclear dual-atom catalyst that can be applied to nitrogen reduction reactions as a photocatalyst.TiN is an electrocatalyst that cooperates with Bi single atoms (SAs) to promote the hydrogenation of N2 into NH3 and the desorption of NH3.TiN-BM is an electrocatalyst that is treated by ball-milling method, contributing to the overall performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.TiN-PE is an electrocatalyst that is processed by plasma-etching and is highly electrochemically active for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) to produce NH3.TiO2, or titanium dioxide, is a versatile catalyst material that plays a crucial role in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR) aimed at ammonia production. Its performance can be significantly enhanced through various modifications and composite formations. For instance, TiO2 modified with copper nanoparticles exhibits improved NRR performance, while nitrogen-impregnated carbon coatings further enhance its catalytic capabilities. Additionally, doping TiO2 with palladium (Pd) has been shown to facilitate nitrate electroreduction to produce NH3.

The chemical compound TiO2 is instrumental in activating the first protonation step of the nitrogen reduction process and increasing N2 chemisorption, which is essential for effective nitrogen fixation. When compared to Fe2O3 alone, TiO2 demonstrates superior activity in nitrogen reduction reactions, and it is also utilized in the preparation of the alpha-Fe2O3@mTiO2 nanocomposite, which is beneficial for NRR.

TiO2 can be synthesized in various forms, including lotus-root-like amorphous nanofibers that enhance N2 activation and electron transport capacity. It serves as a support for single yttrium sites in electrocatalytic nitrogen fixation and is used to fabricate defect-rich nanosheets that act as efficient electrocatalysts. Furthermore, doping TiO2 with iron or incorporating Ti3+ defect sites has been shown to improve its performance in NRR.

The material can also be composited with silver (Ag) to enhance nitrogen reduction reaction performance. In different phases, such as anatase and rutile, TiO2 is employed in the synthesis of carbon nanocomposites for NRR. The incorporation of TiO2 nanoparticles (NPs) enhances the specific surface area (SSA) of Ti3C2Tx, contributing to the overall catalytic performance. These nanoparticles can be grown in situ on Ti3C2Tx, resulting in a synergistically active Ti-based nanohybrid catalyst for nitrogen reduction electrocatalysis. Additionally, doping TiO2 with vanadium (V) has been noted to improve its efficacy in ammonia production through NRR.TiO2(101) is a support material for Ni clusters that enhances their catalytic activity for nitrogen reduction.TiO2-delta N delta is a three-dimensional integrated electrode fabricated to enhance electrocatalytic activity for NRR.TiO2-rGO is a hybrid electrocatalyst that behaves efficiently for nitrogen reduction reactions with excellent selectivity.ÑXd␀␀␀␀␀ÀXd␀␀␀␀␀Yd␀␀␀␀␀ÖYd␀␀␀␀␀˙Yd␀␀␀␀␀dZd␀␀␀␀␀¢Zd␀␀␀␀␀[d␀␀␀␀␀q[d␀␀␀␀␀Ω[d␀␀␀␀␀B\d␀␀␀␀␀å^d␀␀␀␀␀·^d␀␀␀␀␀J_d␀␀␀␀␀√_d␀␀␀␀␀S`d␀␀␀␀␀Æ`d␀␀␀␀␀ad␀␀␀␀␀ubd␀␀␀␀␀ùdd␀␀␀␀␀ˆdd␀␀␀␀␀5ed␀␀␀␀␀áed␀␀␀␀␀–ed␀␀␀␀␀5fd␀␀␀␀␀≥fd␀␀␀␀␀,gd␀␀␀␀␀ügd␀␀␀␀␀hd␀␀␀␀␀Jhd␀␀␀␀␀•hd␀␀␀␀␀ƒid␀␀␀␀␀jd␀␀␀␀␀Zjd␀␀␀␀␀⁄jd␀␀␀␀␀[kd␀␀␀␀␀∆kd␀␀␀␀␀3ld␀␀␀␀␀ôld␀␀␀␀␀ld␀␀␀␀␀ùmd␀␀␀␀␀¨od␀␀␀␀␀”pd␀␀␀␀␀?qd␀␀␀␀␀Åqd␀␀␀␀␀ﬂqd␀␀␀␀␀0rd␀␀␀␀␀ìrd␀␀␀␀␀⁄rd␀␀␀␀␀®td␀␀␀␀␀ud␀␀␀␀␀ùud␀␀␀␀␀vd␀␀␀␀␀xvd␀␀␀␀␀␀wd␀␀␀␀␀%xd␀␀␀␀␀óxd␀␀␀␀␀yd␀␀␀␀␀qyd␀␀␀␀␀zd␀␀␀␀␀vzd␀␀␀␀␀∑zd␀␀␀␀␀{d␀␀␀␀␀Å{d␀␀␀␀␀È{d␀␀␀␀␀x|d␀␀␀␀␀„|d␀␀␀␀␀4}d␀␀␀␀␀ä}d␀␀␀␀␀~d␀␀␀␀␀s~d␀␀␀␀␀∏~d␀␀␀␀␀Dd␀␀␀␀␀zÅd␀␀␀␀␀÷Åd␀␀␀␀␀1Çd␀␀␀␀␀~Çd␀␀␀␀␀‘Çd␀␀␀␀␀SÉd␀␀␀␀␀—Éd␀␀␀␀␀@Ñd␀␀␀␀␀dÜd␀␀␀␀␀≤Üd␀␀␀␀␀-ád␀␀␀␀␀©ád␀␀␀␀␀
