Ú«␀␀␀␀␀ﬂÚ«␀␀␀␀␀ˇÚ«␀␀␀␀␀Û«␀␀␀␀␀?Û«␀␀␀␀␀TiO2-x is an electrocatalyst combined with Pt to form Pt/TiO2-x NA, which shows excellent mechanical properties for nitrogen reduction reactions.TiO2/Ag/Cu7S4@SeCC is a heterogeneous electrocatalyst that generates a unique sandwich structure to enhance electron transfer efficiency for nitrogen reduction.TiO2/Ti3C2Tx is an electrocatalyst prepared via a one-step ethanolthermal treatment of Ti3C2Tx MXene, featuring oxygen-vacancy-rich TiO2 nanoparticles.TiO2@N-C is an enhanced catalyst for electrochemical nitrogen reduction, showing improved ammonia yield and Faradaic efficiency.TiOxNy is a species formed on the surface of TiN that is electrocatalytically active for nitrogen reduction reactions.Titania is a photo(electro)catalyst known for its ability to reduce nitrogen to ammonia under varying electrochemical potentials and illumination conditions.TM-O2N2/G is a type of single atom catalyst (SAC) that is investigated for the electroreduction of nitrogen.TM2@Ir(100) is an electrocatalyst based on transition metal doped Ir(100) surface designed for nitrogen reduction reactions.TM2N4@graphene refers to the dual-atom catalysts embedded in graphene for nitrogen reduction reaction screening.TM3 @Gra is an electrocatalyst supported on graphene for nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) with high catalytic activity.TM@C4N4 refers to single transition metal-atom-doped C4N4 monolayers that serve as electrocatalysts for N2RR.TM@C9N4 refers to transition metal single atom centers anchored on the C9N4 substrate, which are candidates for catalyzing N2 reduction.TM@MVG and TM@DVG are morphologies of monovacancy and divacancy defective graphenes decorated with single 3d transition metal atoms used as electrocatalysts for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR).TM@Nvs-g-C3N4/graphene heterostructures consist of g-C3N4 with nitrogen vacancies and graphene, which are investigated for their catalytic activities for N2 reduction.TON (Turnover Number) is a measure of the catalytic activity, with a reported value of 2.83% in the titanocene based electrocatalytic system.Transition metal nitrides are materials explored for their potential in nitrogen reduction reactions, particularly for ammonia synthesis.Transition metal nitrides (TMNs) are explored as catalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions due to their favorable Mars-van Krevelen mechanism.Transition metal-based electrocatalysts are materials that facilitate the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) by providing active sites for the reaction.Transition metals (TM) such as Sc, V, Mn, Y, Tc, and Os are investigated for their catalytic activity in the nitrogen reduction reaction when supported on monolayer graphyne.Transition metals (TMs) such as Fe, Ru, Rh, and Pd are studied for their activity in nitrogen reduction to ammonia synthesis.The trimetallic synergistic interactions in NiFe@MoS2 NCs enhance the exposure of active sites for electrocatalytic performance.Trimethoxy (3,3,4,4,5,5,6,6,7,7,8,8,8-trideca fluorooctyl) silane (FOTS) is another surfactant used to modify the surface of Ti3C2Tix MXene for improved hydrophobicity.Turn-over-frequencies (TOF) indicate the rate of ammonia production per active site in nitrogen reduction reactions.The specific two-dimensional nanosheet morphology of the Fe1Mo1-N catalyst is believed to contribute significantly to its electrochemical performance.U-L Co3O4 is an electrocatalyst designed with an urchin-like morphology that exhibits enhanced performance in the electrocatalytic nitrogen reduction reaction (ENRR).Ultralow potential is the low voltage at which MXene (Ti3C2Tx) achieves a high faradic efficiency for NH3 electrosynthesis.Ultrathin carbon nanosheets are used as a support structure for molybdenum carbide nanodots in the Mo2C/C electrocatalyst.UV light is a reaction condition that facilitates the generation of center dot H from sulfite and H2O in the electrolyte solutions during the NRR process.V is a chemical element that plays a significant role in enhancing catalytic activity and improving the performance of various materials in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is incorporated into ferric oxide to create V-alpha-Fe2O3, which enhances its catalytic properties. Additionally, V serves as an effective dopant for TiO2, significantly improving its NRR performance. When anchored in g-C3N4, V contributes to the activation of N2 molecules, facilitating the reduction process. Furthermore, V is utilized as a transition metal in the construction of single cluster catalysts (SCCs) specifically designed for NRR. It is also one of the transition metal atoms screened for anchoring on a monolayer graphdiyne, further demonstrating its versatility and importance in electrocatalytic N2 reduction applications.V-1 is a single metal atom supported on pyrrolic-N-3-G evaluated for its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.V-alpha-Fe2O3 is a vanadium-decorated ferric oxide catalyst with enhanced nitrogen reduction reaction activity.V-N-2 is an electrocatalyst that exhibits excellent electrocatalytic NRR performance with a faradaic efficiency of 76.53% and an NH3 yield rate of 31.41 Œºg(NH3) h(-1) mgCat. -1 at -0.3 V vs RHE.V-NiS2 is an electrocatalyst that exhibits excellent catalytic activity with a high NH3 yield of 47.63 Œºg h^(-1) mg(cat.)^(-1) at an applied potential of -0.45 V and good faradaic efficiency of 9.37% at -0.35 V in a 0.1 M HCl solution.V-O refers to intrinsic oxygen vacancies present in the synthesized TiO2 nanofibers, which play a crucial role in enhancing N2 activation.V/gt-C3N4 is a catalyst that performs better than others for lying-on adsorbed N2 cases with a limiting potential of -0.79 V.V2O3/C is an electrocatalyst whose nitrogen reduction reaction activity is enhanced by phosphorus doping.V2O3/VN is a two-dimensional core/shell nanomesh electrocatalyst designed for the nitrogen reduction reaction.V4C3Tx is the morphology of the MXene used as a support for Ni nanoparticles in the Ni@MX electrocatalyst.V8C7/C is a high-performance electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reaction under ambient conditions.V@G-C3N4 is a transition metal anchored on s-triazine-based g-C3N4, demonstrating significant potential for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). This material's unique structural characteristics and composition position it as a promising electrocatalyst for enhancing ammonia (NH3) yield and faradaic efficiency in electrochemical processes.V@GDY is a monolayer single-atom catalyst that exhibits the best nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) catalytic character with the lowest limiting potential of -0.67 V.Vacancies are a type of defect in electrocatalysts that can enhance nitrogen adsorption and improve catalytic performance.Vanadium tetroxide nanowire is an electrocatalyst supported on reduced graphene oxide, known for its excellent performance in ammonia production.VN nanosheet array on Ti mesh is developed as an active and selective electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) in acid.VN/CC is a high-performance catalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions under ambient conditions.VO2 is a non-precious metal catalyst proposed for the nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR) that operates efficiently and stably at neutral pH.Void-like defects are structural features created in the catalyst that enhance van der Waals interactions with nitrogen molecules.VS2 is a material that is compared with VS4-140 regarding their performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.VS4-140 is a highly effective electrocatalyst for nitrogen reduction reactions, demonstrating a remarkable NH3 yield of 46.74 Œºg h(-1) mgcat(-1) and a faradaic efficiency of 8.5% at a potential of -0.59 V (vs RHE). This material is noted for its superior performance in the electrocatalytic conversion of nitrogen, making it a promising candidate for applications aimed at enhancing ammonia production through electrochemical methods.W is a chemical element that plays a significant role in the field of nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). It is involved in mediating N2RR electrocatalysis across various complexes, demonstrating its importance in facilitating the conversion of nitrogen to ammonia. W receives strong electron transfer from molybdenum (Mo), which enhances its effectiveness in the nitrogen reduction process. Additionally, W is one of the transition metal atoms that have been screened for anchoring on a monolayer graphdiyne, a promising material for electrocatalytic N2 reduction. Overall, W is a key transition metal studied for its potential in ammonia synthesis through nitrogen reduction.W-1 is a single metal atom supported on pyrrolic-N-3-G evaluated for its performance in nitrogen reduction reactions.W18O49 is an electrocatalyst that serves as a baseline for comparison in the context of nitrogen reduction reactions. It exhibits an NH3 yield rate that is approximately half of that achieved by a modified catalyst. Notably, when W18O49 is doped with iron (Fe), it demonstrates enhanced performance, achieving high ammonia yield and Faradaic efficiency at low overpotential. This indicates that the modification of W18O49 with Fe significantly improves its catalytic properties, making it a promising candidate for optimizing electrochemical nitrogen reduction processes.W1C3 is a single W atom embedded in graphene that exhibits the best performance for N2 reduction with an extremely low onset potential.W@C9N4 is a specific catalyst that demonstrates excellent selectivity and activity toward NRR with a low limiting potential of -0.24 V.WO3, or tungsten trioxide, is a versatile material utilized as a support for isolated platinum (Pt) sites, significantly enhancing the electrochemical nitrogen reduction reaction (NRR). This near electroneutral nanobelt catalyst exhibits promising electrocatalytic activity for nitrogen reduction, making it a valuable component in the quest for efficient nitrogen fixation. Additionally, WO3 is characterized by tailored surface oxygen vacancies, which further contribute to its potential as an effective electrocatalyst for N2 fixation. Overall, WO3 stands out for its ability to optimize the performance of nitrogen reduction processes through its unique structural and electronic properties.Yolk-shell nanocomposites contain highly active Au nanostar cores and permeable POP shells for ambient electrocatalytic NRR to NH3.Yttrium is a chemical element used in the synthesis of unique hetero-phase nanocatalysts for electrocatalytic nitrogen fixation.Zeolitic imidazolate framework-8 (ZIF-8) is a precursor used in the synthesis of Fe, N co-doped porous carbon materials for nitrogen reduction reactions.ZIF-67 is a metal-organic framework precursor used to obtain Co@N-doped carbon (NC) via pyrolysis.ZIF-8, or zeolitic imidazolate framework-8, is a versatile material utilized primarily as a precursor for the synthesis of iron and nitrogen co-doped porous carbon materials. This framework plays a significant role in enhancing the performance of electrochemical reactions, particularly in the context of nitrogen reduction. Additionally, ZIF-8 is incorporated into Ti3C2 MXene to modulate the rate of hydrogen evolution reactions during the nitrogen reduction process. Its unique structural characteristics and ability to influence reaction kinetics make ZIF-8 a valuable component in optimizing electrochemical systems aimed at improving ammonia yield and faradaic efficiency.Zigzag graphene nanoribbons are used as electrocatalysts for nitrogen reduction reactions, with single transition metal atoms anchored to them.Zinc (Zn) is a versatile chemical element that plays a significant role in various electrochemical processes, particularly in the context of nitrogen reduction reactions (NORR). It is utilized in the construction of metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) that enhance electrocatalytic performance for nitrogen reduction. Additionally, Zn is integral to the development of Zn-nitrate battery systems aimed at facilitating nitrogen reduction. As a transition metal, Zn is employed in the fabrication of single cluster catalysts (SCCs) specifically designed for NORR, showcasing its effectiveness in this area. Furthermore, Zn is a component of transition-metal-embedded graphdiyne monolayers (TM@GDY), which are studied for their potential in nitrogen reduction applications. Its electrocatalytic performance has been evaluated in various studies, and Zn is also a constituent element of high-entropy alloys (HEAs) that are being investigated for their capabilities in nitrogen reduction reactions. Overall, Zn's multifaceted applications in electrochemistry underscore its importance in advancing technologies related to nitrogen reduction and energy storage.Zn-Fe is an electrocatalyst that shows high NH3 yield and faradaic efficiency in the nitrogen reduction process.The Zn-N-2 battery is a rechargeable energy storage system that incorporates advanced materials to optimize its performance in nitrogen fixation. It utilizes CoPi/HSNPC as a cathode catalyst, which has been shown to achieve significant ammonia yield and high Faradaic efficiency. Additionally, the battery employs an OV-Ti2O3 cathode, further enhancing its nitrogen fixation capabilities. This combination of catalysts and materials positions the Zn-N-2 battery as a promising technology for efficient ammonia production through electrochemical processes.Zn/NC NSs are synthesized as an efficient nitrogen reduction reaction catalyst with atomically dispersed zinc active sites.ZnMn2O4 is an electrocatalyst derived from spent primary ZnC battery, used for nitrogen reduction reactions.Zirconium (Zr) is a chemical element recognized for its role in theoretical calculations that predict nitrogen adsorption and reduction in electrocatalysts. As a transition metal, Zr can be anchored in molybdenum disulfide (MoS2), which significantly enhances the electrocatalytic activity for nitrogen reduction reactions (NRR). This dual functionality underscores Zr's importance in optimizing electrochemical processes aimed at improving ammonia (NH3) yield and faradaic efficiency in nitrogen reduction applications.ZrO2, or zirconium dioxide, is a non-noble metal catalyst that is synthesized through the thermolysis of zirconium-based biphenyl-4,4'-dicarboxylic acid metal-organic frameworks (MOFs). This material plays a significant role in electrochemical nitrogen reduction reactions, particularly as an additive that effectively suppresses the competitive hydrogen evolution reaction. By mitigating this side reaction, ZrO2 enhances the overall efficiency of the nitrogen reduction process, thereby improving the yield of ammonia (NH3) and increasing the faradaic efficiency of the electrochemical system.ZrO2/C is a non-noble metal catalyst that achieves nitrogen fixation to ammonia at ambient conditions.The [Fe-S-2-Mo] system is an electrocatalyst that shows high activity for the conversion of N-2 to NH3.œı«␀␀␀␀␀`ˆ«␀␀␀␀␀␀˜«␀␀␀␀␀␀˜«␀␀␀␀␀ó˜«␀␀␀␀␀¯«␀␀␀␀␀ç¯«␀␀␀␀␀ç¯«␀␀␀␀␀*˘«␀␀␀␀␀ñ˘«␀␀␀␀␀˙«␀␀␀␀␀Ç˙«␀␀␀␀␀˙˙«␀␀␀␀␀g˚«␀␀␀␀␀Ô˚«␀␀␀␀␀Ô˚«␀␀␀␀␀Ô˚«␀␀␀␀␀∂¸«␀␀␀␀␀]˝«␀␀␀␀␀Í˝«␀␀␀␀␀s˛«␀␀␀␀␀ˇ«␀␀␀␀␀ôˇ«␀␀␀␀␀G␀»␀␀␀␀␀ƒ␀»␀␀␀␀␀D»␀␀␀␀␀Ï»␀␀␀␀␀Ï»␀␀␀␀␀`»␀␀␀␀␀ˆ»␀␀␀␀␀ú»␀␀␀␀␀»␀␀␀␀␀ë»␀␀␀␀␀+»␀␀␀␀␀n»␀␀␀␀␀„»␀␀␀␀␀R	»␀␀␀␀␀R	»␀␀␀␀␀R	»␀␀␀␀␀
